ForgeData.register("chem",{"CHEM-1":{"label":"Atomic Structure & Bonding","color":"#7c3aed","questions":[{"id":"CHEM-01","stem":"The relative atomic mass of chlorine is 35.5. A student says: 'This means chlorine atoms have 35.5 protons.' Why is this wrong?","options":{"A":"Weighted mean mass of all naturally occurring isotopes relative to 1/12 of carbon-12.","B":"It increases the average kinetic energy of the particles, raising the activation energy.","C":"Chlorine has a relative atomic mass of 35.5 because it has 35.5 neutrons.","D":"Relative atomic mass equals the number of electrons in the neutral atom."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ATOMIC-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Relative atomic mass (Ar) = weighted mean mass of naturally occurring isotopes relative to 1/12 of carbon-12. Chlorine: 75% 35Cl + 25% 37Cl → Ar = (0.75×35) + (0.25×37) = 35.5. Every individual chlorine ATOM has exactly 17 protons (atomic number = proton number = always whole). The fractional Ar reflects the isotopic mixture in a sample — not any individual atom.","reforge":{"stem":"Bromine has two isotopes: 79Br (50.7%) and 81Br (49.3%). Calculate the relative atomic mass of bromine to 1 decimal place.","options":{"A":"80.5","B":"79.5","C":"80.0","D":"79.0"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.1"},{"id":"CHEM-02","stem":"Which of the following correctly explains why the second ionisation energy of sodium is much higher than the first?","options":{"A":"The second electron is removed from a higher energy level, further from the nucleus.","B":"The second electron is removed from a full inner shell (2p)","C":"Sodium has fewer protons after losing the first electron.","D":"The second ionisation energy is higher because the atom becomes negatively charged."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ATOMIC-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Na: 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s¹. First IE removes the lone 3s¹ electron — outer shell, well-shielded, low energy. Second IE removes a 2p electron from the 2p⁶ sub-shell — this is an INNER shell, less shielded, much closer to the nucleus (higher nuclear attraction). The large jump between 1st and 2nd IE identifies sodium as Group 1 (one outer electron). This pattern of IE jumps is key to identifying group number.","reforge":{"stem":"An element has successive ionisation energies (kJ/mol): 738, 1451, 7733, 10,540. Which group is this element in?","options":{"A":"Group 1","B":"Group 7","C":"Group 3","D":"Group 2"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.1"},{"id":"CHEM-03","stem":"A student draws a dot-and-cross diagram for water (H₂O) with no lone pairs on the oxygen. What is wrong?","options":{"A":"oxygen has no lone pairs in water.","B":"Water has three bonds, not two.","C":"Oxygen has 6 valence electrons: 2 are used in bonds with hydrogen","D":"Metallic bonding — delocalised electrons hold the structure together."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCHEM-BONDING-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Water: O has 6 valence electrons. Shares 1 with each H (2 bonding pairs). Remaining 4 electrons = 2 lone pairs on O. VSEPR: 4 electron pairs around O (tetrahedral electron geometry) but 2 are lone pairs → bent/V-shaped molecule. Bond angle ≈ 104.5° (less than 109.5° because lone pairs repel more than bonding pairs). Without lone pairs, students predict 180° (linear) — wrong.","reforge":{"stem":"Using VSEPR theory, predict the shape of NH₃ (ammonia). Nitrogen has 5 valence electrons.","options":{"A":"Trigonal pyramidal — three bonds, one lone pair","B":"Trigonal planar — three bonds and no lone pairs","C":"Linear — like CO₂.","D":"Tetrahedral — same as methane."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.3"},{"id":"CHEM-04","stem":"Which type of bonding explains why diamond has a very high melting point?","options":{"A":"Metallic bonding — delocalised electrons hold the structure together.","B":"Ionic bonding — alternating positive and negative ions.","C":"Van der Waals forces — strongest when molecular mass is high.","D":"Covalent bonding in a giant covalent lattice"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCHEM-BONDING-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Giant covalent structures: diamond (C), silicon (Si), silicon dioxide (SiO₂). High MP because: MANY strong covalent bonds must be broken throughout the lattice — requires enormous energy. Contrast with simple molecular covalent (e.g. CO₂, H₂O) — LOW MP because only weak intermolecular forces (London dispersion, dipole-dipole, H-bonding) between molecules are broken. The bonds WITHIN molecules are not broken on melting.","reforge":{"stem":"Iodine (I₂) has a much lower melting point than silicon dioxide (SiO₂), despite both being covalently bonded. The reason is:","options":{"A":"I₂ has weaker covalent bonds within its molecules.","B":"I₂ is simple molecular; SiO₂ is a giant lattice","C":"SiO₂ has ionic bonding that is stronger than covalent.","D":"Iodine atoms are lighter so the structure melts more easily."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.3"},{"id":"CHEM-05","stem":"A student confuses the terms 'empirical formula' and 'molecular formula.' Which correctly distinguishes them?","options":{"A":"Empirical formula shows the simplest whole-number ratio of atoms in a compound","B":"They are the same thing — different names for the same concept.","C":"Molecular formula is only used for gases.","D":"Empirical formula always has the same subscript numbers as the molecular formula."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCHEM-MOLES-FORMULAE","scaffold":"Empirical formula = simplest ratio. Molecular formula = actual. They can be identical (e.g. H₂O, HCl) or different (e.g. ethene: molecular C₂H₄, empirical CH₂). To find molecular from empirical: divide the molar mass by the empirical formula mass, then multiply all subscripts. This is tested in every moles/calculation paper.","reforge":{"stem":"A compound has an empirical formula of CH₂ and a molar mass of 56 g/mol. The empirical formula mass is 14 g/mol. What is the molecular formula?","options":{"A":"CH₂","B":"0.50 grams regardless of the substance's formula mass","C":"C₄H₈ — (56 ÷ 14 = 4), so multiply CH₂ by 4.","D":"C₃H₆"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.2"},{"id":"CHEM-06","stem":"Why does fluorine have a higher electronegativity than chlorine, despite chlorine having more protons?","options":{"A":"Fluorine has more electrons than chlorine.","B":"Fluorine has a smaller atomic radius","C":"Fluorine has a higher boiling point than chlorine.","D":"Chlorine's extra protons are cancelled out by its extra electrons."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ATOMIC-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Electronegativity increases across a period (more protons, same shielding) and decreases down a group (more shielding, larger radius). F is most electronegative element: small atom + low shielding + high effective nuclear charge → very strong pull on bonding electrons. Cl has 10 inner electrons shielding its nucleus; F only has 2. The shielding effect overwhelms the extra proton count.","reforge":{"stem":"Down Group 7 (F → Cl → Br → I), electronegativity decreases because:","options":{"A":"The number of protons decreases.","B":"The number of valence electrons changes.","C":"The halogens become less reactive.","D":"Radius and shielding both increase"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.3"},{"id":"CHEM-07","stem":"In a mass spectrometer, what is detected to produce the mass spectrum?","options":{"A":"The mass of neutral atoms.","B":"The colour of light emitted when atoms are heated.","C":"The mass-to-charge ratio (m/z) of positive ions","D":"The electrical charge of electrons in each atom."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCHEM-MASS-SPECTRA","scaffold":"Mass spectrometer steps: (1) Vaporise sample. (2) Ionise — high-energy electrons knock electrons off atoms → positive ions. (3) Accelerate through electric field. (4) Deflect in magnetic field — lighter/more charged ions deflect more. (5) Detect — ions hit detector, signal proportional to abundance. x-axis = m/z (mass-to-charge ratio), y-axis = relative abundance. Used to find Ar and identify isotopes.","reforge":{"stem":"In a mass spectrum of a sample of neon, two peaks appear at m/z = 20 and m/z = 22. What does this tell us?","options":{"A":"Neon has two isotopes, ²⁰Ne and ²²Ne","B":"Neon exists as two different molecules.","C":"Neon has lost two electrons in the ionisation stage.","D":"The mass spectrometer is incorrectly calibrated."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.1"},{"id":"CHEM-08","stem":"A student writes: 'Ionic compounds conduct electricity because they contain free electrons.' Why is this wrong?","options":{"A":"It isn't — ionic compounds do contain free electrons.","B":"Ionic compounds never conduct electricity.","C":"Ionic compounds conduct through electron movement in the solid state only.","D":"Ionic compounds conduct when molten or dissolved"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCHEM-BONDING-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Conduction in ionic compounds: solid = no (ions fixed in lattice). Molten or dissolved = yes (ions free to move). Charge carriers = ions (not electrons). Contrast with metals: conduct via delocalised electrons in all states. Contrast with graphite: conducts via delocalised electrons within layers. This distinction — ions vs electrons as charge carriers — is tested frequently.","reforge":{"stem":"Which correctly explains why metals conduct electricity in the solid state but ionic compounds do not?","options":{"A":"Metals have stronger bonds than ionic compounds.","B":"Metals have delocalised electrons","C":"Metals have a higher melting point.","D":"Ionic compounds have more electrons than metals."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.3"},{"id":"CHEM-E1-01","spec":"CHEM-1","stem":"The first ionisation energy is the energy needed to remove:","options":{"A":"one electron from each atom in one mole of gaseous atoms","B":"one proton from a nucleus","C":"It isn't wrong — atoms can legitimately have fractional numbers of protons.","D":"one neutron from an ion"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ATOMIC-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"The definition specifies gaseous atoms and formation of singly positive gaseous ions.","reforge":{"stem":"What happens when an atom in the gas phase loses its first electron?","options":{"A":"The second electron is removed from a higher energy level, further from the nucleus.","B":"A neutron is removed from the nucleus","C":"A gaseous positive ion forms and the required energy is its first ionisation energy","D":"The atom becomes a different isotope"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.1"},{"id":"CHEM-E1-02","spec":"CHEM-1","stem":"An isotope has the same number of protons but a different number of:","options":{"A":"electrons in every ion","B":"neutrons","C":"orbitals","D":"energy levels"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ATOMIC-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Isotopes are atoms of the same element with different mass numbers.","reforge":{"stem":"Two atoms have the same proton number but different mass numbers. How are they related?","options":{"A":"They have different electron shells but identical nuclei","B":"They are ions with different charges","C":"They are different elements with the same nucleus","D":"They are isotopes with different numbers of neutrons"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.1"},{"id":"CHEM-E1-03","spec":"CHEM-1","stem":"The relative atomic mass of an element is a weighted mean based on:","options":{"A":"only the most common isotope","B":"the number of electrons in a molecule","C":"isotopic masses and their abundances","D":"the boiling point"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ATOMIC-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Abundances determine how much each isotope contributes to the mean.","reforge":{"stem":"Why do abundant isotopes contribute more strongly to an element's relative atomic mass?","options":{"A":"The weighted mean gives their masses a larger contribution","B":"Only the least abundant isotope is included","C":"Electron number determines the mass instead","D":"Boiling point replaces isotopic abundance in the calculation"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.1"},{"id":"CHEM-E1-04","spec":"CHEM-1","stem":"One mole contains:","options":{"A":"one gram of every substance","B":"one atom of every element","C":"They are the same thing — different names for the same concept.","D":"the Avogadro constant number of specified particles"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCHEM-MOLES-FORMULAE","scaffold":"The particles may be atoms, molecules, ions or electrons as specified.","reforge":{"stem":"A sample contains 0.50 mol of water molecules. What does this amount represent?","options":{"A":"Exactly 0.50 water molecules","B":"0.50 times the Avogadro constant number of water molecules","C":"Empirical formula always has the same subscript numbers as the molecular formula.","D":"The same volume of water under every condition"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.2"},{"id":"CHEM-E1-05","spec":"CHEM-1","stem":"The empirical formula shows:","options":{"A":"the simplest whole-number ratio of atoms","B":"the exact three-dimensional shape","C":"the actual number of molecules in a sample","D":"the reaction mechanism"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCHEM-MOLES-FORMULAE","scaffold":"Molecular formulae can be multiples of empirical formulae.","reforge":{"stem":"A compound has molecular formula C6H12O6. What does its empirical formula show?","options":{"A":"The number of molecules in every sample","B":"Its exact three-dimensional molecular shape","C":"The simplest whole-number ratio of its atoms","D":"The sequence of steps in its reaction mechanism"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.2"},{"id":"CHEM-E1-06","spec":"CHEM-1","stem":"A coordinate bond forms when:","options":{"A":"Lone pairs are optional in dot-and-cross diagrams.","B":"both bonding electrons are donated by one atom","C":"an ionic lattice melts","D":"two neutrons are shared"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCHEM-BONDING-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"The shared pair still forms a covalent bond after donation.","reforge":{"stem":"In a coordinate covalent bond, where do the shared electrons originate?","options":{"A":"Two neutrons combine outside the nucleus","B":"Each atom loses a proton to create the pair","C":"The ionic lattice supplies electrons only when it melts","D":"One atom supplies both electrons in the shared pair"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.3"},{"id":"CHEM-E1-07","spec":"CHEM-1","stem":"A molecule with polar bonds may be non-polar overall if:","options":{"A":"it contains no electrons","B":"it has an ionic lattice","C":"bond dipoles cancel because of its symmetrical shape","D":"Ionic bonding — alternating positive and negative ions."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCHEM-BONDING-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Molecular shape determines whether individual bond dipoles cancel.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is carbon dioxide non-polar overall despite having polar C=O bonds?","options":{"A":"Its linear symmetry causes the bond dipoles to cancel","B":"Van der Waals forces — strongest when molecular mass is high.","C":"It forms an ionic lattice in the gas phase","D":"Its atoms have identical mass numbers"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.3"},{"id":"CHEM-E1-08","spec":"CHEM-1","stem":"A giant covalent structure generally has:","options":{"A":"SiO₂ has ionic bonding that is stronger than covalent.","B":"mobile ions in the solid","C":"low melting point in every case","D":"many strong covalent bonds and a high melting point"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCHEM-BONDING-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Diamond and graphite are giant structures with different properties due to bonding and layers.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does diamond have a high melting point?","options":{"A":"It contains only weak intermolecular attractions","B":"Many strong covalent bonds must be broken throughout its giant structure","C":"Its solid contains mobile ions","D":"Ionic compounds conduct through electron movement in the solid state only."},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.3"},{"id":"CHEM-E1-09","spec":"CHEM-1","stem":"An ionic compound conducts electricity when molten because:","options":{"A":"its ions are free to move","B":"its covalent bonds become electrons","C":"its atoms disappear","D":"its protons move through the circuit"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCHEM-BONDING-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Ions are fixed in a solid lattice but mobile when molten or dissolved.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can molten sodium chloride conduct electricity when solid sodium chloride cannot?","options":{"A":"The protons move through the external circuit","B":"Covalent bonds become free electrons on melting","C":"The ions are mobile in the molten state but fixed in the solid lattice","D":"Dative bonds are ionic in nature — the electron donor becomes positively charged."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.3"},{"id":"CHEM-E1B-01","spec":"CHEM-1","stem":"The electron arrangement of a magnesium atom is:","options":{"A":"2,8,8","B":"2,8,2","C":"2,10","D":"2,6,2"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ATOMIC-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Magnesium has atomic number 12, so its electrons occupy shells as 2,8,2.","reforge":{"stem":"A student applies the idea to this case: The electron arrangement of a magnesium atom is:","options":{"A":"2,8,2","B":"orbitals","C":"energy levels","D":"the boiling point"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.1"},{"id":"CHEM-N1-01","stem":"An atom of ³⁷Cl contains:","options":{"A":"37 protons, 17 neutrons and 37 electrons.","B":"20 protons, 17 neutrons and 20 electrons.","C":"17 protons, 20 neutrons and 17 electrons","D":"17 protons, 37 neutrons and 20 electrons."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ATOMIC-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Atomic number = proton number, and fixes the element. Mass number = protons + neutrons. So for ³⁷Cl: 17 protons (chlorine is element 17), 37 − 17 = 20 neutrons, and 17 electrons in a neutral atom. Isotopes of an element share proton number but differ in neutron number, so they have identical chemistry — electrons determine reactions — but different masses, which is what mass spectrometry separates.","reforge":{"stem":"Two isotopes of the same element always have the same:","options":{"A":"Number of protons and electrons","B":"Mass number for each atom.","C":"Number of neutrons in the nucleus.","D":"Density in the solid state."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.1"},{"id":"CHEM-N1-02","stem":"The electron configuration of a chromium atom is unusual because it is:","options":{"A":"[Ar]4s²3d⁴, following the normal filling order.","B":"[Ar]3d⁸, with no 4s electrons at all.","C":"[Ar]4s²3d⁶, with the 4s filled first.","D":"[Ar]4s¹3d⁵, giving a half-filled 3d subshell"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ATOMIC-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Chromium is [Ar]4s¹3d⁵ and copper is [Ar]4s¹3d¹⁰: promoting one 4s electron gives a half-filled or full 3d subshell, which is slightly lower in energy. Note that when transition metals form ions, the 4s electrons are lost first even though 4s fills first — so Fe²⁺ is [Ar]3d⁶, not [Ar]4s²3d⁴. That ordering catches students out more often than the chromium and copper exceptions themselves.","reforge":{"stem":"Which is the correct electron configuration of an Fe²⁺ ion?","options":{"A":"[Ar]4s²3d⁴, losing two 3d electrons.","B":"[Ar]3d⁶, losing 4s first","C":"[Ar]4s²3d⁶, unchanged from the atom.","D":"[Ar]4s¹3d⁵, promoting one electron."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.1"},{"id":"CHEM-N1-03","stem":"First ionisation energy generally decreases down a group because:","options":{"A":"The outer electron is further out, more shielded","B":"The outer electrons pair up in the same orbital.","C":"Nuclear charge decreases down the group.","D":"Atoms become more electronegative down the group."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ATOMIC-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Three factors set ionisation energy: nuclear charge, distance of the outer electron from the nucleus, and shielding by inner shells. Down a group, nuclear charge rises but distance and shielding rise more, so attraction on the outer electron weakens and ionisation energy falls. Across a period, nuclear charge rises with little change in shielding, so it generally increases — with dips at Group 3 (2p higher than 2s) and Group 6 (paired 2p electrons repel).","reforge":{"stem":"The first ionisation energy of sulfur is lower than that of phosphorus because in sulfur:","options":{"A":"There is an additional filled inner shell.","B":"The nuclear charge is smaller than phosphorus.","C":"Two electrons pair in one 3p orbital and repel","D":"The outer electron occupies a 4s orbital."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.1"},{"id":"CHEM-N1-04","stem":"In a time-of-flight mass spectrometer, ions are accelerated so that they all have the same:","options":{"A":"Velocity as they enter the flight tube.","B":"Kinetic energy entering the flight tube","C":"Number of neutrons in the nucleus.","D":"Mass-to-charge ratio on detection."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCHEM-MASS-SPECTRA","scaffold":"Stages: ionisation (electrospray or electron impact), acceleration to constant kinetic energy, drift through a field-free flight tube, then detection. Since KE = ½mv² is equal for all ions, heavier ions travel more slowly and arrive later, so time of flight gives m/z. The detector converts ion arrivals into current, and relative peak heights give isotopic abundances, from which relative atomic mass is calculated as a weighted mean.","reforge":{"stem":"In a mass spectrum, a heavier isotope reaches the detector later because it has a:","options":{"A":"Greater charge on each ion formed.","B":"Longer path length through the tube.","C":"Smaller mass-to-charge ratio overall.","D":"Lower velocity at equal energy"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.1"},{"id":"CHEM-N1-05","stem":"One mole of any substance contains:","options":{"A":"24 dm³ of gas at any temperature.","B":"12 g of that substance exactly.","C":"6.02 × 10²³ particles","D":"The same mass as one mole of carbon."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCHEM-MOLES-FORMULAE","scaffold":"A mole is 6.02 × 10²³ particles (the Avogadro constant). Key relationships: moles = mass ÷ Mr; moles = concentration × volume in dm³; and for a gas at room temperature and pressure, moles = volume in dm³ ÷ 24. The molar gas volume applies only at the stated conditions, and the ideal gas equation pV = nRT covers the general case with pressure in Pa, volume in m³ and temperature in K.","reforge":{"stem":"How many moles are present in 88 g of carbon dioxide (Mr = 44)?","options":{"A":"2 moles, from 88 divided by 44","B":"0.5 moles, from 44 divided by 88.","C":"44 moles, from 88 divided by 2.","D":"3872 moles, from 88 multiplied by 44."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.2"},{"id":"CHEM-N1-06","stem":"The empirical formula of a compound gives the:","options":{"A":"Actual number of atoms in one molecule.","B":"Arrangement of atoms in three dimensions.","C":"Total mass of one mole of the compound.","D":"Simplest whole-number ratio of atoms"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCHEM-MOLES-FORMULAE","scaffold":"Empirical formula is the simplest whole-number ratio; molecular formula gives the actual numbers and is a whole-number multiple of it. To find an empirical formula: divide each percentage or mass by the atomic mass, divide through by the smallest result, then scale to whole numbers. Ethane is CH₃ empirical, C₂H₆ molecular. Ionic compounds and giant structures are always written as empirical formulae since no discrete molecule exists.","reforge":{"stem":"A compound has empirical formula CH₂ and a relative molecular mass of 56. Its molecular formula is:","options":{"A":"CH₂, unchanged from the empirical formula.","B":"C₄H₈, since 56 ÷ 14 gives 4","C":"C₂H₄, since 56 ÷ 28 gives 2.","D":"C₅H₁₀, since 56 is close to 70."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.2"},{"id":"CHEM-N1-07","stem":"The percentage yield of a reaction is calculated as:","options":{"A":"Actual yield divided by theoretical yield, times 100","B":"Mass of product divided by mass of all reactants.","C":"Theoretical yield divided by actual yield, times 100.","D":"Moles of product divided by moles of solvent."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCHEM-MOLES-FORMULAE","scaffold":"Percentage yield = (actual ÷ theoretical) × 100, and is below 100% because of incomplete reaction, reversible equilibria, competing side reactions, and losses during transfer and purification. Atom economy = (Mr of desired product ÷ total Mr of reactants) × 100, and measures how much of the reactant mass ends up as the wanted product. Addition reactions have 100% atom economy; substitution reactions do not.","reforge":{"stem":"An addition reaction has a higher atom economy than a substitution reaction because it:","options":{"A":"Always produces a higher percentage yield.","B":"Requires no catalyst to proceed.","C":"Forms only one product, wasting no atoms","D":"Runs at a lower temperature."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.2"},{"id":"CHEM-N1-08","stem":"An ionic bond is best described as the electrostatic attraction between:","options":{"A":"Nuclei and a shared pair of electrons.","B":"Oppositely charged ions in a lattice","C":"Positive ions and delocalised electrons.","D":"Permanent dipoles on adjacent molecules."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCHEM-BONDING-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Ionic: electrons transferred from metal to non-metal, giving oppositely charged ions held in a giant lattice — high melting point, brittle, conducts only when molten or dissolved. Covalent: a shared pair attracted to both nuclei. Metallic: positive ions in a sea of delocalised electrons — conducts when solid, malleable. Ionic character increases with electronegativity difference, and bonding sits on a continuum rather than in strict categories.","reforge":{"stem":"Sodium chloride conducts electricity when molten but not when solid because the ions:","options":{"A":"Gain extra electrons on melting.","B":"Lose their charge in the solid lattice.","C":"Become covalently bonded when solid.","D":"Are free to move only in the liquid"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.3"},{"id":"CHEM-N1-09","stem":"A dative covalent bond differs from an ordinary covalent bond because:","options":{"A":"It is significantly weaker once formed.","B":"The electrons are transferred rather than shared.","C":"Both shared electrons come from one atom","D":"It forms only between identical atoms."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCHEM-BONDING-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"In a dative (coordinate) bond both electrons in the shared pair come from one atom, usually via a lone pair — as in NH₄⁺, where nitrogen's lone pair bonds to H⁺, and in H₃O⁺ and complex ions with ligands. Once formed it is identical in length and strength to any other covalent bond of that type, which is why all four N–H bonds in the ammonium ion are equivalent. It is shown by an arrow from donor to acceptor.","reforge":{"stem":"All four N–H bonds in the ammonium ion are identical in length. This shows that a dative bond:","options":{"A":"Is identical to any covalent bond","B":"Prevents the ion from carrying a charge.","C":"Only forms at high temperature.","D":"Is weaker than a normal covalent bond."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.3"},{"id":"CHEM-N1-10","stem":"Applying electron pair repulsion theory, a molecule of ammonia is:","options":{"A":"Trigonal planar with bond angles of 120°.","B":"Linear with bond angles of 180°.","C":"Tetrahedral with bond angles of 109.5°.","D":"Pyramidal with bond angles of about 107°"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCHEM-BONDING-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Electron pairs repel to maximum separation, and lone pairs repel more strongly than bonding pairs, reducing bond angles by about 2.5° each. Methane: 4 bonding pairs, tetrahedral, 109.5°. Ammonia: 3 bonding + 1 lone, pyramidal, 107°. Water: 2 bonding + 2 lone, bent, 104.5°. Count total electron pairs around the central atom first, then subtract for the shape actually described, which refers only to atom positions.","reforge":{"stem":"The bond angle in water is smaller than in ammonia because water has:","options":{"A":"A greater number of bonding pairs.","B":"Two lone pairs, repelling more","C":"A heavier central atom than nitrogen.","D":"Hydrogen bonding between molecules."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.3"},{"id":"CHEM-N1-11","stem":"Electronegativity is best defined as the ability of an atom to:","options":{"A":"Attract a bonding pair of electrons","B":"Conduct electricity in the molten state.","C":"Lose electrons and form a positive ion.","D":"Form the largest number of covalent bonds."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCHEM-BONDING-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Electronegativity rises across a period as nuclear charge increases with similar shielding, and falls down a group as the bonding pair sits further from the nucleus and is more shielded. Fluorine is the most electronegative element. A difference between bonded atoms creates a polar bond with δ+ and δ− ends. A molecule is only polar overall if those bond dipoles do not cancel by symmetry — CO₂ is non-polar despite polar bonds.","reforge":{"stem":"Carbon dioxide has polar bonds but is a non-polar molecule because the:","options":{"A":"Bonds are too weak to create a dipole.","B":"Oxygen atoms have no lone pairs.","C":"Dipoles cancel in a linear molecule","D":"Molecule contains only double bonds."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.3"},{"id":"CHEM-N1-12","stem":"Van der Waals (London) forces arise from:","options":{"A":"Permanent dipoles on polar molecules only.","B":"Instantaneous dipoles inducing others","C":"Hydrogen bonded to nitrogen, oxygen or fluorine.","D":"The transfer of electrons between molecules."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCHEM-BONDING-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Intermolecular forces in increasing strength: London forces (present in all molecules, from momentary uneven electron distribution, stronger with more electrons and greater surface contact); permanent dipole–dipole (polar molecules); hydrogen bonding (H bonded to N, O or F interacting with a lone pair). These determine melting and boiling points, not the covalent bonds inside molecules, which are unbroken when a simple molecular substance melts.","reforge":{"stem":"Boiling a sample of liquid bromine requires energy to overcome:","options":{"A":"The covalent bonds within each Br₂ molecule.","B":"Hydrogen bonds between bromine molecules.","C":"Ionic attractions in a giant lattice.","D":"London forces between the molecules"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.3"},{"id":"CHEM-N1-13","stem":"Water has an unusually high boiling point for its molecular mass because of:","options":{"A":"Its very strong covalent O–H bonds.","B":"Its giant covalent lattice structure.","C":"Hydrogen bonding between molecules","D":"Ionic attraction between H⁺ and OH⁻."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCHEM-BONDING-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Each water molecule can form up to four hydrogen bonds — two through its lone pairs and two through its hydrogens — giving an extensive network that requires much energy to break, hence the high boiling point. Ice is less dense than liquid water because hydrogen bonds hold the molecules in an open lattice with the molecules further apart on average than in the liquid, which is why ice floats.","reforge":{"stem":"Ice is less dense than liquid water because hydrogen bonds hold molecules:","options":{"A":"In an open lattice, further apart","B":"In rapidly moving random positions.","C":"Closer together than in the liquid.","D":"In pairs rather than larger clusters."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.3"},{"id":"CHEM-N1-14","stem":"Diamond has a very high melting point because it has:","options":{"A":"Strong London forces between layers.","B":"Delocalised electrons throughout the structure.","C":"Ions held in a regular repeating lattice.","D":"A giant covalent lattice of strong bonds"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCHEM-BONDING-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Diamond: each carbon bonded to four others in a giant covalent lattice, very hard, extremely high melting point, non-conducting since all outer electrons are in bonds. Graphite: three bonds per carbon in layers with weak forces between them, so layers slide (lubricant), and the fourth delocalised electron allows conduction along layers. Both are carbon: the difference is structure, which is why properties differ so sharply.","reforge":{"stem":"Graphite conducts electricity but diamond does not because in graphite each carbon atom:","options":{"A":"Forms four bonds instead of three.","B":"Has one delocalised electron","C":"Carries a permanent negative charge.","D":"Is held by weak London forces only."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.3"},{"id":"CHEM-N1-15","stem":"Metals are malleable because:","options":{"A":"Layers of ions can slide past each other","B":"Covalent bonds break and reform easily.","C":"The ions are held only by London forces.","D":"Delocalised electrons hold ions rigidly in place."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCHEM-BONDING-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Metallic bonding is the attraction between positive ions and a sea of delocalised electrons. Layers of ions can slide without breaking the bonding, since the electron sea is non-directional — hence malleability and ductility. Delocalised electrons also carry charge and heat, giving high conductivity. Bond strength, and so melting point, rises with greater ionic charge and smaller ionic radius: magnesium melts far higher than sodium.","reforge":{"stem":"Magnesium has a higher melting point than sodium mainly because Mg²⁺ ions:","options":{"A":"Are larger than sodium ions.","B":"Form covalent bonds with each other.","C":"A higher charge and more electrons","D":"Are held together by hydrogen bonding."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.3"},{"id":"CHEM-N1-16","stem":"In the equation pV = nRT, the temperature must be expressed in:","options":{"A":"Degrees Celsius above freezing point.","B":"Kelvin, the absolute scale","C":"Whatever unit the pressure uses.","D":"Degrees Fahrenheit for consistency."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCHEM-MOLES-FORMULAE","scaffold":"For pV = nRT use SI units throughout: pressure in pascals, volume in m³ (1 dm³ = 1 × 10⁻³ m³), temperature in kelvin (K = °C + 273), n in moles, R = 8.31 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹. Unit conversion is where most marks are lost, particularly cm³ to m³ (divide by 10⁶). The equation assumes ideal behaviour: negligible molecular volume and no intermolecular forces, which fails at high pressure and low temperature.","reforge":{"stem":"Real gases deviate most from ideal behaviour at:","options":{"A":"High temperature and low pressure.","B":"Standard room temperature and pressure.","C":"High temperature and high volume.","D":"Low temperature and high pressure"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.2"},{"id":"CHEM-N1-17","stem":"A student dissolves 0.1 mol of solute in water and makes the solution up to 500 cm³. The concentration is:","options":{"A":"0.05 mol dm⁻³, from 0.1 × 0.5.","B":"50 mol dm⁻³, from 0.1 × 500.","C":"0.2 mol dm⁻³, from 0.1 ÷ 0.5","D":"2.0 mol dm⁻³, from 500 ÷ 0.1 ÷ 1000."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCHEM-MOLES-FORMULAE","scaffold":"Concentration (mol dm⁻³) = moles ÷ volume in dm³, so convert cm³ to dm³ by dividing by 1000 first: 500 cm³ = 0.5 dm³, giving 0.1 ÷ 0.5 = 0.2 mol dm⁻³. In titration calculations, find moles of the known solution from concentration × volume, use the balanced equation's ratio to get moles of the unknown, then divide by its volume. Forgetting the cm³ to dm³ conversion is the commonest error.","reforge":{"stem":"In a titration, 25.0 cm³ of 0.100 mol dm⁻³ NaOH reacts exactly with HCl in a 1:1 ratio. Moles of HCl are:","options":{"A":"2.50 × 10⁻³ mol","B":"2.50 mol, from 25.0 × 0.100.","C":"0.250 mol, from 25.0 ÷ 100.","D":"4.00 × 10⁻³ mol, from 0.100 ÷ 25.0."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.2"},{"id":"CHEM-N1-18","stem":"In a titration, the burette is rinsed with the solution it will contain rather than with water because water would:","options":{"A":"React chemically with the burette glass.","B":"Prevent the indicator changing colour.","C":"Make the tap leak during the titration.","D":"Dilute it, giving a larger titre"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCHEM-INORGANIC-TESTS","scaffold":"Rinse the burette and pipette with the solutions they will hold; the conical flask may be rinsed with distilled water, since extra water there does not change the moles of substance present. Take an initial rough titration then repeat until titres agree within 0.10 cm³, and average only those concordant results. Read the bottom of the meniscus at eye level to avoid parallax error, and add dropwise near the endpoint.","reforge":{"stem":"Concordant titres in a titration are those that agree within:","options":{"A":"1.00 cm³ of one another.","B":"0.10 cm³ of one another","C":"5.00 cm³ of one another.","D":"0.01 cm³ of one another."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.2"},{"id":"CHEM-N1-19","stem":"Adding acidified silver nitrate to a solution produces a cream precipitate. The halide present is:","options":{"A":"Bromide, which gives a cream precipitate","B":"Chloride, which gives a white precipitate.","C":"Iodide, which gives a yellow precipitate.","D":"Fluoride, which gives no precipitate."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCHEM-INORGANIC-TESTS","scaffold":"Silver nitrate halide tests: chloride gives white AgCl (dissolves in dilute ammonia); bromide gives cream AgBr (dissolves in concentrated ammonia); iodide gives pale yellow AgI (insoluble in concentrated ammonia); fluoride gives no precipitate. Acidify with dilute nitric acid first to remove carbonate and hydroxide ions, which would otherwise precipitate and give a false positive. Solubility in ammonia distinguishes the three.","reforge":{"stem":"Nitric acid is added before silver nitrate in the halide test in order to:","options":{"A":"Increase the solubility of the silver halide.","B":"Speed up the precipitation reaction.","C":"Remove carbonate ions that also precipitate","D":"Convert the halide ions into halogens."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.6"},{"id":"CHEM-N1-20","stem":"A gas turns damp red litmus paper blue. The gas is:","options":{"A":"Carbon dioxide, from a carbonate.","B":"Ammonia, from warming with alkali","C":"Hydrogen, from a metal and acid.","D":"Chlorine, from an oxidation reaction."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCHEM-INORGANIC-TESTS","scaffold":"Gas tests: ammonia turns damp red litmus blue and is released when an ammonium salt is warmed with sodium hydroxide; carbon dioxide turns limewater milky; hydrogen gives a squeaky pop with a lit splint; oxygen relights a glowing splint; chlorine bleaches damp litmus. For anions: carbonate fizzes with acid giving CO₂; sulfate gives a white precipitate with acidified barium chloride; halides are identified with acidified silver nitrate.","reforge":{"stem":"Acidified barium chloride added to a solution gives a white precipitate. This indicates:","options":{"A":"Ammonium ions are present.","B":"Carbonate ions are present.","C":"Chloride ions are present.","D":"Sulfate ions are present"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.6"},{"id":"CHEM-N1-21","stem":"Successive ionisation energies for an element show a large jump between the third and fourth values. The element is in:","options":{"A":"Group 1, with one outer electron.","B":"Group 2, with two outer electrons.","C":"Group 3, with three outer electrons","D":"Group 4, with four outer electrons."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ATOMIC-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"A large jump appears when the next electron must come from a shell closer to the nucleus with less shielding. The number of electrons removed before the jump equals the number in the outer shell, giving the group number. A jump between the 3rd and 4th means three outer electrons, so Group 3. Successive ionisation energies always increase, since removing an electron from an increasingly positive ion is progressively harder.","reforge":{"stem":"Successive ionisation energies of any element always increase because each electron is removed from a:","options":{"A":"Progressively more positive ion","B":"Shell with increasing shielding.","C":"Larger and more diffuse atom.","D":"Region of lower nuclear charge."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.1"},{"id":"CHEM-N1-22","stem":"What is the correct view of the atomic radius trend across Period 3?","options":{"A":"It increases because more shells are added.","B":"It stays constant since the shell number is fixed.","C":"It increases because shielding rises sharply.","D":"It decreases as nuclear charge rises"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ATOMIC-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Across a period, protons are added to the nucleus while electrons enter the same shell, so shielding barely changes and the increasing nuclear charge pulls the outer shell inwards — atomic radius decreases. Down a group, an extra shell is added each time and shielding rises, so radius increases. Ionic radii follow related trends: cations are smaller than their atoms, anions larger, because of the changed electron-to-proton ratio.","reforge":{"stem":"A sodium ion is smaller than a sodium atom because the ion has:","options":{"A":"Gained an electron into a new shell.","B":"Lost its outer shell entirely","C":"A smaller nuclear charge than the atom.","D":"More shielding from inner electrons."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.1"},{"id":"CHEM-N1-23","stem":"The mass spectrum of an element shows peaks at m/z 24 (79%), 25 (10%) and 26 (11%). Its relative atomic mass is:","options":{"A":"24.3, the weighted mean","B":"25.0, the middle of the three values.","C":"75.0, the sum of the three m/z values.","D":"24.0, the most abundant isotope alone."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCHEM-MASS-SPECTRA","scaffold":"Relative atomic mass is the weighted mean: multiply each isotope's mass by its fractional abundance and add. Here (0.79×24) + (0.10×25) + (0.11×26) = 18.96 + 2.50 + 2.86 = 24.32, so 24.3 — magnesium. Two common errors are taking a simple average of the masses, ignoring abundance, and quoting only the most abundant isotope. Where abundances are given as peak heights, divide each by the total first.","reforge":{"stem":"Relative atomic mass is calculated as a weighted mean rather than a simple average because:","options":{"A":"Isotopes have different chemical properties.","B":"Peak heights are always equal in a spectrum.","C":"The isotopes are present in unequal amounts","D":"Mass spectrometers cannot detect light isotopes."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.1"},{"id":"CHEM-N1-24","stem":"A molecule of BF₃ is trigonal planar because the central boron atom has:","options":{"A":"Four bonding pairs and no lone pairs.","B":"Three bonding pairs and no lone pairs","C":"Three bonding pairs and one lone pair.","D":"Two bonding pairs and two lone pairs."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCHEM-BONDING-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Count electron pairs around the central atom, then subtract lone pairs to name the shape: 2 pairs linear (180°), 3 trigonal planar (120°), 4 tetrahedral (109.5°), 5 trigonal bipyramidal (120° and 90°), 6 octahedral (90°). BF₃ has three bonding pairs and no lone pairs, so it is trigonal planar. Boron is an exception to the octet rule here, having only six electrons in its outer shell.","reforge":{"stem":"A molecule with six bonding pairs and no lone pairs around the central atom is:","options":{"A":"Trigonal bipyramidal, with 120° and 90° angles.","B":"Tetrahedral, with 109.5° bond angles.","C":"Trigonal planar, with 120° bond angles.","D":"Octahedral, with 90° bond angles"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.3"},{"id":"CHEM-N1-25","stem":"Iodine has a higher boiling point than chlorine because iodine molecules have:","options":{"A":"Stronger covalent bonds within the molecule.","B":"Permanent dipoles that chlorine lacks.","C":"More electrons, so stronger forces","D":"Hydrogen bonding between the molecules."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCHEM-BONDING-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Down Group 7 the molecules gain electrons, so instantaneous dipoles are larger and London forces stronger, raising melting and boiling points: chlorine gas, bromine liquid, iodine solid at room temperature. The covalent bond within each molecule actually weakens down the group, but that bond is not broken on boiling — a distinction worth stating explicitly, since it is a frequent source of confused answers.","reforge":{"stem":"When iodine sublimes, the forces overcome are:","options":{"A":"London forces between I₂ molecules","B":"Ionic attractions in the crystal lattice.","C":"The I–I covalent bonds within molecules.","D":"Hydrogen bonds linking the molecules."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.3"},{"id":"CHEM-N1-26","stem":"In the reaction Mg + 2HCl → MgCl₂ + H₂, magnesium is:","options":{"A":"Acting as a catalyst only.","B":"Reduced, gaining electrons.","C":"Neither oxidised nor reduced.","D":"Oxidised, losing electrons"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ACIDS-REDOX","scaffold":"Oxidation Is Loss of electrons, Reduction Is Gain. Magnesium goes from 0 to +2, losing two electrons, so it is oxidised and acts as the reducing agent. Hydrogen goes from +1 to 0, gaining electrons, so it is reduced and HCl is the oxidising agent. Assign oxidation numbers systematically: uncombined elements 0, simple ions their charge, oxygen usually −2, hydrogen usually +1, and the total equals the overall charge.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the oxidation number of sulfur in the sulfate ion, SO₄²⁻?","options":{"A":"−2, the same as the overall charge.","B":"+6, since 4 × (−2) plus x equals −2","C":"+4, matching the number of oxygens.","D":"0, since it is a combined element."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.7"},{"id":"CHEM-N1-27","stem":"A Brønsted–Lowry acid is defined as a species that:","options":{"A":"Donates a proton","B":"Releases hydroxide ions in water.","C":"Increases the pH of a solution.","D":"Accepts a pair of electrons."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ACIDS-REDOX","scaffold":"Brønsted–Lowry: an acid donates a proton, a base accepts one. Each acid has a conjugate base formed by losing H⁺, and each base a conjugate acid. Strong acids dissociate fully so [H⁺] equals the acid concentration; weak acids dissociate partially, needing Ka. Note strength (extent of dissociation) is separate from concentration (amount per volume) — a concentrated weak acid is entirely possible.","reforge":{"stem":"A concentrated solution of ethanoic acid has a higher pH than a dilute solution of hydrochloric acid because ethanoic acid:","options":{"A":"Contains fewer hydrogen atoms per molecule.","B":"Is present at a lower concentration.","C":"Only partially dissociates in solution","D":"Acts as a base rather than an acid."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.12"},{"id":"CHEM-N1-28","stem":"Which pair of substances would form a buffer solution?","options":{"A":"A strong acid and a strong base in equal amounts.","B":"A weak acid and its salt with a strong base","C":"A strong acid and water in any proportion.","D":"A weak base and a strong base together."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ACIDS-REDOX","scaffold":"A buffer resists pH change on adding small amounts of acid or alkali, and is made from a weak acid with its conjugate base (e.g. ethanoic acid and sodium ethanoate), or a weak base with its conjugate acid. Added H⁺ is removed by the conjugate base and added OH⁻ by the weak acid, so pH shifts little. pH is calculated from Ka and the acid-to-salt ratio; a buffer works best when that ratio is near 1.","reforge":{"stem":"When a small amount of acid is added to an ethanoic acid / ethanoate buffer, the pH changes little because:","options":{"A":"The buffer prevents any reaction occurring.","B":"The water present neutralises all the acid.","C":"Ethanoic acid dissociates completely.","D":"The ethanoate ions react with the added H⁺"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.12"},{"id":"CHEM-N1-29","stem":"A student must dilute a concentrated acid. The correct procedure is to:","options":{"A":"Add water to the concentrated acid quickly.","B":"Mix equal volumes in a stoppered flask.","C":"Add acid to water slowly, stirring","D":"Heat the acid before adding any water."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCHEM-INORGANIC-TESTS","scaffold":"Dilution is strongly exothermic, so acid is added to water — the larger volume of water absorbs the heat released. Adding water to concentrated acid can make the small volume boil and spit corrosive liquid. Standard precautions: eye protection, work over a tray or sink, add slowly with stirring, and allow the solution to cool before making up to the mark in a volumetric flask, since warm liquid contracts on cooling.","reforge":{"stem":"A solution is made up to the mark in a volumetric flask while still warm. The measured concentration will be:","options":{"A":"Too high; it contracts on cooling","B":"Impossible to determine at all.","C":"Too low, since the liquid expanded.","D":"Unaffected by the temperature."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.12"},{"id":"CHEM-N1-30","stem":"The bonding in an ammonium ion, NH₄⁺, includes:","options":{"A":"Two dative bonds and two ionic bonds.","B":"Four ionic bonds between N⁻ and H⁺.","C":"Three covalent bonds and one hydrogen bond.","D":"Four identical bonds, one dative"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCHEM-BONDING-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Ammonia has three N–H bonds and one lone pair. That lone pair forms a dative covalent bond to H⁺, giving NH₄⁺ with four bonds. Once formed all four are identical, and the ion is tetrahedral with 109.5° angles — the lone pair is now bonding, so there is no lone-pair compression. The positive charge belongs to the ion as a whole rather than sitting on any one atom.","reforge":{"stem":"The bond angle in the ammonium ion is 109.5° rather than 107° because the ion has:","options":{"A":"A heavier central atom than ammonia.","B":"No remaining lone pair on nitrogen","C":"An overall positive charge.","D":"Weaker N–H bonds than ammonia."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.3"},{"id":"CHEM-N1-31","stem":"Which species has the same number of electrons as a neon atom?","options":{"A":"A sodium ion, Na⁺","B":"A magnesium atom, Mg.","C":"A chloride ion, Cl⁻.","D":"A fluorine atom, F."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ATOMIC-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Isoelectronic species have the same electron count. Neon has 10. Na⁺ (11 protons, 10 electrons), Mg²⁺, F⁻ and O²⁻ are all isoelectronic with neon. Among isoelectronic species, the one with the most protons has the smallest radius, since the same electron cloud is pulled in more strongly — so the order of size runs O²⁻ > F⁻ > Ne > Na⁺ > Mg²⁺.","reforge":{"stem":"Among the isoelectronic species O²⁻, F⁻, Na⁺ and Mg²⁺, the smallest is:","options":{"A":"O²⁻, with the fewest protons.","B":"F⁻, with one negative charge.","C":"Mg²⁺, with the most protons","D":"Na⁺, with eleven protons."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.1"},{"id":"CHEM-N1-32","stem":"The molar mass of calcium carbonate, CaCO₃, is:","options":{"A":"68 g mol⁻¹, adding Ca and C only.","B":"100 g mol⁻¹, from 40 + 12 + 48","C":"84 g mol⁻¹, using one oxygen atom.","D":"140 g mol⁻¹, counting calcium twice."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCHEM-MOLES-FORMULAE","scaffold":"Molar mass sums the relative atomic masses of every atom in the formula: Ca 40 + C 12 + (O 16 × 3 = 48) = 100 g mol⁻¹. Watch brackets, since Ca(OH)₂ contains two oxygens and two hydrogens, and water of crystallisation, since CuSO₄·5H₂O includes 5 × 18 = 90. Errors in molar mass propagate through every subsequent calculation, so it is worth checking before continuing.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the molar mass of Ca(OH)₂?","options":{"A":"57 g mol⁻¹, counting one OH group.","B":"114 g mol⁻¹, doubling the calcium.","C":"58 g mol⁻¹, ignoring the hydrogens.","D":"74 g mol⁻¹, from 40 + 2(16 + 1)"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.2"},{"id":"CHEM-N1-33","stem":"A reaction is described as exothermic when:","options":{"A":"The products have more energy than the reactants.","B":"The surroundings become cooler during it.","C":"Energy is released, so ΔH is negative","D":"It requires a catalyst to proceed at all."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ENERGETICS","scaffold":"Exothermic: energy released to the surroundings, temperature rises, ΔH negative, products lower in energy than reactants — combustion, neutralisation, most oxidations. Endothermic: energy absorbed, temperature falls, ΔH positive — thermal decomposition, photosynthesis. The sign convention describes the system, so a rising thermometer reading in the surroundings corresponds to a negative ΔH, which is a frequent point of confusion.","reforge":{"stem":"A student dissolves a salt and the temperature of the solution falls. The dissolving process is:","options":{"A":"Endothermic, with a positive ΔH","B":"Exothermic, with a negative ΔH.","C":"At equilibrium, so ΔH is zero.","D":"Catalysed by the water present."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.4"},{"id":"CHEM-N1-34","stem":"Hess's law allows an enthalpy change to be calculated indirectly because the total enthalpy change is:","options":{"A":"Always zero for a complete cycle of steps.","B":"Determined only by the activation energy.","C":"Proportional to the number of steps used.","D":"Independent of the route taken"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ENERGETICS","scaffold":"Hess's law states that the enthalpy change of a reaction is the same regardless of route, since enthalpy is a state function. This lets values be found that cannot be measured directly, such as the enthalpy of formation of an unstable compound. Using formation data: ΔH = ΣΔHf(products) − ΣΔHf(reactants). Using combustion data the arrows point the other way: ΔH = ΣΔHc(reactants) − ΣΔHc(products).","reforge":{"stem":"Using standard enthalpies of formation, the enthalpy change of a reaction equals:","options":{"A":"Reactants minus products, then halved.","B":"Products minus reactants","C":"The sum of all values in the cycle.","D":"Products plus reactants."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.4"},{"id":"CHEM-N1-35","stem":"Bond enthalpy calculations give only approximate values because tabulated bond enthalpies are:","options":{"A":"Mean values across compounds","B":"Always quoted for the liquid state.","C":"Calculated rather than measured.","D":"Measured only for ionic compounds."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ENERGETICS","scaffold":"Mean bond enthalpies are averages across many different compounds, so the actual C–H bond in methane differs slightly from that in ethanol. They also apply to gaseous species, so calculations involving liquids or solids ignore the enthalpy of vaporisation. ΔH = bonds broken − bonds made: breaking is endothermic and making is exothermic, so a reaction is exothermic when the bonds formed are stronger overall.","reforge":{"stem":"Using bond enthalpies, a reaction is exothermic when the energy needed to break bonds is:","options":{"A":"Exactly equal to the energy released.","B":"Greater than the energy released making bonds.","C":"Less than the energy released making bonds","D":"Zero for all the reactant molecules."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.4"},{"id":"CHEM-N1-36","stem":"The standard enthalpy of formation of an element in its standard state is:","options":{"A":"Always negative and large.","B":"Zero by definition","C":"Equal to its enthalpy of combustion.","D":"Impossible to define."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ENERGETICS","scaffold":"Standard enthalpy of formation is the change when one mole of a compound forms from its elements in their standard states under standard conditions (100 kPa, stated temperature, usually 298 K). For an element already in its standard state no change occurs, so the value is zero — which is why elements drop out of Hess cycle calculations. Standard enthalpy of combustion is the change on completely burning one mole in excess oxygen.","reforge":{"stem":"Standard enthalpy of combustion is defined for the complete burning of:","options":{"A":"Any convenient mass of the substance.","B":"The substance until oxygen runs out.","C":"One gram of the substance in air.","D":"One mole in excess oxygen"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.4"},{"id":"CHEM-N1-37","stem":"In a calorimetry experiment, the main reason the measured enthalpy change is smaller than the true value is:","options":{"A":"Using distilled water rather than tap water.","B":"The reaction going to completion too quickly.","C":"Heat loss to the surroundings","D":"The thermometer reading in Celsius."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ENERGETICS","scaffold":"Calorimetry uses q = mcΔT, where m is the mass of solution (not of the reactant), c is 4.18 J g⁻¹ K⁻¹ for aqueous solutions, and ΔT the temperature change; divide by moles for ΔH per mole, and make it negative if exothermic. Errors: heat lost to surroundings and apparatus, incomplete reaction, and evaporation. Insulation, a lid, and extrapolating a cooling curve back to the moment of mixing all reduce the loss.","reforge":{"stem":"In q = mcΔT for a neutralisation in solution, m refers to the mass of:","options":{"A":"The solution in the calorimeter","B":"The calorimeter and its lid.","C":"The acid added to the flask only.","D":"The solid reactant used."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.4"},{"id":"CHEM-N4-01","stem":"A student reports a titre of 24.5 cm³ from a burette. The uncertainty in that titre is:","options":{"A":"±1.00 cm³, the practical limit of reading.","B":"±0.05 cm³, from a single reading.","C":"±0.50 cm³, the smallest scale division.","D":"±0.10 cm³, from two readings"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCHEM-INORGANIC-TESTS","scaffold":"A burette is read to ±0.05 cm³, and a titre needs an initial and a final reading, so uncertainties add to ±0.10 cm³. Percentage uncertainty = (uncertainty ÷ measurement) × 100, so a larger titre reduces the percentage error — one reason to use a titre above 20 cm³. Overall uncertainty in a calculated result is found by adding the percentage uncertainties of each measurement contributing to it.","reforge":{"stem":"To reduce the percentage uncertainty in a titration, a student should aim for a titre that is:","options":{"A":"As small as the burette can measure.","B":"As large as the burette allows","C":"Exactly equal to the pipette volume.","D":"Unrelated to the uncertainty involved."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.2"}]},"CHEM-2":{"label":"Bonding, Energetics & Equilibria","color":"#0891b2","questions":[{"id":"CHEM-09","stem":"A student says water has a high boiling point 'because the O–H covalent bonds are very strong'. What is the correct explanation?","options":{"A":"The high boiling point is due to hydrogen bonding between molecules","B":"The O–H bonds break during boiling, requiring large energy.","C":"Water molecules are unusually heavy for their size.","D":"Dative bonds share three electrons rather than two, making them stronger."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCHEM-BONDING-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Boiling breaks INTERmolecular forces, never the covalent bonds within molecules. Water's high bp comes from hydrogen bonding (H bonded to O, plus a lone pair on O). Order of strength: covalent/ionic bonds >> hydrogen bonds > permanent dipole-dipole > London (induced dipole) forces. Classic error: attributing physical properties (bp, mp) to bond breaking rather than to overcoming intermolecular forces. Only in giant covalent structures (diamond, SiO2) does melting break covalent bonds.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does HF have a higher boiling point than HCl, despite HCl having more electrons?","options":{"A":"HCl molecules are smaller and pack more loosely.","B":"HCl is a giant covalent structure.","C":"HF forms hydrogen bonds, stronger than dipoles","D":"Chlorine is less reactive than fluorine."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.3"},{"id":"CHEM-10","stem":"An exothermic reaction has which sign for ΔH, and what happens to the energy of the surroundings?","options":{"A":"ΔH is positive; the surroundings cool down.","B":"ΔH is negative; energy is released to the surroundings, which heat up.","C":"ΔH is positive; the surroundings heat up.","D":"It must have a positive activation energy alone under standard conditions"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ENERGETICS","scaffold":"Exothermic: energy released to surroundings, ΔH negative, surroundings warm, products lower in energy than reactants. Endothermic: energy absorbed, ΔH positive, surroundings cool. The sign refers to the SYSTEM. Common error: confusing the system and surroundings, or thinking 'releases energy' means positive ΔH. Bond breaking is endothermic (needs energy); bond making is exothermic (releases energy). ΔH = energy to break bonds − energy released making bonds.","reforge":{"stem":"Using bond enthalpies, a reaction is found to be exothermic. This means:","options":{"A":"The activation energy was zero.","B":"The enthalpy change forming one mole of gas from any liquid under standard conditions in a typical experiment","C":"No bonds were broken during the reaction.","D":"More energy was released making new bonds than was needed to break the original bonds."},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.4"},{"id":"CHEM-11","stem":"For the equilibrium N2(g) + 3H2(g) ⇌ 2NH3(g) (forward reaction exothermic), what does INCREASING the temperature do to the yield of ammonia?","options":{"A":"The enthalpy change forming one mole of gas from any liquid under standard conditions in a typical experiment","B":"No effect — temperature does not shift equilibria.","C":"Decreases yield — Le Chatelier's principle: the system shifts in the endothermic (reverse)","D":"Doubles the yield for every 10°C rise."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCHEM-EQUILIBRIA","scaffold":"Le Chatelier: a system at equilibrium shifts to oppose an imposed change. For an exothermic forward reaction, raising T favours the endothermic reverse → less product, lower Kc. Raising pressure favours the side with fewer gas moles (here, products, 2 vs 4). The Haber compromise: moderate T (~450°C) balances yield (favoured by low T) against rate (favoured by high T). Key distinction: a catalyst speeds up equilibrium but does NOT change the position or yield.","reforge":{"stem":"Adding a catalyst to the Haber process equilibrium does what to the yield of ammonia?","options":{"A":"Leaves the yield unchanged, reaching it faster","B":"Decreases the yield by favouring the reverse reaction.","C":"Increases the yield by shifting equilibrium right.","D":"Removes the need for high pressure."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.6"},{"id":"CHEM-12","stem":"A student writes the electron configuration of a Ca2+ ion as 1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 4s2. What is the error?","options":{"A":"Calcium cannot form a 2+ ion.","B":"The 3p subshell should hold 10 electrons.","C":"Chlorine has a relative atomic mass of 35.5 because it has 35.5 neutrons.","D":"The 4s2 should be removed: Ca2+ has lost its two outer 4s electrons"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ATOMIC-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Ca (Z=20): 1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 4s2. Forming Ca2+ removes the two highest-energy electrons — the 4s electrons — giving the argon configuration. Key rule: 4s FILLS before 3d but EMPTIES first when ionising. For transition metals: Fe is [Ar]3d6 4s2, but Fe2+ is [Ar]3d6 (4s lost first), Fe3+ is [Ar]3d5. Common error: removing 3d or 3p electrons instead of the outer 4s. The stable half-filled 3d5 explains Fe3+'s stability.","reforge":{"stem":"Iron is [Ar]3d6 4s2. What is the electron configuration of Fe3+?","options":{"A":"[Ar]3d3 4s2 — three 3d electrons removed.","B":"[Ar]3d5 — the two 4s electrons are removed first, then one 3d, leaving a stable half-filled 3d5.","C":"The enthalpy change forming one mole of gas from any liquid under standard conditions in a typical experiment","D":"[Ar]3d4 4s1."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.1"},{"id":"CHEM-13","stem":"In terms of collision theory, why does increasing concentration increase the rate of reaction?","options":{"A":"More particles per unit volume means more frequent collisions","B":"It lowers the activation energy so fewer collisions are needed to react.","C":"It increases the average kinetic energy of the particles, raising the activation energy.","D":"It changes the reaction mechanism from endothermic to exothermic."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCHEM-RATES","scaffold":"Rate depends on the frequency of SUCCESSFUL collisions (correct orientation AND energy ≥ activation energy). Concentration/pressure: more particles per volume → more frequent collisions. Temperature: increases both frequency AND (crucially) the proportion of particles with E ≥ Ea — the dominant effect. Catalyst: provides an alternative pathway with LOWER Ea, so a greater proportion of collisions succeed. Only temperature and catalysts change the proportion of successful collisions; concentration changes only frequency.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does a small temperature rise increase rate far more than the same proportional rise in concentration?","options":{"A":"Concentration has no effect on rate at all.","B":"Temperature lowers the activation energy directly.","C":"More particles exceed the activation energy","D":"Higher temperature makes all collisions successful."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.5"},{"id":"CHEM-14","stem":"A student describes a dative (coordinate) covalent bond as 'weaker than a normal covalent bond because only one atom provides the electrons'. Why is this wrong?","options":{"A":"Dative bonds are ionic in nature — the electron donor becomes positively charged.","B":"Once formed, a dative bond is identical to any other covalent bond","C":"Dative bonds share three electrons rather than two, making them stronger.","D":"Only metal atoms can act as electron donors in dative bonding."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCHEM-BONDING-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"A dative/coordinate bond differs only in ORIGIN: both shared electrons come from one atom (the donor, with a lone pair) rather than one from each. Once formed it is indistinguishable from and equal in strength to a normal covalent bond. Examples: NH4+ (N donates to H+), H3O+, and ligand-metal bonds in complex ions. Shown by an arrow → from donor to acceptor. Common error: treating the bond as weaker or as a different bond type after formation.","reforge":{"stem":"In the ammonium ion NH4+, all four N–H bonds are found to be identical in length and strength. Why?","options":{"A":"The dative bond is longer than the other three.","B":"The extra proton makes one bond ionic.","C":"Nitrogen only bonds to three hydrogens; the fourth is separate.","D":"All four N–H bonds become equivalent"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.3"},{"id":"CHEM-15","stem":"25.0 cm3 of 0.100 mol dm⁻³ NaOH is neutralised by HCl. How many moles of NaOH are present?","options":{"A":"0.025 mol — 0.100 × 25 ÷ 100.","B":"2.5 mol — 0.100 × 25.","C":"0.0025 mol — moles = concentration × volume in dm3 = 0.100 × 0.0250.","D":"Empirical formula always has the same subscript numbers as the molecular formula."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCHEM-MOLES-FORMULAE","scaffold":"n = c × V where V MUST be in dm3 (litres): 25.0 cm3 = 0.0250 dm3. So n = 0.100 × 0.0250 = 0.00250 mol. The single most common titration error is forgetting to convert cm3 to dm3 (divide by 1000), giving an answer 1000× too large. In titration calculations: (1) moles of known, (2) use the balanced equation ratio, (3) moles of unknown, (4) divide by its volume for concentration. Check units at every step.","reforge":{"stem":"The 0.00250 mol of NaOH reacts with HCl in a 1:1 ratio and required 20.0 cm3 of HCl. What is the concentration of the HCl?","options":{"A":"0.125 mol dm⁻³ — n(HCl) = 0.00250, then c = n ÷ V = 0.00250 ÷ 0.0200.","B":"Increases yield — higher temperature always speeds up product formation.","C":"0.05 mol dm⁻³.","D":"2.50 mol dm⁻³."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.2"},{"id":"CHEM-16","stem":"Why does electronegativity INCREASE across a period (left to right)?","options":{"A":"Atoms get larger across a period, attracting bonding electrons more strongly.","B":"The number of electron shells increases across a period, reducing shielding.","C":"Metallic character increases across a period, making atoms more electronegative.","D":"Nuclear charge rises across a period while shielding stays similar"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ATOMIC-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Electronegativity trends depend on: nuclear charge, atomic radius, and shielding. Across a period: proton number rises, shielding roughly constant (same shell), radius decreases → stronger attraction for bonding pair → electronegativity increases. Down a group: more shells, greater shielding, larger radius → weaker attraction → electronegativity decreases. Same three factors explain ionisation energy and atomic radius trends. Common error: claiming atoms get bigger across a period (they get smaller).","reforge":{"stem":"Why does electronegativity DECREASE down a group?","options":{"A":"Nuclear charge decreases down a group.","B":"More shells and shielding outweigh the charge","C":"The number of outer electrons decreases.","D":"Elements become more non-metallic down a group."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.1"},{"id":"CHEM-E2-01","spec":"CHEM-2","stem":"An exothermic reaction has:","options":{"A":"a negative enthalpy change","B":"a positive activation energy only","C":"no energy transfer","D":"a negative temperature in every case"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ENERGETICS","scaffold":"Products have lower enthalpy than reactants for an exothermic reaction.","reforge":{"stem":"A reaction releases 120 kJ of energy to the surroundings as it proceeds. What does this tell you about its enthalpy change?","options":{"A":"It is exothermic, so the enthalpy change is negative","B":"It must have a positive activation energy alone under standard conditions","C":"No energy has been transferred at all","D":"The temperature must fall below zero every time"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.4"},{"id":"CHEM-E2-02","spec":"CHEM-2","stem":"A catalyst increases rate by:","options":{"A":"increasing the enthalpy change","B":"providing an alternative route with lower activation energy","C":"moving the equilibrium position","D":"It lowers the activation energy so fewer collisions are needed to react."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCHEM-RATES","scaffold":"A catalyst speeds both forward and reverse reactions without changing equilibrium position.","reforge":{"stem":"Adding a catalyst speeds up a reaction without being used up itself. How does it achieve this?","options":{"A":"By increasing the overall enthalpy change of the reaction under standard conditions","B":"By providing an alternative reaction pathway with lower activation energy","C":"By shifting the position of equilibrium","D":"By permanently raising the temperature of the mixture"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.5"},{"id":"CHEM-E2-03","spec":"CHEM-2","stem":"At dynamic equilibrium:","options":{"A":"Using only the initial concentrations before reaction under standard conditions","B":"all reactions have stopped","C":"forward and reverse rates are equal in a closed system","D":"only products remain"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCHEM-EQUILIBRIA","scaffold":"Concentrations remain constant because the opposing rates are equal.","reforge":{"stem":"In a sealed flask, a reversible reaction reaches a point where the concentrations of reactants and products stop changing. What is actually happening at this point?","options":{"A":"Reactant and product concentrations happen to be equal under standard conditions","B":"Every reaction in the flask has stopped completely","C":"The forward and reverse reaction rates have become equal","D":"Only products remain in the flask"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.6"},{"id":"CHEM-E2-04","spec":"CHEM-2","stem":"Increasing pressure favours the equilibrium side with:","options":{"A":"more solid particles","B":"more total atoms in solution","C":"the larger activation energy","D":"fewer moles of gas"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCHEM-EQUILIBRIA","scaffold":"Le Chatelier's principle applies to gaseous mole numbers when pressure changes.","reforge":{"stem":"A gaseous equilibrium is subjected to a rise in pressure. Which side of the equilibrium does this favour?","options":{"A":"The side with more solid particles","B":"The side with more total atoms in solution","C":"The side with the larger activation energy","D":"The side with fewer moles of gas"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.6"},{"id":"CHEM-E2-05","spec":"CHEM-2","stem":"The oxidation state of oxygen in most oxides is:","options":{"A":"−2","B":"+2","C":"0","D":"−1"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ACIDS-REDOX","scaffold":"Exceptions include peroxides and compounds with fluorine.","reforge":{"stem":"What oxidation state does oxygen almost always have when it is combined in an oxide?","options":{"A":"−2, since oxygen gains two electrons","B":"+2, behaving like a typical metal ion","C":"0, exactly as in its uncombined elemental form","D":"−1, as it behaves specifically inside a peroxide"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.7"},{"id":"CHEM-E2-06","spec":"CHEM-2","stem":"A reducing agent is a species that:","options":{"A":"−2, the same as the overall charge.","B":"loses electrons and is oxidised","C":"accepts protons only","D":"never changes oxidation state"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ACIDS-REDOX","scaffold":"Remember OIL RIG: oxidation is loss, reduction is gain of electrons.","reforge":{"stem":"In a redox reaction, one species loses electrons and is itself oxidised. What is this species called?","options":{"A":"A species that gains electrons and is reduced","B":"A reducing agent","C":"A species that only accepts protons","D":"A species whose oxidation state never changes"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.7"},{"id":"CHEM-E2-07","spec":"CHEM-2","stem":"The standard electrode potential of a half-cell is measured relative to:","options":{"A":"a saturated salt bridge only","B":"pure oxygen at any pressure","C":"the standard hydrogen electrode","D":"a carbon electrode with no reference"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ACIDS-REDOX","scaffold":"The standard hydrogen electrode is assigned zero volts under standard conditions.","reforge":{"stem":"Against which reference electrode is the standard electrode potential of a half-cell conventionally measured?","options":{"A":"A saturated salt bridge alone","B":"Pure oxygen gas at any pressure","C":"The standard hydrogen electrode","D":"A carbon electrode with no defined reference"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.11"},{"id":"CHEM-E2-08","spec":"CHEM-2","stem":"A strong acid is almost completely:","options":{"A":"undissociated in water","B":"+4, matching the number of oxygens.","C":"converted into a base","D":"dissociated in aqueous solution"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ACIDS-REDOX","scaffold":"Strong acids have a high proportion of dissociated molecules in water.","reforge":{"stem":"A solution of hydrochloric acid is found to have ionised almost completely into H+ and Cl- ions. What does this indicate about the acid?","options":{"A":"It is a strong acid, almost entirely undissociated in solution","B":"It has already been neutralised before dissolving","C":"It has been converted into a base","D":"It is a strong acid, almost fully dissociated in solution"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.12"},{"id":"CHEM-E2-09","spec":"CHEM-2","stem":"The pH of a solution is defined using:","options":{"A":"the negative logarithm of hydrogen ion concentration","B":"It is a strong acid, almost entirely undissociated in solution","C":"mass divided by volume","D":"the number of salt ions only"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ACIDS-REDOX","scaffold":"pH = −log10[H+], with concentration in mol dm−3.","reforge":{"stem":"How is the pH of a solution formally defined?","options":{"A":"As the negative logarithm of the hydrogen ion concentration","B":"As the positive logarithm of the hydroxide ion concentration","C":"As mass divided by volume","D":"As the number of salt ions present"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.12"},{"id":"CHEM-E2-10","spec":"CHEM-2","stem":"The rate equation expresses rate in terms of:","options":{"A":"It changes the reaction mechanism from endothermic to exothermic.","B":"concentrations raised to experimentally determined orders","C":"the equilibrium constant alone","D":"the melting point"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCHEM-RATES","scaffold":"Orders must be found experimentally and need not match stoichiometric coefficients.","reforge":{"stem":"A rate equation is determined experimentally rather than read directly from the balanced equation. What does it express rate in terms of?","options":{"A":"Only the coefficients from the balanced equation under standard conditions","B":"Concentrations raised to experimentally determined orders","C":"The equilibrium constant alone","D":"The melting point of the reactants"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.9"},{"id":"CHEM-E2-11","spec":"CHEM-2","stem":"The unit of the rate constant depends on:","options":{"A":"the colour of the reactants","B":"Concentration has no effect on rate at all.","C":"the overall order of the reaction","D":"the catalyst's name"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCHEM-RATES","scaffold":"The units change so the rate equation gives concentration per unit time.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can the unit of a rate constant differ between two different reactions?","options":{"A":"Because it depends on the colour of the reactants","B":"Because it depends only on the shape of the reaction vessel","C":"Because it depends on the overall order of the reaction","D":"Because it depends on the name given to the catalyst"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.9"},{"id":"CHEM-E2-12","spec":"CHEM-2","stem":"Kp is calculated using:","options":{"A":"masses of all solids only","B":"Reactant and product concentrations happen to be equal under standard conditions","C":"the activation energy","D":"equilibrium partial pressures raised to their stoichiometric powers"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCHEM-EQUILIBRIA","scaffold":"Pure solids and liquids are omitted from the Kp expression.","reforge":{"stem":"How is Kp calculated for a gaseous equilibrium?","options":{"A":"Using the masses of all solids present only","B":"Using only the initial concentrations before reaction under standard conditions","C":"Using the activation energy of the forward reaction","D":"Using equilibrium partial pressures raised to their stoichiometric powers"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.10"},{"id":"CHEM-E2-13","spec":"CHEM-2","stem":"A buffer solution resists changes in:","options":{"A":"pH when small amounts of acid or alkali are added","B":"As the positive logarithm of the hydroxide ion concentration","C":"pressure in a gas","D":"mass during evaporation"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ACIDS-REDOX","scaffold":"A weak acid and its conjugate base can remove added hydrogen or hydroxide ions.","reforge":{"stem":"A solution's pH barely changes when a small amount of acid or alkali is added to it. What kind of solution is this?","options":{"A":"A buffer solution, which resists changes in pH","B":"A solution that resists changes in temperature when heated","C":"A solution that resists changes in gas pressure","D":"A solution that resists changes in mass during evaporation"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.12"},{"id":"CHEM-E2-14","spec":"CHEM-2","stem":"The enthalpy change of formation is for forming:","options":{"A":"one atom from an ion","B":"one mole of a compound from its elements in standard states","C":"More energy was needed to break bonds than was released making them.","D":"one bond in isolation"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ENERGETICS","scaffold":"Standard enthalpy definitions specify the amount and standard states.","reforge":{"stem":"What does the standard enthalpy change of formation of a compound specifically measure?","options":{"A":"The enthalpy change forming one atom from an ion","B":"The enthalpy change forming one mole of the compound from its elements in standard states","C":"The enthalpy change forming one mole of gas from any liquid under standard conditions in a typical experiment","D":"The enthalpy change forming one bond in isolation"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.4"},{"id":"CHEM-E2-15","spec":"CHEM-2","stem":"A feasibility calculation using Gibbs free energy considers:","options":{"A":"only activation energy","B":"only concentration","C":"enthalpy, entropy and temperature","D":"The products have more energy than the reactants."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ENERGETICS","scaffold":"A negative ΔG indicates feasibility under the stated conditions, not necessarily a fast reaction.","reforge":{"stem":"Which three quantities are combined in a Gibbs free energy calculation to assess whether a reaction is feasible?","options":{"A":"Only the activation energy","B":"Only the concentration of reactants","C":"Enthalpy, entropy and temperature","D":"Only the atomic number of the elements involved"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.8"},{"id":"CHEM-E2-16","spec":"CHEM-2","stem":"The oxidation number of an uncombined element is:","options":{"A":"one","B":"equal to its proton number","C":"always negative","D":"zero"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ACIDS-REDOX","scaffold":"Oxidation numbers are assigned relative to electron ownership rules.","reforge":{"stem":"What oxidation number is assigned to an element that has not combined with anything else?","options":{"A":"One","B":"A value equal to its proton number","C":"Always a negative value","D":"Zero"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.7"},{"id":"CHEM-E2-17","spec":"CHEM-2","stem":"A titration endpoint is usually detected by:","options":{"A":"a suitable indicator colour change or instrument signal","B":"the solution becoming completely colourless in every case","C":"the burette reaching zero","D":"the temperature falling to zero"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ACIDS-REDOX","scaffold":"The endpoint should be close to the equivalence point and chosen for the reaction.","reforge":{"stem":"How is the endpoint of a titration usually identified in practice?","options":{"A":"By a suitable indicator colour change or an instrument signal","B":"By the solution becoming completely colourless in every case under standard conditions","C":"By the burette reading returning to exactly zero","D":"By the temperature of the mixture falling to zero"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.2"},{"id":"CHEM-E2-18","spec":"CHEM-2","stem":"A redox disproportionation reaction involves:","options":{"A":"A strong acid and a strong base in equal amounts.","B":"the same species being both oxidised and reduced","C":"two catalysts reacting","D":"no change in oxidation state"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ACIDS-REDOX","scaffold":"One oxidation state splits into higher and lower oxidation states.","reforge":{"stem":"In some redox reactions, a single species is simultaneously oxidised and reduced. What is this type of reaction called?","options":{"A":"A simple acid-base neutralisation","B":"A disproportionation reaction","C":"A reaction between two separate catalysts","D":"A reaction with no change in oxidation state"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.7"},{"id":"CHEM-E4-01","spec":"CHEM-2","stem":"Down Group 2, the first ionisation energy generally:","options":{"A":"stays constant for every element","B":"Relative atomic mass equals the number of electrons in the neutral atom.","C":"decreases as atomic radius and shielding increase","D":"becomes zero in aqueous solution"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ATOMIC-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"The outer electron is farther from the nucleus and more shielded down the group.","reforge":{"stem":"Moving down Group 2 of the periodic table, what generally happens to the first ionisation energy?","options":{"A":"It stays exactly constant for every element","B":"It increases, because the atoms gain extra electrons","C":"It decreases, as atomic radius and shielding both increase","D":"It becomes zero once the element is in aqueous solution under standard conditions"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.2"},{"id":"CHEM-E4-02","spec":"CHEM-2","stem":"Aqueous barium ions are commonly identified by adding sulfate ions, which produce:","options":{"A":"a silver mirror","B":"React chemically with the burette glass.","C":"a green solution","D":"a white precipitate"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCHEM-INORGANIC-TESTS","scaffold":"Barium sulfate is insoluble and forms a white precipitate.","reforge":{"stem":"A student adds sulfate ions to a solution suspected of containing barium ions. What observation confirms their presence?","options":{"A":"A silver mirror forms on the test tube","B":"No visible change occurs","C":"The solution turns green","D":"A white precipitate forms"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.6"},{"id":"CHEM-E4-03","spec":"CHEM-2","stem":"Which observation is consistent with chlorine being more reactive than bromide ions?","options":{"A":"Chlorine displaces bromine from bromide solution","B":"Both halogens form identical products in water","C":"Bromide displaces chlorine from chloride solution","D":"Chlorine cannot gain electrons"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCHEM-INORGANIC-TESTS","scaffold":"A more reactive halogen oxidises the ions of a less reactive halogen.","reforge":{"stem":"Chlorine water is added to a solution of potassium bromide. What observation shows that chlorine is more reactive than bromide ions?","options":{"A":"Chlorine displaces bromine from the bromide solution","B":"Bromide displaces chlorine from a chloride solution under standard conditions","C":"Both halogens form identical products in water","D":"Chlorine is shown to be unable to gain electrons"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.3"},{"id":"CHEM-E4-04","spec":"CHEM-2","stem":"A transition-metal complex is coloured mainly because it:","options":{"A":"contains no delocalised electrons","B":"absorbs particular frequencies during electronic transitions","C":"Bromide displaces chlorine from a chloride solution under standard conditions","D":"always forms a white precipitate"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCHEM-INORGANIC-TESTS","scaffold":"The ligand field splits d-orbital energies, allowing selected wavelengths to be absorbed.","reforge":{"stem":"A solution containing a transition-metal complex appears strongly coloured. What is the main reason for this?","options":{"A":"It contains no delocalised electrons at all","B":"It absorbs particular frequencies of light during electronic transitions","C":"It simply has a higher boiling point than water under standard conditions","D":"It always forms a white precipitate"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.5"},{"id":"CHEM-E4B-01","spec":"CHEM-2","stem":"Which trend is expected across Period 3?","options":{"A":"It simply has a higher boiling point than water under standard conditions","B":"all elements become metals","C":"the oxides change from mainly ionic to mainly covalent","D":"every oxide is neutral"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCHEM-INORGANIC-TESTS","scaffold":"Increasing nuclear charge and changing bonding account for the trend in oxide character.","reforge":{"stem":"Moving across Period 3 from sodium to chlorine, what trend is expected in the nature of the oxides formed?","options":{"A":"Atomic radius increases steadily across the period under standard conditions","B":"Every element in the period becomes a metal","C":"The oxides change from mainly ionic to mainly covalent","D":"Every oxide formed is chemically neutral"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.4"},{"id":"CHEM-E4B-02","spec":"CHEM-2","stem":"When magnesium reacts with steam, the main products are:","options":{"A":"magnesium hydroxide and chlorine","B":"magnesium nitrate and water","C":"magnesium carbonate and oxygen","D":"magnesium oxide and hydrogen"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCHEM-INORGANIC-TESTS","scaffold":"Steam produces magnesium oxide and hydrogen, unlike cold water.","reforge":{"stem":"Magnesium ribbon is heated in steam. What are the main products of this reaction?","options":{"A":"Magnesium hydroxide and chlorine gas","B":"Magnesium nitrate and water","C":"Magnesium carbonate and oxygen gas","D":"Magnesium oxide and hydrogen gas"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.2"},{"id":"CHEM-E4B-03","spec":"CHEM-2","stem":"A precipitate test for chloride ions uses acidified silver nitrate and gives:","options":{"A":"a white precipitate","B":"Prevent the indicator changing colour.","C":"a yellow flame","D":"a brown gas"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCHEM-INORGANIC-TESTS","scaffold":"Silver chloride is white; acidification removes interfering carbonate ions.","reforge":{"stem":"A student adds acidified silver nitrate solution to a sample suspected of containing chloride ions. What result confirms this?","options":{"A":"A white precipitate forms","B":"The solution turns blue","C":"A yellow flame is produced","D":"A brown gas is released"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.6"},{"id":"CHEM-E4B-04","spec":"CHEM-2","stem":"The oxidation state of a transition metal can vary because:","options":{"A":"Atomic radius increases steadily across the period under standard conditions","B":"both 4s and 3d electrons may be involved in bonding","C":"their nuclei contain variable protons","D":"all their compounds are molecular"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCHEM-INORGANIC-TESTS","scaffold":"The similar energies of 4s and 3d electrons allow several oxidation states.","reforge":{"stem":"Transition metals can form ions with more than one oxidation state. What allows this variability?","options":{"A":"Transition metals have no outer electrons at all","B":"Both the 4s and 3d electrons may be involved in bonding","C":"Their nuclei contain a variable number of protons under standard conditions","D":"All of their compounds are purely molecular"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.5"},{"id":"CHEM-N2-01","stem":"Increasing the temperature increases the rate of reaction mainly because:","options":{"A":"Activation energy is lowered by heating.","B":"The particles become physically larger.","C":"More particles exceed the energy","D":"The enthalpy change becomes more negative."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCHEM-RATES","scaffold":"Raising temperature increases the mean kinetic energy, so a much larger fraction of collisions exceeds the activation energy — the dominant effect — and collisions also become more frequent. On a Maxwell–Boltzmann distribution the curve flattens and shifts right, with the area beyond Ea increasing sharply. Temperature does not change Ea itself; only a catalyst does that, by providing an alternative pathway.","reforge":{"stem":"On a Maxwell–Boltzmann distribution, raising the temperature causes the peak to:","options":{"A":"Lower and shift to higher energy","B":"Stay in exactly the same position.","C":"Rise and shift towards lower energy.","D":"Disappear entirely from the graph."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.5"},{"id":"CHEM-N2-02","stem":"A catalyst increases the rate of reaction by:","options":{"A":"Increasing the average energy of the particles.","B":"Shifting the equilibrium towards the products.","C":"Raising the frequency of all collisions.","D":"Providing a route with lower activation energy"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCHEM-RATES","scaffold":"A catalyst offers an alternative pathway of lower activation energy, so a greater proportion of collisions succeed. It is not consumed overall, does not change ΔH, and does not shift the position of equilibrium — it speeds the forward and reverse reactions equally, so equilibrium is simply reached sooner. Heterogeneous catalysts are in a different phase and work by adsorption at active sites; homogeneous catalysts share the phase.","reforge":{"stem":"Adding a catalyst to a reversible reaction at equilibrium will:","options":{"A":"Shift the position towards the products.","B":"Have no effect on the position","C":"Reduce the yield of the reaction.","D":"Make the reaction more exothermic."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.5"},{"id":"CHEM-N2-03","stem":"Increasing the concentration of a solution increases reaction rate because there are:","options":{"A":"More particles per volume, more collisions","B":"Particles with higher individual kinetic energy.","C":"Fewer successful collisions per second.","D":"Weaker bonds within each reactant molecule."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCHEM-RATES","scaffold":"Collision theory: a reaction occurs when particles collide with at least the activation energy and in the correct orientation. Raising concentration or the pressure of a gas packs more particles into the same volume, so collisions are more frequent, though the proportion that succeed is unchanged. Increasing the surface area of a solid exposes more particles to collision. Only temperature and catalysts change the proportion of successful collisions.","reforge":{"stem":"Powdering a solid reactant increases the rate because it increases the:","options":{"A":"Activation energy of the reaction.","B":"Average kinetic energy of particles.","C":"Surface area exposed to collisions","D":"Concentration of the solid."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.5"},{"id":"CHEM-N2-04","stem":"For a reaction that is first order with respect to A, doubling [A] will:","options":{"A":"Leave the rate unchanged.","B":"Double the rate","C":"Quadruple the rate.","D":"Halve the rate."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCHEM-RATES","scaffold":"Order shows how rate depends on concentration: zero order gives no change when concentration is doubled, first order doubles the rate, second order quadruples it. Orders are found experimentally, not from the balanced equation. The rate equation is rate = k[A]^m[B]^n, and the overall order is m + n. Units of k depend on the overall order — s⁻¹ for first order, mol⁻¹ dm³ s⁻¹ for second.","reforge":{"stem":"Doubling [B] leaves the rate unchanged. The reaction is:","options":{"A":"First order with respect to B.","B":"Second order with respect to B.","C":"Impossible to assign an order.","D":"Zero order with respect to B"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.9"},{"id":"CHEM-N2-05","stem":"The rate-determining step of a reaction mechanism is the:","options":{"A":"Fastest step, which finishes first.","B":"Step producing the most product.","C":"Slowest step, limiting the rate","D":"Final step of the whole mechanism."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCHEM-RATES","scaffold":"In a multi-step mechanism the slowest step limits the overall rate, so only species involved in that step (and any preceding fast equilibrium) appear in the rate equation. This is why the rate equation can be used as evidence for a proposed mechanism: the orders show how many of each species take part up to and including the rate-determining step, and species appearing only afterwards do not feature.","reforge":{"stem":"A species appears in the balanced equation but not in the rate equation. This suggests it:","options":{"A":"Is not in the rate-determining step","B":"Must be acting as a catalyst.","C":"Is present in very low concentration.","D":"Has been measured incorrectly."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.9"},{"id":"CHEM-N2-06","stem":"For an exothermic forward reaction at equilibrium, raising the temperature will:","options":{"A":"Shift the equilibrium towards the products.","B":"Stop the reverse reaction entirely.","C":"Leave the position of equilibrium unchanged.","D":"Shift the equilibrium towards the reactants"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCHEM-EQUILIBRIA","scaffold":"Le Chatelier: a system at equilibrium shifts to oppose an imposed change. Raising temperature favours the endothermic direction, which for an exothermic forward reaction means shifting back towards reactants and lowering yield — though rate increases, which is the compromise behind industrial conditions. Raising pressure favours the side with fewer gas moles. Concentration changes shift away from whatever was added.","reforge":{"stem":"In N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃, increasing the pressure shifts the equilibrium:","options":{"A":"Towards the reactants, which have fewer moles.","B":"Towards products, with fewer gas moles","C":"In neither direction, since moles are equal.","D":"Towards whichever side is exothermic."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.6"},{"id":"CHEM-N2-07","stem":"The Haber process uses a compromise temperature of about 450 °C because a lower temperature would give:","options":{"A":"A higher yield but too slow a rate","B":"A lower yield and a slower rate.","C":"A higher yield and a faster rate.","D":"No change in either yield or rate."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCHEM-EQUILIBRIA","scaffold":"Ammonia synthesis is exothermic, so a low temperature favours yield but makes the rate impractically slow; around 450 °C with an iron catalyst is the compromise. High pressure (about 200 atm) favours the product side, which has fewer gas moles, but very high pressures raise plant and safety costs. Removing ammonia by condensation and recycling unreacted gases raises the overall conversion beyond the single-pass equilibrium yield.","reforge":{"stem":"Ammonia is removed from the Haber process mixture by cooling in order to:","options":{"A":"Reduce the pressure inside the reactor.","B":"Increase the activation energy of the reaction.","C":"Shift the equilibrium further towards products","D":"Prevent the iron catalyst from working."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.6"},{"id":"CHEM-N2-08","stem":"For the equilibrium A(g) + B(g) ⇌ C(g), the expression for Kc is:","options":{"A":"[A][B] ÷ [C]","B":"[C] ÷ ([A][B])","C":"[A] + [B] − [C].","D":"[C] × [A] × [B]."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCHEM-EQUILIBRIA","scaffold":"Kc is products over reactants, each concentration raised to the power of its balancing number. Kc changes only with temperature: adding a catalyst or altering pressure or concentration shifts the position of equilibrium but leaves Kc unchanged. A large Kc means the equilibrium lies well to the right. Kp uses partial pressures for gases, calculated as mole fraction × total pressure, and pure solids and liquids are omitted from both.","reforge":{"stem":"Which change alters the value of Kc for a given reaction?","options":{"A":"Adding a suitable catalyst.","B":"Adding more of a reactant.","C":"Increasing the total pressure.","D":"Changing the temperature"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.6"},{"id":"CHEM-N2-09","stem":"In a Kp expression, the partial pressure of a gas is calculated as:","options":{"A":"Total pressure divided by number of gases.","B":"Moles of gas multiplied by the gas constant.","C":"Mole fraction multiplied by total pressure","D":"Total pressure minus the vapour pressure."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCHEM-EQUILIBRIA","scaffold":"Partial pressure = mole fraction × total pressure, where mole fraction is moles of that gas ÷ total moles of gas. Kp is written as products over reactants using partial pressures raised to their balancing numbers, and its units depend on the total powers involved. Solids and pure liquids do not appear. Check that the mole fractions of all gases sum to 1 before substituting — a quick way to catch arithmetic slips.","reforge":{"stem":"A mixture contains 2 mol of X and 3 mol of Y at a total pressure of 500 kPa. The partial pressure of X is:","options":{"A":"200 kPa, from (2/5) × 500","B":"250 kPa, from half the total.","C":"1000 kPa, from 2 × 500.","D":"300 kPa, from (3/5) × 500."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.10"},{"id":"CHEM-N2-10","stem":"The pH of a solution is defined as:","options":{"A":"The concentration of hydrogen ions directly.","B":"The ratio of acid to base concentration.","C":"The logarithm of hydroxide concentration.","D":"The negative logarithm of [H⁺]"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ACIDS-REDOX","scaffold":"pH = −log₁₀[H⁺], so a fall of one pH unit means a tenfold rise in [H⁺]. For a strong monoprotic acid, [H⁺] equals the acid concentration. For a strong base, find [OH⁻], then use Kw = [H⁺][OH⁻] = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ at 298 K to get [H⁺]. For a weak acid, Ka = [H⁺]²/[HA] under the usual approximations. Kw varies with temperature, so neutral pH is only 7 at 298 K.","reforge":{"stem":"A solution of pH 3 compared with one of pH 5 has a hydrogen ion concentration that is:","options":{"A":"Two times greater.","B":"100 times greater","C":"Ten times smaller.","D":"Exactly the same."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.12"},{"id":"CHEM-N2-11","stem":"In a titration of a strong acid with a strong base, the pH at the equivalence point is:","options":{"A":"7, since the salt formed is neutral","B":"Above 9, because the base is strong.","C":"Below 4, because the acid is strong.","D":"Impossible to predict from the data."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ACIDS-REDOX","scaffold":"Equivalence pH depends on the salt formed: strong acid with strong base gives pH 7; weak acid with strong base gives above 7 (the anion hydrolyses); strong acid with weak base gives below 7. The indicator must change colour within the vertical section of the curve — phenolphthalein for weak acid/strong base, methyl orange for strong acid/weak base. A weak acid with a weak base has no sharp vertical section, so no indicator is suitable.","reforge":{"stem":"Phenolphthalein rather than methyl orange is used for a weak acid and strong base titration because its range:","options":{"A":"Covers the whole pH scale.","B":"Is below pH 4, where the curve is steep.","C":"Lies in the vertical section, above pH 7","D":"Is unaffected by the acid used."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.12"},{"id":"CHEM-N2-12","stem":"The ionic product of water, Kw, increases as temperature rises because the dissociation of water is:","options":{"A":"Exothermic, so heat pushes it backwards.","B":"Endothermic, so heat pushes it forwards","C":"Independent of the temperature applied.","D":"Catalysed by the water molecules present."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ACIDS-REDOX","scaffold":"Water dissociates endothermically, so raising the temperature shifts the equilibrium right, increasing both [H⁺] and [OH⁻] and hence Kw. Pure water stays neutral because the two remain equal, but its pH falls below 7 above 298 K — neutral does not mean pH 7 at all temperatures. Kw = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ only at 298 K, so any pH calculation involving a base must use the Kw value for the stated temperature.","reforge":{"stem":"At 50 °C the pH of pure water is below 7. The water is:","options":{"A":"Acidic, since pH is below 7.","B":"No longer able to dissociate.","C":"Alkaline, since Kw has increased.","D":"Still neutral: [H⁺] = [OH⁻]"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.12"},{"id":"CHEM-N2-13","stem":"Entropy is best described as a measure of the:","options":{"A":"Total energy stored within a substance.","B":"Rate at which a reaction proceeds.","C":"Disorder, or ways of arranging","D":"Strength of the bonds within a molecule."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ENERGETICS","scaffold":"Entropy rises from solid to liquid to gas, and when the number of gaseous moles increases or a solid dissolves. ΔS(system) = ΣS(products) − ΣS(reactants). Feasibility uses ΔG = ΔH − TΔS: a reaction is feasible when ΔG is negative or zero. An endothermic reaction can therefore be feasible if ΔS is sufficiently positive and the temperature high enough, which is why thermal decompositions proceed on heating.","reforge":{"stem":"An endothermic reaction becomes feasible at high temperature when:","options":{"A":"ΔS is positive, so TΔS exceeds ΔH","B":"ΔS is negative and ΔH is large.","C":"ΔG becomes increasingly positive.","D":"The activation energy falls to zero."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.8"},{"id":"CHEM-N2-14","stem":"A reaction is thermodynamically feasible when the value of ΔG is:","options":{"A":"Positive and large.","B":"Equal to the activation energy.","C":"Exactly equal to ΔH.","D":"Negative or zero"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ENERGETICS","scaffold":"ΔG = ΔH − TΔS, with ΔS in kJ K⁻¹ mol⁻¹ to match ΔH — converting from J is a frequent slip. Feasible means ΔG ≤ 0. Setting ΔG = 0 gives the minimum feasible temperature, T = ΔH ÷ ΔS. Note feasibility is not the same as observable reaction: a feasible reaction with a very high activation energy may not proceed at a measurable rate, which is why diamond does not turn into graphite.","reforge":{"stem":"A reaction has ΔG negative but does not appear to occur at room temperature. The most likely reason is:","options":{"A":"ΔS must have been calculated wrongly.","B":"A very high activation energy","C":"The reaction is endothermic.","D":"Entropy decreases during the reaction."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.8"},{"id":"CHEM-N2-15","stem":"In a Born–Haber cycle, lattice enthalpy of formation refers to the energy change when:","options":{"A":"One mole of lattice from gaseous ions","B":"One mole of a compound forms from its elements.","C":"Gaseous atoms each gain one electron.","D":"A solid is completely dissolved in water."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ENERGETICS","scaffold":"A Born–Haber cycle links enthalpy of formation with atomisation, ionisation energies, electron affinities and lattice enthalpy. Lattice formation enthalpy (gaseous ions to solid) is exothermic; lattice dissociation is the reverse and endothermic — check which sign a question expects. Lattice enthalpy becomes more exothermic with higher ionic charges and smaller ionic radii, since attraction between the ions is stronger.","reforge":{"stem":"Magnesium oxide has a more exothermic lattice enthalpy than sodium chloride because its ions are:","options":{"A":"Larger and more highly shielded.","B":"Held together by covalent bonds.","C":"More highly charged and smaller","D":"Present in a different crystal shape."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.8"},{"id":"CHEM-N2-16","stem":"A difference between theoretical and experimental lattice enthalpy suggests the compound has:","options":{"A":"Been measured with a faulty calorimeter.","B":"Some covalent character in its bonding","C":"No ionic bonding whatsoever.","D":"An unusually high melting point."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ENERGETICS","scaffold":"Theoretical values assume a purely ionic model of spherical ions. Where the experimental (Born–Haber) value is more exothermic, the bonding has covalent character: a small, highly charged cation polarises a large, polarisable anion, distorting its electron cloud. The effect grows with greater cation charge, smaller cation size and larger anion — which is why silver iodide deviates far more than sodium chloride.","reforge":{"stem":"Polarisation of an anion by a cation is greatest when the cation is:","options":{"A":"Large with a low charge.","B":"Identical in size to the anion.","C":"Neutral rather than charged.","D":"Small with a high charge"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.8"},{"id":"CHEM-N2-17","stem":"In an electrochemical cell, the electrode with the more negative standard electrode potential will:","options":{"A":"Be reduced, gaining electrons.","B":"Remain unchanged throughout.","C":"Be oxidised, losing electrons","D":"Act as the positive terminal."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ACIDS-REDOX","scaffold":"The more negative electrode is the better reducing agent, so it is oxidised and acts as the negative terminal; the more positive electrode is reduced. Cell EMF = E(more positive) − E(more negative), and a positive EMF means the reaction is feasible. Standard conditions are 298 K, 100 kPa and 1.0 mol dm⁻³ solutions, measured against a standard hydrogen electrode defined as 0.00 V.","reforge":{"stem":"Two half-cells have E values of −0.76 V and +0.34 V. The EMF of the cell is:","options":{"A":"1.10 V, from +0.34 − (−0.76)","B":"0.76 V, taking the larger magnitude.","C":"−0.42 V, adding the two values.","D":"0.34 V, using the positive value only."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.11"},{"id":"CHEM-N2-18","stem":"Standard electrode potentials are measured relative to which reference?","options":{"A":"A copper electrode in copper sulfate.","B":"A platinum electrode in pure water.","C":"A silver–silver chloride electrode.","D":"The standard hydrogen electrode"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ACIDS-REDOX","scaffold":"The standard hydrogen electrode — hydrogen gas at 100 kPa bubbled over platinised platinum in 1.0 mol dm⁻³ H⁺ at 298 K — is assigned 0.00 V, because only potential differences can be measured, never an absolute electrode potential. A high-resistance voltmeter is used so negligible current flows and the cell stays at equilibrium, and a salt bridge of saturated KNO₃ completes the circuit without introducing another potential.","reforge":{"stem":"A salt bridge is used in an electrochemical cell in order to:","options":{"A":"Supply electrons to the negative electrode.","B":"Complete the circuit and balance charge","C":"Prevent any current from flowing.","D":"Increase the cell's measured EMF."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.11"},{"id":"CHEM-N2-19","stem":"A reaction predicted to be feasible from electrode potentials may not occur because:","options":{"A":"Feasibility ignores the reaction rate","B":"The EMF value must have been negative.","C":"Electrode potentials apply only to metals.","D":"The reaction would violate Hess's law."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ACIDS-REDOX","scaffold":"Electrode potentials indicate thermodynamic feasibility only. A reaction with positive EMF may still be immeasurably slow if the activation energy is high. Predictions also assume standard conditions: changing concentration shifts the electrode potential, as Le Chatelier applied to the half-equation predicts, so a reaction infeasible under standard conditions may become feasible when concentrations differ substantially.","reforge":{"stem":"Increasing the concentration of Cu²⁺ in a Cu²⁺/Cu half-cell makes its electrode potential:","options":{"A":"More negative, opposing the change.","B":"Unchanged, since E values are fixed.","C":"More positive, favouring reduction","D":"Equal to zero at all concentrations."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.11"},{"id":"CHEM-N2-20","stem":"A hydrogen fuel cell differs from a conventional cell because it:","options":{"A":"Produces carbon dioxide as its only product.","B":"Is continuously supplied with fuel and oxygen","C":"Requires recharging from mains electricity.","D":"Stores all its reactants internally from the start."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ACIDS-REDOX","scaffold":"A fuel cell is fed continuously with hydrogen and oxygen, so it does not run down or need recharging, and produces only water at the point of use, with high efficiency since no combustion or turbine stage is involved. Drawbacks: hydrogen is difficult and energy-intensive to produce, store and transport, most is currently made from methane, and cells use expensive platinum catalysts — so the environmental case depends on how the hydrogen was made.","reforge":{"stem":"The environmental benefit of a hydrogen fuel cell depends most on:","options":{"A":"Whether it uses a salt bridge.","B":"The volume of water it produces.","C":"The temperature at which it operates.","D":"How the hydrogen was produced"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.11"},{"id":"CHEM-N2-21","stem":"Transition metals can form coloured compounds because their ions have:","options":{"A":"A full d subshell in every oxidation state.","B":"Only s electrons available for bonding.","C":"Partially filled d orbitals split in energy","D":"An unusually large atomic radius."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCHEM-BONDING-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"A transition metal forms at least one ion with a partially filled d subshell. Ligands split the d orbitals into two energy levels; electrons absorb visible light to jump the gap, and the transmitted complementary colour is what we see. The size of the gap, and so the colour, depends on the ligand, the oxidation state and the coordination number. Scandium and zinc are excluded: Sc³⁺ is d⁰ and Zn²⁺ is d¹⁰, so both are colourless.","reforge":{"stem":"Zinc is not classed as a transition metal because its common ion has a d subshell that is:","options":{"A":"Completely full","B":"Split into two energy levels.","C":"Completely empty of electrons.","D":"Partially filled with unpaired electrons."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.5"},{"id":"CHEM-N2-22","stem":"A ligand is best described as a species that:","options":{"A":"Catalyses the formation of a precipitate.","B":"Accepts electrons from a metal ion.","C":"Removes the charge from a complex ion.","D":"Donates a lone pair to a metal ion"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCHEM-BONDING-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"A ligand donates a lone pair to a central metal ion, forming a dative covalent bond. Monodentate ligands (H₂O, NH₃, Cl⁻) donate one pair; bidentate ligands such as ethanedioate donate two; multidentate ligands such as EDTA donate several. Coordination number is the number of dative bonds — commonly 6 (octahedral) or 4 (tetrahedral or square planar). Larger ligands such as Cl⁻ often give 4-coordinate complexes.","reforge":{"stem":"A complex ion with six water ligands around a central metal ion is:","options":{"A":"Tetrahedral, with 109.5° angles.","B":"Octahedral, with 90° angles","C":"Linear, with 180° angles.","D":"Square planar, with 90° angles."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.5"},{"id":"CHEM-N2-23","stem":"The chelate effect makes a complex with a bidentate ligand more stable mainly because the substitution:","options":{"A":"Increases the system entropy","B":"Releases far more energy as heat.","C":"Reduces the coordination number.","D":"Removes the charge on the complex."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCHEM-BONDING-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Replacing monodentate ligands with multidentate ones increases the number of free particles — one EDTA replacing six water molecules turns two species into seven — so entropy rises substantially while ΔH is close to zero, since similar numbers of dative bonds break and form. ΔG = ΔH − TΔS is therefore negative, driven almost entirely by the entropy term. This is why EDTA complexes are used to sequester metal ions.","reforge":{"stem":"In a chelate substitution, ΔH is close to zero because the number of dative bonds:","options":{"A":"Falls sharply during the reaction.","B":"Doubles as the ligand binds.","C":"Is roughly unchanged overall","D":"Depends only on temperature."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.5"},{"id":"CHEM-N2-24","stem":"Adding excess ammonia solution to copper(II) sulfate solution gives:","options":{"A":"A permanent pale blue precipitate.","B":"A deep blue solution forms","C":"A colourless solution with no change.","D":"A brown precipitate of copper metal."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCHEM-INORGANIC-TESTS","scaffold":"With a little ammonia, Cu²⁺ gives a pale blue precipitate of Cu(OH)₂ as ammonia acts as a base. With excess, ligand substitution replaces four water molecules to give the deep blue [Cu(NH₃)₄(H₂O)₂]²⁺. Comparable tests: Fe²⁺ gives a green precipitate darkening to brown in air, Fe³⁺ a red-brown precipitate, and Al³⁺ a white precipitate that redissolves in excess sodium hydroxide but not in excess ammonia.","reforge":{"stem":"A white precipitate forms with sodium hydroxide and redissolves in excess. The ion present is:","options":{"A":"Fe³⁺, giving a red-brown precipitate.","B":"Fe²⁺, giving a green precipitate.","C":"Cu²⁺, giving a blue precipitate.","D":"Al³⁺, which is amphoteric"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.5"},{"id":"CHEM-N2-25","stem":"Heterogeneous catalysts such as iron in the Haber process work by:","options":{"A":"Dissolving fully in the reaction mixture.","B":"Raising the activation energy of the reaction.","C":"Adsorbing reactants onto active sites","D":"Being consumed steadily as products form."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCHEM-RATES","scaffold":"A heterogeneous catalyst is in a different phase from the reactants. Molecules adsorb onto active sites, which weakens their bonds and holds them in favourable orientations; reaction follows and products desorb. Increasing surface area — using a support medium or fine mesh — raises activity for less metal. Catalysts are poisoned when impurities such as sulfur bind irreversibly to active sites, which is why feedstocks are purified first.","reforge":{"stem":"Sulfur impurities poison a heterogeneous catalyst because they:","options":{"A":"Bind irreversibly to the active sites","B":"Raise the temperature of the reactor.","C":"React with the desired product.","D":"Dissolve the catalyst in the gas stream."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.5"},{"id":"CHEM-N2-26","stem":"Which measurement would allow the rate of a reaction producing a gas to be followed continuously?","options":{"A":"The colour of the final solution.","B":"The total mass of catalyst added.","C":"The pH measured after completion.","D":"Volume of gas collected over time"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCHEM-RATES","scaffold":"Continuous methods track a property as the reaction proceeds: gas volume in a syringe, mass loss on a balance as gas escapes, colour change by colorimetry, or conductivity where ion numbers change. Initial rate is found from the gradient of the tangent at t = 0, since concentrations are known exactly then. Clock reactions instead measure the time to reach a fixed visible endpoint, giving a rate proportional to 1/t.","reforge":{"stem":"In a clock reaction, the rate is taken as proportional to:","options":{"A":"The total time multiplied by volume.","B":"1 ÷ time to reach the endpoint","C":"The mass of product formed.","D":"The temperature of the mixture."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.5"},{"id":"CHEM-N2-27","stem":"The Arrhenius equation shows that the rate constant k increases with temperature because the term:","options":{"A":"e^(−Ea/RT) increases as T increases","B":"Ea increases as temperature rises.","C":"R decreases at higher temperatures.","D":"A falls as the reaction proceeds."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCHEM-RATES","scaffold":"k = Ae^(−Ea/RT). Raising T makes the exponent less negative, so the exponential term and therefore k increase — a small temperature rise can change rate substantially. Taking logs gives ln k = ln A − Ea/RT, so plotting ln k against 1/T yields a straight line of gradient −Ea/R, from which activation energy is found. A is the pre-exponential factor, relating to collision frequency and orientation.","reforge":{"stem":"A plot of ln k against 1/T has a gradient equal to:","options":{"A":"Ea ÷ R, always positive.","B":"ln A, the intercept value.","C":"−Ea/R, giving Ea","D":"The order of the reaction."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.9"},{"id":"CHEM-N2-28","stem":"A dynamic equilibrium is characterised by:","options":{"A":"Both reactions stopping completely.","B":"Equal forward and reverse rates","C":"Equal concentrations of reactants and products.","D":"The complete conversion of reactants."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCHEM-EQUILIBRIA","scaffold":"At dynamic equilibrium both reactions continue at equal rates in a closed system, so macroscopic concentrations stay constant — but they are not necessarily equal, and neither reaction has stopped. Position depends on conditions; Kc depends only on temperature. A common misconception is that equilibrium means a 50:50 mixture, which is only true when Kc happens to equal 1 for that stoichiometry.","reforge":{"stem":"At equilibrium the concentrations of reactants and products are:","options":{"A":"Always exactly equal to each other.","B":"Both reduced to zero.","C":"Changing steadily over time.","D":"Constant, but not always equal"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.6"},{"id":"CHEM-N2-29","stem":"Sulfuric acid is described as a diprotic acid because each molecule can donate:","options":{"A":"One proton per molecule.","B":"Four protons per molecule.","C":"Two protons per molecule","D":"Two electrons per molecule."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ACIDS-REDOX","scaffold":"Monoprotic acids (HCl, HNO₃) donate one proton, diprotic (H₂SO₄) two, triprotic (H₃PO₄) three. In titration calculations the stoichiometric ratio matters: H₂SO₄ reacts with NaOH in a 1:2 ratio, so half as many moles of acid are needed for a given amount of alkali. Forgetting this ratio and assuming 1:1 is one of the commonest arithmetical errors in volumetric analysis.","reforge":{"stem":"How many moles of NaOH react completely with 0.05 mol of H₂SO₄?","options":{"A":"0.10 mol, in a 1:2 ratio","B":"0.05 mol, in a 1:1 ratio.","C":"0.025 mol, half the acid amount.","D":"0.20 mol, in a 1:4 ratio."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.12"},{"id":"CHEM-N2-30","stem":"Which change would increase the yield of products in an exothermic gas reaction that produces fewer moles of gas?","options":{"A":"Raising the temperature substantially.","B":"Adding a suitable catalyst.","C":"Removing some of the reactant gases.","D":"Lower temperature, higher pressure"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCHEM-EQUILIBRIA","scaffold":"For an exothermic reaction, lowering temperature shifts equilibrium towards products; where the product side has fewer gas moles, raising pressure does the same. Both raise yield but lower temperature reduces rate, hence industrial compromise conditions. A catalyst changes neither the position nor the yield. Removing product as it forms shifts the equilibrium forward and is often the most economical route to high conversion.","reforge":{"stem":"Continuously removing a product from an equilibrium mixture will:","options":{"A":"Shift the equilibrium towards the reactants.","B":"Shift the equilibrium towards the products","C":"Have no effect on the position.","D":"Increase the value of Kc."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.6"},{"id":"CHEM-N2-31","stem":"The oxidation number of chlorine changes from 0 to −1 in a reaction. Chlorine has been:","options":{"A":"Reduced, acting as an oxidising agent","B":"Oxidised, acting as a reducing agent.","C":"Neither oxidised nor reduced.","D":"Converted into a different element."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ACIDS-REDOX","scaffold":"A fall in oxidation number is reduction, and the species reduced is the oxidising agent — it oxidised something else. A rise is oxidation, and that species is the reducing agent. In disproportionation a single element is simultaneously oxidised and reduced, as when chlorine reacts with cold dilute sodium hydroxide going to both −1 and +1. Balance redox half-equations by adding H₂O, H⁺ and electrons in that order.","reforge":{"stem":"Chlorine reacts with cold dilute NaOH forming both Cl⁻ and ClO⁻. This is an example of:","options":{"A":"Neutralisation of an acid.","B":"A precipitation reaction.","C":"Disproportionation","D":"Thermal decomposition."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.7"},{"id":"CHEM-N2-32","stem":"Which of these would be the best conditions for a fast rate but a reduced equilibrium yield in an exothermic reaction?","options":{"A":"Low temperature and no catalyst.","B":"High temperature","C":"Low pressure with fewer product moles.","D":"Removing the catalyst entirely."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCHEM-EQUILIBRIA","scaffold":"Rate and yield often pull in opposite directions for exothermic reactions: high temperature speeds the reaction but shifts equilibrium back towards reactants, lowering yield. Industry therefore chooses a moderate compromise temperature with a catalyst to restore rate, plus pressure chosen from the mole balance, and recycles unreacted material. Explaining that compromise, rather than naming a single optimum, is what such questions are testing.","reforge":{"stem":"A catalyst is valuable in an industrial equilibrium process because it allows:","options":{"A":"A higher yield at the same temperature.","B":"The equilibrium position to shift right.","C":"Kc to be increased substantially.","D":"An acceptable rate at lower temperature"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.6"},{"id":"CHEM-N2-33","stem":"In the reaction of a metal carbonate with dilute acid, the gas evolved would:","options":{"A":"Give a squeaky pop with a lit splint.","B":"Relight a glowing splint.","C":"Turn limewater milky","D":"Turn damp red litmus paper blue."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCHEM-INORGANIC-TESTS","scaffold":"Carbonates react with dilute acid to give a salt, water and carbon dioxide, which turns limewater milky as insoluble calcium carbonate forms. Contrast: hydrogen from metal and acid gives a squeaky pop; oxygen relights a glowing splint; ammonia turns damp red litmus blue. Effervescence alone does not identify a gas, so the confirmatory test matters — several reactions fizz without producing carbon dioxide.","reforge":{"stem":"Limewater turns milky with carbon dioxide because the gas forms:","options":{"A":"Insoluble calcium carbonate","B":"Soluble calcium hydrogencarbonate.","C":"Calcium metal and oxygen.","D":"A precipitate of calcium oxide."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.12"},{"id":"CHEM-N2-34","stem":"A student needs to make 250 cm³ of a standard solution. The correct apparatus is a:","options":{"A":"Measuring cylinder of 250 cm³.","B":"Conical flask marked at 250 cm³.","C":"Burette filled to the 250 mark.","D":"Volumetric flask of 250 cm³"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCHEM-INORGANIC-TESTS","scaffold":"A volumetric flask is calibrated to contain one precise volume and is used for standard solutions: weigh the solute accurately, dissolve fully in a beaker, transfer with rinsings, then make up to the graduation mark with the bottom of the meniscus on the line and invert to mix. A measuring cylinder is far less precise, and a conical flask is not calibrated for accurate volume at all — it is a reaction vessel.","reforge":{"stem":"The beaker used to dissolve the solid is rinsed into the volumetric flask in order to:","options":{"A":"Cool the solution before making up.","B":"Transfer all of the solute","C":"Neutralise any acid on the glass.","D":"Remove any dissolved gases present."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.2"},{"id":"CHEM-N2-35","stem":"An increase in pressure has no effect on the position of an equilibrium when:","options":{"A":"The number of gas moles is equal on both sides","B":"A catalyst is present in the mixture.","C":"The reaction is exothermic in the forward direction.","D":"The temperature is held constant."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCHEM-EQUILIBRIA","scaffold":"Pressure changes shift equilibrium towards the side with fewer gas moles. If both sides have equal numbers of gas moles — as in H₂ + I₂ ⇌ 2HI — there is no shift, though rate still increases because collisions are more frequent. Count only gaseous species: solids and liquids are excluded. Adding an inert gas at constant volume changes total pressure but not partial pressures, so it has no effect either.","reforge":{"stem":"Adding argon to a gaseous equilibrium at constant volume has no effect because it leaves:","options":{"A":"The temperature of the system lower.","B":"The value of Kp increased.","C":"The partial pressures unchanged","D":"The activation energy unaltered."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.6"},{"id":"CHEM-N2-36","stem":"Which pair correctly matches an ion with its flame colour?","options":{"A":"Sodium — lilac flame.","B":"Calcium — brick red flame","C":"Copper(II) — brick red flame.","D":"Potassium — yellow flame."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCHEM-INORGANIC-TESTS","scaffold":"Flame colours: lithium crimson, sodium yellow, potassium lilac, calcium brick red, barium apple green, copper(II) blue-green. Heat energy promotes electrons to higher levels; as they fall back they emit light of a characteristic wavelength set by the energy gap. Use a nichrome wire cleaned in concentrated hydrochloric acid between tests, since sodium contamination is intense and masks weaker colours.","reforge":{"stem":"A flame test wire is cleaned with concentrated hydrochloric acid between tests mainly to:","options":{"A":"Prevent the wire from melting.","B":"Increase the temperature of the flame.","C":"Convert the sample into a chloride.","D":"Remove contamination masking colour"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.6"},{"id":"CHEM-N2-37","stem":"The first electron affinity of an element is the enthalpy change when:","options":{"A":"One mole of a compound forms from its elements.","B":"One mole of gaseous atoms each loses an electron.","C":"One mole of gaseous atoms each gains an electron","D":"One mole of solid forms from gaseous ions."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ENERGETICS","scaffold":"First electron affinity — one mole of gaseous atoms each gaining an electron to form 1− ions — is exothermic, since the nucleus attracts the added electron. Second electron affinity is endothermic, because an electron must be forced onto an already negative ion against repulsion. Both appear in Born–Haber cycles for oxides and sulfides, and getting the sign of the second value wrong is a frequent source of lost marks.","reforge":{"stem":"The second electron affinity of oxygen is endothermic because the electron is added to:","options":{"A":"A negative ion, against repulsion","B":"A neutral gaseous atom.","C":"A positively charged nucleus.","D":"A solid lattice rather than a gas."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.1"},{"id":"CHEM-N4-02","stem":"A reaction has ΔH = +50 kJ mol⁻¹ and ΔS = +100 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹. The minimum temperature for feasibility is:","options":{"A":"0.5 K, from 50 divided by 100.","B":"150 K, from 50 plus 100.","C":"5000 K, from 50 times 100.","D":"500 K, from 50,000 ÷ 100"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ENERGETICS","scaffold":"Feasibility requires ΔG ≤ 0, and ΔG = ΔH − TΔS, so the minimum temperature is T = ΔH ÷ ΔS. The units must match: ΔH in kJ mol⁻¹ must be converted to J mol⁻¹ (×1000), or ΔS converted to kJ K⁻¹ mol⁻¹ (÷1000). Here 50,000 ÷ 100 = 500 K. Failing to convert is the single commonest error, and produces answers wrong by a factor of a thousand.","reforge":{"stem":"In the equation ΔG = ΔH − TΔS, the most common error is failing to:","options":{"A":"Use the correct sign for ΔG.","B":"Convert ΔS into kJ","C":"Measure the temperature at all.","D":"Include the activation energy term."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.8"},{"id":"A1-PHASE7-CHEM2-01","stem":"In a Kp expression, the partial pressure of a gaseous product rises while reactant pressures stay constant. The value of Qp will:","options":{"A":"increase","B":"decrease to zero","C":"remain exactly one","D":"become negative"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCHEM-EQUILIBRIA","scaffold":"A product pressure appears in the numerator, so increasing it raises the reaction quotient.","reforge":{"stem":"In a Kp expression, the partial pressure of a gaseous product rises while the reactant pressures stay constant. What happens to the value of Qp?","options":{"A":"It decreases to zero","B":"It remains exactly one","C":"It increases","D":"It becomes negative"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.10"},{"id":"A1-PHASE7-CHEM2-02","stem":"An electrochemical cell has a positive Ecell under standard conditions. This indicates that the forward reaction is:","options":{"A":"at equilibrium with no net change","B":"thermodynamically feasible","C":"certain to have a negative rate","D":"unable to transfer electrons"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ACIDS-REDOX","scaffold":"A positive standard cell potential corresponds to a favourable overall redox reaction.","reforge":{"stem":"An electrochemical cell is found to have a positive Ecell under standard conditions. What does this indicate about the forward reaction?","options":{"A":"It is at equilibrium, with no net change occurring","B":"It is certain to proceed at a negative rate","C":"It is unable to transfer any electrons","D":"It is thermodynamically feasible"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.11"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-01","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ENERGETICS","stem":"In a calorimetry experiment, 50.0 g of water rises in temperature by 6.0 K. Taking c = 4.18 J g⁻¹ K⁻¹, how much heat energy did the water absorb?","options":{"A":"12.54 kJ","B":"125.4 J","C":"1254 J","D":"300 J"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"Use q = mcΔT with m as the mass of water (or solution) being heated, not the mass of the reactants: q = 50.0 × 4.18 × 6.0 = 1254 J, which is 1.254 kJ. A change of 6.0 K is the same as a change of 6.0 °C, so no conversion is needed. To turn q into ΔH, divide by the moles of the limiting reactant and give the sign: negative if the temperature rose.","reforge":{"stem":"That 1254 J was released by 0.020 mol of the limiting reactant. What is ΔH for the reaction per mole of that reactant?","options":{"A":"−62.7 kJ mol⁻¹","B":"+62.7 kJ mol⁻¹","C":"−25.1 kJ mol⁻¹","D":"−6.27 kJ mol⁻¹"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.4"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-02","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ENERGETICS","stem":"Using mean bond enthalpies, the enthalpy change of a reaction is calculated as:","options":{"A":"sum of bonds formed minus sum of bonds broken","B":"sum of the product formation enthalpies only","C":"sum of bonds broken plus sum of bonds formed","D":"sum of bonds broken minus sum of bonds formed"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"Breaking bonds takes energy in (endothermic, positive); making bonds releases energy (exothermic, negative). So ΔH = Σ(bond enthalpies of bonds broken) − Σ(bond enthalpies of bonds formed). If more energy is released making the product bonds than is needed to break the reactant bonds, ΔH is negative. List every bond in each molecule, multiplied by the number of molecules in the equation.","reforge":{"stem":"In CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O, which bonds are broken?","options":{"A":"Two C=O bonds and four O–H bonds","B":"Four C–H bonds and two O=O bonds","C":"Four C–H bonds and four O–H bonds","D":"One C–H bond and one O=O bond"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.4"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-03","tag":"MC-GCHEM-RATES","stem":"On a Maxwell–Boltzmann distribution, the area under the curve represents:","options":{"A":"the total number of molecules in the sample","B":"the mean energy of the molecules in the sample","C":"the number of molecules with exactly zero energy","D":"the activation energy of the reaction"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"The x-axis is molecular energy and the y-axis is the number of molecules with that energy, so the area under the curve is the total number of molecules. That is why the area stays the same when the temperature changes: the curve flattens and its peak moves right, but no molecules are created. The curve starts at the origin (no molecule has zero energy) and never touches the x-axis at high energy.","reforge":{"stem":"When the temperature of a gas rises, why does the area under its Maxwell–Boltzmann curve stay the same?","options":{"A":"The activation energy falls by the same amount","B":"Fewer molecules now exceed the activation energy","C":"The number of molecules has not changed","D":"The curve shifts left so the two areas cancel"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.5"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-04","tag":"MC-GCHEM-RATES","stem":"A reaction is followed by measuring the mass of CO₂ lost over time. The rate at one particular moment is found from:","options":{"A":"the total mass lost divided by the total time","B":"the gradient of a tangent to the mass–time curve","C":"the mass reading at that instant divided by time","D":"the area under the mass–time curve to that point"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"The rate at an instant is the gradient of the curve at that point, found by drawing a tangent and calculating rise over run. Dividing total change by total time gives only the mean rate over the whole run. The initial rate is the gradient of the tangent at t = 0, which is why initial-rate methods draw the tangent at the very start of the curve.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does the gradient of a concentration–time graph for a reactant become less steep as the reaction proceeds?","options":{"A":"The activation energy rises as more product forms","B":"The catalyst is gradually used up during the reaction","C":"The temperature of the mixture drops as it reacts","D":"Reactant concentration falls, so collisions are rarer"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.5"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-05","tag":"MC-GCHEM-RATES","stem":"Why does increasing the pressure of a gaseous reaction increase its rate?","options":{"A":"The particles gain more kinetic energy on average","B":"The activation energy of the reaction is lowered by the pressure","C":"More particles are in the same volume, so collide more often","D":"A larger fraction of the collisions have the correct orientation"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"Raising the pressure of a gas squeezes the same particles into a smaller volume, which is the same as raising the concentration. Collisions become more frequent, so successful collisions per second rise. The energy of each particle is unchanged, so the fraction of collisions with at least the activation energy is unchanged. Only temperature changes that fraction, and only a catalyst lowers the activation energy.","reforge":{"stem":"Which change increases the proportion of collisions that have at least the activation energy?","options":{"A":"Raising the temperature","B":"Increasing the concentration","C":"Increasing the gas pressure","D":"Using smaller solid pieces"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.5"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-06","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ACIDS-REDOX","stem":"What is the oxidation state of manganese in the manganate(VII) ion, MnO₄⁻?","options":{"A":"−1","B":"+6","C":"+8","D":"+7"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"Oxygen is −2 in almost all its compounds, so four oxygens contribute −8. The oxidation states in an ion add up to the charge on the ion, here −1. So Mn + (−8) = −1, giving Mn = +7. The Roman numeral in the name, manganate(VII), records exactly this. Use the same method for any ion: assign the elements with fixed states first, then solve for the one left.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the oxidation state of chromium in the dichromate(VI) ion, Cr₂O₇²⁻?","options":{"A":"+12","B":"+6","C":"+3","D":"+7"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.7"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-07","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ACIDS-REDOX","stem":"Which half-equation correctly shows Fe²⁺ being oxidised?","options":{"A":"Fe²⁺ → Fe³⁺ + e⁻","B":"Fe²⁺ + e⁻ → Fe³⁺","C":"Fe³⁺ + e⁻ → Fe²⁺","D":"Fe²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Fe"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"Oxidation is loss of electrons (OIL RIG), so the electrons appear on the product side of an oxidation half-equation. Fe²⁺ losing one electron becomes Fe³⁺: Fe²⁺ → Fe³⁺ + e⁻. Check the charges balance: 2+ on the left, 3+ and 1− (net 2+) on the right. A half-equation with electrons on the left is a reduction.","reforge":{"stem":"When MnO₄⁻ is reduced to Mn²⁺ in acidic solution, how many electrons appear in the balanced half-equation?","options":{"A":"Two","B":"Seven","C":"Five","D":"Three"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.7"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-08","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ACIDS-REDOX","stem":"In the reaction 2Na + Cl₂ → 2NaCl, which species is the oxidising agent?","options":{"A":"Na, because it loses electrons","B":"Cl₂, because it gains electrons","C":"NaCl, because it is the product","D":"Na, because it gains electrons"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"An oxidising agent oxidises something else, and does so by taking its electrons, so the oxidising agent is itself reduced. Chlorine goes from 0 in Cl₂ to −1 in NaCl: it gains electrons, so it is the oxidising agent. Sodium goes from 0 to +1: it loses electrons, is oxidised, and is the reducing agent.","reforge":{"stem":"Hydrogen peroxide converts iodide ions into iodine. What role does the hydrogen peroxide play?","options":{"A":"Reducing agent, as it donates electrons","B":"Acid, as it donates a proton to iodide","C":"Catalyst, as it is left unchanged","D":"Oxidising agent, as it takes electrons"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.7"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-09","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ACIDS-REDOX","stem":"When two half-equations are combined into an overall redox equation, they are first scaled so that:","options":{"A":"the oxidation states on both sides add to zero","B":"the numbers of H⁺ ions on each side are equal","C":"the electrons lost equal the electrons gained","D":"each species appears with a coefficient of one"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"Electrons cannot appear in an overall equation, because every electron released by the species being oxidised is taken up by the species being reduced. Multiply one or both half-equations so the electron numbers match, add them, then cancel the electrons and any H⁺ or H₂O that appear on both sides.","reforge":{"stem":"Combine Zn → Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻ with Ag⁺ + e⁻ → Ag. What is the overall equation?","options":{"A":"Zn + 2Ag⁺ → Zn²⁺ + 2Ag","B":"Zn + Ag⁺ → Zn²⁺ + Ag","C":"Zn²⁺ + 2Ag → Zn + 2Ag⁺","D":"2Zn + Ag⁺ → 2Zn²⁺ + Ag"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.7"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-10","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ENERGETICS","stem":"Which process has a positive entropy change, ΔS?","options":{"A":"Water freezing to ice at 0 °C","B":"Steam condensing to liquid water","C":"Two moles of gas combining to give one mole of gas","D":"Solid ammonium chloride decomposing to two gases"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"Entropy measures the number of ways particles and their energy can be arranged. It rises from solid to liquid to gas, and rises when the number of moles of gas increases. NH₄Cl(s) → NH₃(g) + HCl(g) turns an ordered solid into two moles of gas, so ΔS is strongly positive. Condensing, freezing and reducing the moles of gas all decrease entropy.","reforge":{"stem":"For N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) → 2NH₃(g), what is the sign of ΔS, and why?","options":{"A":"Positive, as a product forms from elements","B":"Negative, as four moles of gas become two","C":"Zero, as every species is a gas","D":"Positive, as the reaction is exothermic"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.8"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-11","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ENERGETICS","stem":"A reaction has ΔH = −90 kJ mol⁻¹ and ΔS = −200 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹. When is it feasible?","options":{"A":"Only below 450 K","B":"At all temperatures","C":"Only above 450 K","D":"At no temperature"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"A reaction is feasible when ΔG = ΔH − TΔS ≤ 0. With ΔS negative, the −TΔS term is positive and grows with temperature, so the reaction is feasible only while ΔH outweighs it. Convert ΔS to kJ: −0.200 kJ K⁻¹ mol⁻¹. ΔG = 0 when T = ΔH/ΔS = −90 / −0.200 = 450 K. Below 450 K, ΔG is negative.","reforge":{"stem":"A reaction has a positive ΔH and a negative ΔS. What can be said about its feasibility?","options":{"A":"It becomes feasible at low temperature","B":"It becomes feasible at high temperature","C":"It is never feasible at any temperature","D":"It is feasible at all temperatures"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.8"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-12","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ENERGETICS","stem":"The enthalpy of atomisation of an element is the enthalpy change when:","options":{"A":"one mole of the element in its standard state forms from gaseous atoms","B":"one mole of gaseous atoms forms from the element in its standard state","C":"one mole of the element burns completely in oxygen under standard conditions","D":"one mole of gaseous ions forms from the element in its standard state"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"Atomisation always produces one mole of gaseous atoms, and it is always endothermic because bonds (or metallic bonding) must be broken. For chlorine it is ½Cl₂(g) → Cl(g), so it is half the Cl–Cl bond enthalpy. For sodium it is Na(s) → Na(g). In a Born–Haber cycle these steps come before ionisation and electron affinity.","reforge":{"stem":"In a Born–Haber cycle for sodium chloride, which step is the first electron affinity?","options":{"A":"Na(s) → Na(g)","B":"½Cl₂(g) → Cl(g)","C":"Na(g) → Na⁺(g) + e⁻","D":"Cl(g) + e⁻ → Cl⁻(g)"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.8"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-13","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ENERGETICS","stem":"The enthalpy of solution of an ionic solid is equal to:","options":{"A":"lattice formation enthalpy plus the sum of the hydration enthalpies","B":"hydration enthalpy of the cation minus that of the anion","C":"lattice dissociation enthalpy plus the sum of the hydration enthalpies","D":"lattice dissociation enthalpy minus the sum of the hydration enthalpies"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"Dissolving can be split into two steps: break the lattice into gaseous ions (lattice dissociation, endothermic) and then surround each gaseous ion with water (hydration, exothermic). So ΔsolH = ΔLdissH + ΣΔhydH. If hydration releases more than the lattice costs, dissolving is exothermic. Using lattice formation enthalpy instead gets the sign of that step wrong.","reforge":{"stem":"Hydration enthalpy is more exothermic for Mg²⁺ than for Na⁺ because Mg²⁺:","options":{"A":"has a higher charge and a smaller radius","B":"has a larger ionic radius and a lower charge","C":"has more electrons in its outer shell","D":"forms covalent bonds with water"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.8"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-14","tag":"MC-GCHEM-RATES","stem":"For rate = k[A][B]², what is the overall order of the reaction?","options":{"A":"Fourth","B":"Second","C":"First","D":"Third"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"The overall order is the sum of the individual orders in the rate equation: first order in A plus second order in B gives third order overall. The orders come from experiment, not from the coefficients in the balanced equation, so you cannot predict them by looking at the equation alone.","reforge":{"stem":"For rate = k[A][B]², with rate in mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹, what are the units of k?","options":{"A":"mol⁻¹ dm³ s⁻¹","B":"mol⁻² dm⁶ s⁻¹","C":"mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹","D":"s⁻¹"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.9"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-15","tag":"MC-GCHEM-RATES","stem":"In initial-rate experiments, tripling the concentration of X multiplies the rate by nine. What is the order with respect to X?","options":{"A":"Second","B":"Zero","C":"Fourth","D":"First"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"If rate ∝ [X]ⁿ, then multiplying [X] by 3 multiplies the rate by 3ⁿ. Here 3ⁿ = 9, so n = 2: second order. First order would give a threefold rise, zero order no change at all. Change one concentration at a time and keep everything else constant, or the effects cannot be separated.","reforge":{"stem":"A reaction is zero order with respect to Y. What does a graph of rate against [Y] look like?","options":{"A":"A curve falling towards zero","B":"A straight line through the origin","C":"A horizontal straight line","D":"A curve that rises ever more steeply"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.9"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-16","tag":"MC-GCHEM-RATES","stem":"The half-life of a first-order reaction:","options":{"A":"halves each time a half-life passes","B":"stays constant whatever the concentration","C":"doubles each time a half-life passes","D":"depends directly on the starting concentration"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"A first-order reaction has a constant half-life: it takes the same time to fall from 1.0 to 0.5 mol dm⁻³ as from 0.5 to 0.25. That makes a constant half-life on a concentration–time graph a quick test for first order. Radioactive decay is the classic example. For other orders the half-life changes as the concentration falls.","reforge":{"stem":"A first-order reaction has a half-life of 20 s. What fraction of the reactant remains after 60 s?","options":{"A":"One third","B":"One quarter","C":"One sixth","D":"One eighth"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.9"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-17","tag":"MC-GCHEM-RATES","stem":"For NO₂ + CO → NO + CO₂, rate = k[NO₂]². What does this suggest about the rate-determining step?","options":{"A":"It involves one NO₂ and one CO","B":"It involves only CO molecules","C":"It involves two NO₂ and no CO","D":"It must be the final step"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"Species in the rate equation, with their orders, tell you what takes part in the rate-determining step. Second order in NO₂ and zero order in CO means the slow step involves two NO₂ particles and no CO; CO must react in a later, fast step. The overall equation alone cannot tell you this.","reforge":{"stem":"A mechanism has a slow first step A + B → X, then a fast step X + B → C. What is the rate equation?","options":{"A":"rate = k[A][B]","B":"rate = k[A][B]²","C":"rate = k[X][B]","D":"rate = k[A]"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.9"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-18","tag":"MC-GCHEM-RATES","stem":"In the Arrhenius equation, k = Ae^(−Ea/RT), increasing the activation energy:","options":{"A":"increases k at a given temperature","B":"has no effect on the value of k","C":"increases the value of A instead","D":"decreases k at a given temperature"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"A larger Ea makes −Ea/RT more negative, so e^(−Ea/RT) is smaller and k is smaller: fewer collisions have enough energy. Raising T makes −Ea/RT less negative and increases k. Taking logs gives ln k = ln A − Ea/RT, so a plot of ln k against 1/T is a straight line with gradient −Ea/R.","reforge":{"stem":"A plot of ln k against 1/T has a gradient of −6000 K. What is Ea? (R = 8.31 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹)","options":{"A":"6.00 kJ mol⁻¹","B":"49.9 kJ mol⁻¹","C":"722 J mol⁻¹","D":"−49.9 kJ mol⁻¹"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.9"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-19","tag":"MC-GCHEM-EQUILIBRIA","stem":"For N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g), what is the expression for Kp?","options":{"A":"p(NH₃)² / p(N₂) × p(H₂)³","B":"p(N₂) × p(H₂)³ / p(NH₃)²","C":"2p(NH₃) / p(N₂) × 3p(H₂)","D":"p(NH₃)² / [p(N₂) + p(H₂)³]"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"Kp is written like Kc but with partial pressures: products on top, reactants underneath, each raised to the power of its coefficient in the equation. Coefficients become powers, never multipliers, and the terms are multiplied, never added. Only gases appear in Kp.","reforge":{"stem":"What are the units of Kp for N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g), with pressures in kPa?","options":{"A":"kPa²","B":"kPa⁻¹","C":"kPa⁻²","D":"no units"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.10"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-20","tag":"MC-GCHEM-EQUILIBRIA","stem":"The mole fraction of a gas in an equilibrium mixture is:","options":{"A":"its partial pressure times the total pressure","B":"its moles divided by the total moles of gas","C":"its moles divided by the moles of product","D":"its mass divided by the total mass of gas"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"Mole fraction x(A) = moles of A ÷ total moles of gas, so the mole fractions in a mixture add up to 1. The partial pressure is then p(A) = x(A) × total pressure, and the partial pressures add up to the total pressure. Use moles, not masses, because pressure depends on the number of particles.","reforge":{"stem":"At equilibrium, 1.0 mol of N₂O₄ and 2.0 mol of NO₂ are at a total pressure of 150 kPa. What is p(NO₂)?","options":{"A":"50 kPa","B":"75 kPa","C":"300 kPa","D":"100 kPa"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.10"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-21","tag":"MC-GCHEM-EQUILIBRIA","stem":"Increasing the total pressure on a gaseous equilibrium at constant temperature changes Kp by:","options":{"A":"increasing it if there are fewer moles of gas on the right","B":"increasing it in proportion to the rise in pressure","C":"nothing, because only a temperature change alters Kp","D":"decreasing it if there are more moles of gas on the right"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"Kp, like Kc, depends only on temperature. Raising the pressure may shift the position of equilibrium towards the side with fewer moles of gas, but the partial pressures then readjust so the ratio in the Kp expression returns to the same value. Position and constant are different things: the position can move while the constant stays fixed.","reforge":{"stem":"For an exothermic equilibrium, raising the temperature makes Kp:","options":{"A":"smaller, as the equilibrium shifts to the left","B":"larger, as the equilibrium shifts to the right","C":"unchanged, as only pressure affects Kp","D":"larger, as the reaction rate increases"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.10"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-22","tag":"MC-GCHEM-EQUILIBRIA","stem":"Kp for H₂(g) + I₂(g) ⇌ 2HI(g) has no units because:","options":{"A":"Kp never has units for any equilibrium in the gas phase","B":"partial pressures are always given as fractions of the total pressure","C":"hydrogen iodide is not a gas at the temperature used","D":"the pressure terms cancel, with two moles of gas on each side"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"Work out units by substituting kPa into the expression: Kp = p(HI)² / p(H₂)p(I₂) gives kPa² / kPa² — the units cancel. That happens whenever the total moles of gas are equal on each side. Where they differ, Kp has units such as kPa or kPa⁻², so always work them out from the expression.","reforge":{"stem":"For 2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2SO₃(g), Kp = 3.0 × 10⁴ kPa⁻¹ at a given temperature. What does the large value suggest?","options":{"A":"The reaction is endothermic","B":"The equilibrium lies well to the right","C":"The forward reaction is very fast","D":"The equilibrium lies far over to the left"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.10"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-23","tag":"MC-GCHEM-EQUILIBRIA","stem":"In an equilibrium mixture, the partial pressures of all the gases add up to:","options":{"A":"the total pressure","B":"the value of Kp","C":"one atmosphere","D":"the standard pressure of 100 kPa"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"Each partial pressure is the share of the total pressure caused by one gas: p(A) = x(A) × p(total). Because the mole fractions add up to 1, the partial pressures add up to the total pressure of the mixture, whatever that pressure is. That gives a useful check on any Kp calculation.","reforge":{"stem":"A mixture at 200 kPa has p(A) = 50 kPa and p(B) = 30 kPa. Gas C is the only other gas present. What is the mole fraction of C?","options":{"A":"0.25","B":"0.40","C":"0.60","D":"0.15"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.10"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-24","tag":"MC-GCHEM-EQUILIBRIA","stem":"More of a gaseous reactant is added at constant temperature, and the equilibrium shifts right. What happens to Kp?","options":{"A":"It increases, because more product forms","B":"It stays the same once equilibrium is restored","C":"It decreases, because there is now more reactant present","D":"It doubles if the added amount doubles"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"Adding reactant makes the ratio in the Kp expression smaller than Kp for a moment, so the system reacts in the forward direction until the ratio is back to Kp. The position has moved to the right, but the constant has not changed. Only a change in temperature changes the value of Kp.","reforge":{"stem":"Kp for a reaction is 0.25 at 500 K and 4.0 at 700 K. What does this show?","options":{"A":"A catalyst was added at 700 K","B":"The forward reaction is exothermic","C":"Pressure was raised between the two","D":"The forward reaction is endothermic"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.10"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-25","tag":"MC-GCHEM-EQUILIBRIA","stem":"For PCl₅(g) ⇌ PCl₃(g) + Cl₂(g), Kp = 180 kPa. If p(PCl₃) = p(Cl₂) = 60 kPa at equilibrium, what is p(PCl₅)?","options":{"A":"3.0 kPa","B":"0.33 kPa","C":"20 kPa","D":"200 kPa"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"Write the expression first: Kp = p(PCl₃) × p(Cl₂) / p(PCl₅). Rearrange for the unknown: p(PCl₅) = p(PCl₃) × p(Cl₂) / Kp = 60 × 60 / 180 = 20 kPa. Check the units: kPa × kPa / kPa leaves kPa, as a pressure should.","reforge":{"stem":"In an equilibrium mixture, the mole fraction of NH₃ is 0.20 and the total pressure is 20 000 kPa. What is p(NH₃)?","options":{"A":"4000 kPa","B":"20 kPa","C":"200 kPa","D":"100 000 kPa"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.10"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-26","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ACIDS-REDOX","stem":"In the cell representation Zn(s)|Zn²⁺(aq)||Cu²⁺(aq)|Cu(s), what does the double line represent?","options":{"A":"The voltmeter","B":"The external wire","C":"A phase boundary","D":"The salt bridge"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"In conventional cell representation a single vertical line is a phase boundary (solid to solution) and a double line is the salt bridge. The more negative electrode goes on the left, written reduced form | oxidised form; the right-hand half-cell is written oxidised form | reduced form. Then E(cell) = E(right) − E(left).","reforge":{"stem":"In the conventional representation of a cell, which half-cell is written on the left?","options":{"A":"The one with the more positive E°","B":"The one with the more negative E°","C":"The one that contains the salt bridge","D":"Whichever one contains a metal"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.11"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-27","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ACIDS-REDOX","stem":"E°(Cl₂/Cl⁻) = +1.36 V and E°(Br₂/Br⁻) = +1.09 V. What can be predicted?","options":{"A":"Cl₂ will oxidise Br⁻ to Br₂","B":"Br₂ will oxidise Cl⁻ to Cl₂","C":"Cl⁻ will reduce Br₂ to Br⁻","D":"No reaction occurs either way"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"The half-cell with the more positive E° is the better oxidising agent and is reduced; the one with the less positive E° is pushed the other way and oxidised. So Cl₂ (+1.36 V) takes electrons from Br⁻ (+1.09 V), giving Cl⁻ and Br₂. This is the electrode-potential explanation for chlorine displacing bromine.","reforge":{"stem":"Which is the strongest oxidising agent: Fe³⁺ (+0.77 V), Ag⁺ (+0.80 V), Zn²⁺ (−0.76 V) or Cu²⁺ (+0.34 V)?","options":{"A":"Fe³⁺","B":"Zn²⁺","C":"Ag⁺","D":"Cu²⁺"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.11"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-28","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ACIDS-REDOX","stem":"Why is a standard hydrogen electrode made with a platinum electrode?","options":{"A":"Platinum reacts with the H⁺ ions to release hydrogen gas","B":"Platinum is inert and gives a surface for electron transfer","C":"Platinum sets the electrode potential exactly to zero volts","D":"Platinum makes the hydrogen gas dissolve into the acid faster"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"The H⁺/H₂ half-cell contains no solid metal of its own, so an electrical contact is needed. Platinum is inert, so it takes no part in the reaction, and it conducts electrons between the H⁺/H₂ couple and the external circuit. The potential of zero is a definition of the reference, not something platinum causes.","reforge":{"stem":"Which conditions define a standard hydrogen electrode?","options":{"A":"Pure water, 100 kPa H₂ and 373 K","B":"1.00 mol dm⁻³ H⁺, 1 kPa H₂, 273 K","C":"0.10 mol dm⁻³ H⁺, 100 kPa H₂, 298 K","D":"1.00 mol dm⁻³ H⁺, 100 kPa H₂, 298 K"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.11"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-29","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ACIDS-REDOX","stem":"Why does a lithium-ion cell need a non-aqueous electrolyte?","options":{"A":"Water would freeze inside the cell","B":"Water would conduct electricity too well","C":"Lithium reacts vigorously with water","D":"Lithium ions do not dissolve in water"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"Lithium is a very reactive Group 1 metal with a very negative electrode potential, so in contact with water it would react, releasing hydrogen. Lithium-ion cells therefore use a lithium salt in an organic solvent or polymer. The large potential difference this makes possible is why the cells are light and powerful.","reforge":{"stem":"Recharging a lithium-ion cell works by:","options":{"A":"applying a current to reverse the cell reactions","B":"adding fresh lithium metal to the negative electrode","C":"replacing the electrolyte with a fresh supply","D":"letting the cell rest so the reactions reverse"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.11"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-30","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ATOMIC-STRUCTURE","stem":"Elements are classified into the s, p, d and f blocks according to:","options":{"A":"the charge on the ion they most commonly form","B":"the number of electron shells that their atoms contain","C":"whether they are metals, non-metals or semi-metals","D":"the sub-shell that holds their highest-energy electron"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"The block is named after the sub-shell being filled by an element's highest-energy electrons. Groups 1 and 2 fill an s sub-shell, Groups 3 to 0 fill a p sub-shell, and the transition elements fill d. Read it from the configuration: aluminium is [Ne] 3s² 3p¹, so it is p block even though it is a metal.","reforge":{"stem":"Bromine has the electron configuration [Ar] 3d¹⁰ 4s² 4p⁵. Which block is it in?","options":{"A":"f block","B":"p block","C":"d block","D":"s block"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.1"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-31","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ATOMIC-STRUCTURE","stem":"Why does first ionisation energy generally increase across Period 3?","options":{"A":"Nuclear charge rises while the shielding stays similar","B":"The outer electrons move into a new shell further out","C":"Shielding increases as more inner electrons are added","D":"The number of neutrons rises steadily across the period"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"Across a period, protons are added to the nucleus but the electrons go into the same outer shell, so shielding barely changes. The outer electrons feel a larger attraction, the atom shrinks, and more energy is needed to remove one. The two dips, at aluminium and sulfur, are exceptions explained by sub-shells, not by a reversal of this trend.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is the first ionisation energy of aluminium lower than that of magnesium?","options":{"A":"Its 3s electrons are paired, so they repel each other","B":"Aluminium has a smaller nuclear charge than magnesium","C":"Its outer electron is in 3p, higher in energy than 3s","D":"It has more neutrons, which shield its outer electron"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.1"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-32","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ATOMIC-STRUCTURE","stem":"Why is the first ionisation energy of sulfur lower than that of phosphorus?","options":{"A":"Sulfur's outer electron is in a higher-energy 3d orbital","B":"Two of sulfur's 3p electrons share an orbital and repel","C":"Sulfur has a smaller nuclear charge than phosphorus does","D":"Sulfur's outer electrons are in a shell further from the nucleus"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"Phosphorus is 3s² 3p³ with one electron in each 3p orbital. Sulfur is 3s² 3p⁴, so two electrons must share one 3p orbital. The repulsion between that pair makes one of them easier to remove, despite sulfur's extra proton. Both electrons are in the same shell, so distance is not the explanation.","reforge":{"stem":"Across Period 3, which element has the highest first ionisation energy?","options":{"A":"Phosphorus","B":"Chlorine","C":"Sodium","D":"Argon"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.1"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-33","tag":"MC-GCHEM-BONDING-STRUCTURE","stem":"Why does silicon have the highest melting point in Period 3?","options":{"A":"It forms an ionic lattice of Si⁴⁺ and Si⁴⁻ ions","B":"It has the strongest metallic bonding of any element in the period","C":"It is macromolecular, with many strong covalent bonds to break","D":"Its simple molecules have strong forces between them"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"Silicon has a giant covalent (macromolecular) structure like diamond: every atom is bonded covalently to four others, so melting means breaking a vast number of strong bonds. Sodium, magnesium and aluminium are metallic; phosphorus, sulfur and chlorine are simple molecules with weak van der Waals forces; argon is monatomic. Melting point peaks at silicon and then drops sharply.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does the melting point rise from sodium to magnesium to aluminium?","options":{"A":"More delocalised electrons and smaller, more charged ions","B":"Stronger van der Waals forces between ever larger molecules","C":"A gradual change from metallic to giant covalent bonding","D":"Fewer delocalised electrons and larger ions across the period"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.1"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-34","tag":"MC-GCHEM-BONDING-STRUCTURE","stem":"Why do phosphorus (P₄), sulfur (S₈) and chlorine (Cl₂) have low melting points?","options":{"A":"They form giant lattices held together by weak ionic bonds","B":"Their covalent bonds are weak and break easily on heating","C":"They have metallic bonding with few delocalised electrons","D":"Simple molecules held only by weak van der Waals forces"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"Melting a simple molecular substance separates the molecules; it does not break the covalent bonds inside them. Only the weak van der Waals forces between molecules are overcome, so little energy is needed. The size of the molecule decides the order: S₈ > P₄ > Cl₂, because larger molecules have more electrons and stronger van der Waals forces.","reforge":{"stem":"Sulfur (S₈) has a higher melting point than phosphorus (P₄) because:","options":{"A":"sulfur atoms have a smaller atomic radius than phosphorus atoms","B":"S₈ molecules are larger, so van der Waals forces are stronger","C":"sulfur forms a giant covalent lattice in the solid state","D":"sulfur has stronger covalent bonds within each of its molecules"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.1"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-35","tag":"MC-GCHEM-BONDING-STRUCTURE","stem":"How does the melting point change from silicon to phosphorus in Period 3?","options":{"A":"It falls sharply: giant covalent gives way to simple molecular","B":"It rises, because phosphorus has more delocalised electrons","C":"It stays similar, because both elements are non-metals","D":"It falls slightly, because phosphorus has a larger atomic radius"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"The drop from silicon to phosphorus is a change of structure, not a gradual trend. Silicon is giant covalent, so its covalent bonds must break to melt it. Phosphorus is P₄ molecules, so only weak van der Waals forces are overcome. Explain melting points by first naming the structure and then the force that has to be overcome.","reforge":{"stem":"Which Period 3 element has the lowest melting point?","options":{"A":"Sodium","B":"Chlorine","C":"Argon","D":"Sulfur"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.1"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-36","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ATOMIC-STRUCTURE","stem":"Why are sodium to argon all placed in Period 3?","options":{"A":"They all have three electrons in their outer shell","B":"Their outer electrons are all in the third shell","C":"They all form ions with a charge of three plus","D":"They each have three more neutrons than protons"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"The period number is the shell that the outer electrons occupy: every Period 3 element has outer electrons in the n = 3 shell. The group number tells you how many outer electrons there are. Periodicity means properties repeat in a regular pattern each period because the outer-shell arrangements repeat.","reforge":{"stem":"Elements in the same group have similar chemical properties because they have:","options":{"A":"the same number of neutrons","B":"the same number of electron shells","C":"the same relative atomic mass as each other","D":"the same number of outer-shell electrons"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.1"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-37","tag":"MC-GCHEM-BONDING-STRUCTURE","stem":"Why does sodium have a lower melting point than magnesium?","options":{"A":"Sodium has more delocalised electrons per atom than magnesium","B":"Sodium atoms are smaller, so the metallic bonding is weaker","C":"Na⁺ has a smaller charge, so the metallic bonding is weaker","D":"Sodium forms a simple molecular structure, not a metallic one"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"Metallic bonding is the attraction between positive ions and delocalised electrons. Magnesium gives two electrons per atom to the sea of electrons and forms Mg²⁺, which is also smaller than Na⁺. More charge and less distance mean a stronger attraction, so more energy is needed to melt magnesium.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does first ionisation energy fall sharply from neon to sodium?","options":{"A":"Its outer electron is in a new, more shielded shell further out","B":"Sodium has fewer protons in its nucleus than neon has","C":"Neon's electrons are all paired, so they repel each other","D":"Sodium's outer electron is in a p orbital rather than an s orbital"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.1"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-38","tag":"MC-GCHEM-INORGANIC-TESTS","stem":"Down Group 2, the solubility of the hydroxides:","options":{"A":"stays the same","B":"decreases","C":"decreases then increases","D":"increases"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"Group 2 hydroxides become more soluble down the group: Mg(OH)₂ is only sparingly soluble, while Ba(OH)₂ dissolves readily, giving a strongly alkaline solution. The sulfates show the opposite trend: MgSO₄ is soluble and BaSO₄ is insoluble. Learn the pair together, because questions often test them side by side.","reforge":{"stem":"Down Group 2, the solubility of the sulfates:","options":{"A":"increases","B":"decreases","C":"stays the same","D":"increases then falls"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.2"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-39","tag":"MC-GCHEM-INORGANIC-TESTS","stem":"Why is barium sulfate safe to swallow in a barium meal, even though barium ions are toxic?","options":{"A":"It is so insoluble that very few Ba²⁺ ions dissolve","B":"Barium ions are removed by stomach acid straight away","C":"The sulfate ion cancels out the toxicity of barium","D":"It is only absorbed by the lining of the large intestine"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"Soluble barium compounds are poisonous, but BaSO₄ is so insoluble that almost no Ba²⁺ is released into the body. It is opaque to X-rays, so a suspension outlines the gut in an image and passes out unchanged. This is a use of the trend that Group 2 sulfates become less soluble down the group.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is the solution acidified before barium chloride is added in the test for sulfate ions?","options":{"A":"To turn all of the sulfate ions into sulfuric acid first","B":"To make the barium sulfate dissolve, so that it can be seen clearly","C":"To remove carbonate ions, which also give a white precipitate","D":"To stop the barium ions from being oxidised by the air"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.2"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-40","tag":"MC-GCHEM-INORGANIC-TESTS","stem":"Magnesium hydroxide is used in indigestion remedies because it:","options":{"A":"kills the bacteria that cause acid","B":"neutralises excess stomach acid","C":"reacts with water to release oxygen","D":"dissolves fat in the stomach lining"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"Mg(OH)₂ is a base: Mg(OH)₂ + 2HCl → MgCl₂ + 2H₂O. Because it is only sparingly soluble, it does not make the stomach strongly alkaline. Calcium hydroxide is used the same way on a larger scale, to neutralise acidic soil. Both are uses of Group 2 hydroxides as bases.","reforge":{"stem":"Farmers spread calcium hydroxide on their fields mainly to:","options":{"A":"add phosphate for growth","B":"add nitrogen to the soil","C":"kill weeds in the crop","D":"neutralise acidic soil"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.2"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-41","tag":"MC-GCHEM-INORGANIC-TESTS","stem":"Calcium oxide or calcium carbonate is used in coal-fired power stations to:","options":{"A":"absorb nitrogen oxides formed in the furnace","B":"remove carbon dioxide from the flue gases","C":"remove sulfur dioxide from the flue gases","D":"act as a catalyst for burning the coal"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"Burning coal releases SO₂, which causes acid rain. A slurry of calcium oxide or calcium carbonate reacts with it: CaO + SO₂ → CaSO₃, or CaCO₃ + SO₂ → CaSO₃ + CO₂. SO₂ is an acidic oxide and CaO a basic one, so this is a neutralisation. The product can be oxidised to calcium sulfate and sold as gypsum.","reforge":{"stem":"Which equation shows calcium oxide removing sulfur dioxide?","options":{"A":"CaO + SO₂ → CaSO₃","B":"CaO + SO₂ → CaSO₄","C":"CaO + SO₂ → Ca + SO₃","D":"CaO + SO₂ → CaO₂ + S"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.2"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-42","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ACIDS-REDOX","stem":"In the extraction of titanium, magnesium is used to:","options":{"A":"electrolyse molten titanium oxide","B":"dissolve titanium sulfate in aqueous solution","C":"heat titanium ore with carbon","D":"reduce titanium(IV) chloride to titanium"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"Titanium cannot be extracted by heating its oxide with carbon, because titanium carbide forms and makes the metal brittle. Instead, TiO₂ is converted to TiCl₄, which is then reduced by a more reactive metal: TiCl₄ + 2Mg → Ti + 2MgCl₂. Magnesium is oxidised from 0 to +2 and titanium is reduced from +4 to 0.","reforge":{"stem":"In TiCl₄ + 2Mg → Ti + 2MgCl₂, magnesium acts as:","options":{"A":"an oxidising agent","B":"a reducing agent","C":"a catalyst","D":"an acid"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.2"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-43","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ATOMIC-STRUCTURE","stem":"Why does the reactivity of Group 2 metals with water increase down the group?","options":{"A":"Larger atoms lose their outer electrons more easily","B":"Denser metals sink, so they react faster with water","C":"The nuclear charge falls as you go down the group","D":"Each atom gains more electrons as you go down the group"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"Reacting means losing the two outer electrons. Down the group the atoms are larger and the outer electrons are more shielded, so ionisation energies fall and the electrons are lost more easily. Nuclear charge actually rises down the group; it is outweighed by the extra distance and shielding.","reforge":{"stem":"Calcium reacts with cold water. What are the products?","options":{"A":"Calcium oxide and hydrogen","B":"Calcium hydroxide and oxygen","C":"Calcium hydroxide and hydrogen","D":"Calcium carbonate and hydrogen"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.2"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-44","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ATOMIC-STRUCTURE","stem":"Why does atomic radius increase down Group 2?","options":{"A":"The outer electrons pair up more often","B":"Each element has one more shell of electrons","C":"The nuclear charge decreases down the group","D":"There are fewer protons pulling on the electrons"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"Each step down a group adds a full shell, so the outer electrons are further from the nucleus and more shielded by the inner shells. Although the nuclear charge rises, the extra distance and shielding win. This one idea explains the falling ionisation energy and rising reactivity down the group.","reforge":{"stem":"Why do the melting points of the Group 2 metals generally decrease down the group?","options":{"A":"The ionic bonds get weaker as the ionic charge falls","B":"Each atom contributes fewer delocalised electrons","C":"The intermolecular forces between atoms become weaker","D":"Larger ions attract the delocalised electrons less"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.2"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-45","tag":"MC-GCHEM-INORGANIC-TESTS","stem":"What is seen when magnesium is placed in cold water?","options":{"A":"No reaction at all, even after several days","B":"Rapid fizzing as the metal melts into a shiny ball","C":"A very slow reaction, giving a few bubbles of hydrogen","D":"A bright white flame, and a white solid forms on the metal"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"Magnesium reacts only very slowly with cold water, forming magnesium hydroxide and a little hydrogen: Mg + 2H₂O → Mg(OH)₂ + H₂. With steam it reacts much faster, burning with a bright white flame to give magnesium oxide. Further down the group, calcium, strontium and barium react with cold water increasingly vigorously.","reforge":{"stem":"Sodium hydroxide is added to a solution containing Mg²⁺ ions. What is seen?","options":{"A":"A white precipitate of Mg(OH)₂","B":"A blue precipitate of Mg(OH)₂","C":"A brown gas is given off","D":"No change, as Mg(OH)₂ is soluble"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.2"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-46","tag":"MC-GCHEM-BONDING-STRUCTURE","stem":"Why does the boiling point of the halogens increase down Group 7?","options":{"A":"Hydrogen bonding between the molecules increases down the group","B":"The covalent bond between the two atoms gets stronger down the group","C":"The molecules become more polar down the group, so their dipoles attract","D":"Larger molecules have more electrons, so stronger van der Waals forces"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"Halogens are diatomic, non-polar molecules, so boiling separates molecules held only by van der Waals forces. Going down the group the molecules have more electrons, which gives larger temporary dipoles and stronger forces. Boiling does not break the X–X bond. That is why fluorine and chlorine are gases, bromine a liquid and iodine a solid.","reforge":{"stem":"At room temperature, iodine is a:","options":{"A":"pale green gas","B":"grey-black solid","C":"red-brown liquid","D":"colourless gas"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.3"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-47","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ATOMIC-STRUCTURE","stem":"Why does electronegativity decrease down Group 7?","options":{"A":"The bonding pair is further away and more shielded","B":"The nuclear charge of the atoms falls as you go down the group","C":"The atoms have more electrons in their outer shell","D":"The halogens become more metallic in their bonding"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"Electronegativity is the power of an atom to attract the bonding pair in a covalent bond. Down the group the atoms are larger, so the bonding pair is further from the nucleus and shielded by more inner shells. Nuclear charge rises, but distance and shielding win. Every halogen has seven outer electrons.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is fluorine the strongest oxidising agent in Group 7?","options":{"A":"Its molecules have the strongest covalent bond of all","B":"It has the largest number of electron shells in the group","C":"Its small atom attracts an incoming electron strongly","D":"It is the only halogen that is a gas at room temperature"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.3"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-48","tag":"MC-GCHEM-INORGANIC-TESTS","stem":"Concentrated sulfuric acid is added to solid sodium chloride. What are the products?","options":{"A":"Sodium sulfate, chlorine gas and sulfur dioxide","B":"Sodium hydrogensulfate and hydrogen chloride","C":"Hydrogen sulfide, sulfur and chlorine","D":"Sodium hydroxide and chlorine gas"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"Chloride is too weak a reducing agent to reduce sulfuric acid, so only an acid–base reaction happens: NaCl + H₂SO₄ → NaHSO₄ + HCl. Misty fumes of HCl are seen. Bromide and iodide are stronger reducing agents, so with them redox reactions follow, giving SO₂ and, for iodide, S and H₂S as well.","reforge":{"stem":"Concentrated sulfuric acid is added to solid sodium iodide. Why is H₂S among the products?","options":{"A":"The sodium ions reduce the sulfate ions to hydrogen sulfide","B":"Iodide is a strong oxidising agent and oxidises sulfur to +6","C":"Sulfuric acid decomposes on its own when it is warmed","D":"Iodide is a strong reducing agent, reducing S from +6 to −2"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.3"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-49","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ACIDS-REDOX","stem":"Why do halide ions become stronger reducing agents down Group 7?","options":{"A":"The nuclear charge falls as you go down the group","B":"Larger ions attract extra electrons more strongly","C":"Larger ions lose their outer electron more easily","D":"Smaller ions give up their electrons more readily"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"A halide ion acts as a reducing agent by giving away an electron. In a larger ion the outer electrons are further from the nucleus and more shielded, so they are lost more easily. So iodide is the strongest reducing agent of the halides and fluoride the weakest. This is the reverse of the halogens' trend in oxidising power.","reforge":{"stem":"Concentrated sulfuric acid is added to solid sodium bromide. Which gases are produced?","options":{"A":"Hydrogen bromide, bromine and sulfur dioxide","B":"Hydrogen bromide only, with no redox step","C":"Hydrogen sulfide, sulfur and hydrogen iodide","D":"Chlorine and sulfur trioxide"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.3"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-50","tag":"MC-GCHEM-INORGANIC-TESTS","stem":"Silver nitrate is added to sodium bromide solution. What is seen, and how does the precipitate behave in ammonia?","options":{"A":"White precipitate, which dissolves in dilute ammonia","B":"No precipitate forms, as silver bromide is soluble","C":"Yellow precipitate, insoluble even in concentrated ammonia","D":"Cream precipitate, which dissolves in concentrated ammonia"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"Acidified silver nitrate gives AgCl (white), AgBr (cream) and AgI (yellow). Because the colours are hard to tell apart, ammonia is used to confirm: AgCl dissolves in dilute ammonia, AgBr only in concentrated ammonia, and AgI in neither. Silver fluoride is soluble, so fluoride gives no precipitate at all.","reforge":{"stem":"A halide solution gives a yellow precipitate with acidified silver nitrate that does not dissolve in concentrated ammonia. The halide is:","options":{"A":"bromide","B":"iodide","C":"chloride","D":"fluoride"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.3"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-51","tag":"MC-GCHEM-INORGANIC-TESTS","stem":"Why does fluoride give no result in the silver nitrate test for halides?","options":{"A":"Silver fluoride is soluble, so no precipitate forms","B":"Fluoride ions are oxidised by the nitric acid","C":"Silver fluoride is a gas at room temperature","D":"Fluoride forms a black precipitate with silver ions"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"The test relies on an insoluble silver halide precipitating: Ag⁺(aq) + X⁻(aq) → AgX(s). AgCl, AgBr and AgI are insoluble, but AgF is soluble, so the solution stays clear. A negative result therefore cannot rule out fluoride. The nitric acid is there to remove carbonate and hydroxide ions, which would also precipitate.","reforge":{"stem":"Chlorine is added to drinking water. Why is its small health risk considered acceptable?","options":{"A":"It turns the water alkaline, which prevents corrosion","B":"It removes all of the dissolved minerals from the water supply","C":"It kills bacteria, and that benefit outweighs the risk","D":"It reacts completely, leaving no chlorine at all"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.3"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-52","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ACIDS-REDOX","stem":"Chlorine reacts with water: Cl₂ + H₂O ⇌ HCl + HClO. What has happened to the chlorine?","options":{"A":"It has only been oxidised, from 0 to +1","B":"It has been both oxidised and reduced","C":"It has only been reduced, from 0 to −1","D":"It has acted only as an acid"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"Chlorine starts at 0 in Cl₂. In HCl it is −1 (reduced) and in HClO it is +1 (oxidised). The same element being oxidised and reduced in one reaction is disproportionation. HClO (chloric(I) acid) is what kills bacteria, which is why chlorine is used to treat water.","reforge":{"stem":"In sunlight, chlorine water slowly gives off a gas: 2Cl₂ + 2H₂O → 4HCl + O₂. What is the gas?","options":{"A":"Hydrogen chloride","B":"Hydrogen gas","C":"Chlorine gas","D":"Oxygen gas"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.3"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-53","tag":"MC-GCHEM-INORGANIC-TESTS","stem":"Chlorine water is added to potassium iodide solution. What colour change is seen?","options":{"A":"Colourless to green, as chlorine dissolves","B":"Brown to colourless, as iodide is formed","C":"Colourless to brown, as iodine is displaced","D":"No change, because chlorine is less reactive than iodine"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"A more reactive halogen oxidises the halide of a less reactive one: Cl₂ + 2I⁻ → 2Cl⁻ + I₂. Iodine in water is brown. Chlorine displaces bromide and iodide, bromine displaces only iodide, and iodine displaces neither. That order follows the oxidising power of the halogens, which falls down the group.","reforge":{"stem":"Which mixture would show no displacement reaction?","options":{"A":"Bromine water with potassium chloride","B":"Chlorine water with potassium bromide","C":"Chlorine water with potassium iodide","D":"Bromine water with potassium iodide"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.3"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-54","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ACIDS-REDOX","stem":"Chlorine reacts with cold, dilute sodium hydroxide. What is the solution formed mainly used for?","options":{"A":"Fuel, containing dissolved hydrogen","B":"Fertiliser, containing sodium nitrate","C":"Antacid, containing sodium carbonate","D":"Bleach, containing sodium chlorate(I)"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"Cl₂ + 2NaOH → NaCl + NaClO + H₂O. The sodium chlorate(I), NaClO, is the active ingredient in household bleach. Chlorine is reduced to −1 in NaCl and oxidised to +1 in NaClO, so this is another disproportionation reaction.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the formula of sodium chlorate(I)?","options":{"A":"NaClO₃","B":"NaClO","C":"NaCl","D":"NaClO₄"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.3"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-55","tag":"MC-GCHEM-INORGANIC-TESTS","stem":"Sodium oxide is added to water. What forms, and what is the approximate pH?","options":{"A":"Sodium hydroxide, pH 14","B":"Sodium hydroxide, pH 9","C":"Nothing dissolves, so pH 7","D":"Sodium carbonate, pH 11"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"Na₂O is a basic ionic oxide. The oxide ion reacts with water to give hydroxide ions: Na₂O + H₂O → 2NaOH, which is very soluble, so the pH is about 14. MgO gives Mg(OH)₂, which is only slightly soluble, so its solution is about pH 9. The non-metal oxides give acidic solutions.","reforge":{"stem":"Phosphorus(V) oxide, P₄O₁₀, reacts with water. What is the product?","options":{"A":"Phosphoric(III) acid, H₃PO₃","B":"Phosphorus hydroxide, P(OH)₅","C":"Phosphoric(V) acid, H₃PO₄","D":"Phosphine gas, PH₃"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.4"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-56","tag":"MC-GCHEM-BONDING-STRUCTURE","stem":"Why does magnesium oxide have a higher melting point than sodium oxide?","options":{"A":"Sodium oxide has only weak van der Waals forces between molecules","B":"Mg²⁺ has a larger charge, so the ionic attraction is stronger","C":"Magnesium oxide is giant covalent, while sodium oxide is ionic","D":"Magnesium oxide contains more delocalised electrons"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"Both are giant ionic lattices. Mg²⁺ carries twice the charge of Na⁺ and is smaller, so the electrostatic attraction to O²⁻ is much stronger and more energy is needed to break the lattice. Across Period 3 the oxides change from ionic (Na₂O, MgO, Al₂O₃) to giant covalent (SiO₂) to simple molecular (P₄O₁₀, SO₂).","reforge":{"stem":"Why do SO₂ and P₄O₁₀ have much lower melting points than SiO₂?","options":{"A":"They contain larger atoms, which give them more shielding","B":"Their covalent bonds are weaker than those in SiO₂ molecules","C":"They are ionic, while SiO₂ is held together by metallic bonding","D":"They are simple molecular, but SiO₂ is giant covalent"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.4"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-57","tag":"MC-GCHEM-INORGANIC-TESTS","stem":"Aluminium oxide is described as amphoteric. This means it:","options":{"A":"contains both ionic and covalent bonding","B":"is insoluble in water but soluble in oil","C":"reacts with both acids and bases","D":"acts as both an oxidising and a reducing agent"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"An amphoteric oxide can behave as a base or as an acid. Al₂O₃ reacts with acids to form aluminium salts (Al₂O₃ + 6HCl → 2AlCl₃ + 3H₂O) and with hot alkali to form aluminate ions (Al₂O₃ + 2NaOH + 3H₂O → 2NaAl(OH)₄). It sits between the basic metal oxides and the acidic non-metal oxides in Period 3.","reforge":{"stem":"Silicon dioxide is classed as an acidic oxide because it:","options":{"A":"reacts with hot, concentrated sodium hydroxide","B":"dissolves in water to give an acidic solution","C":"reacts with hydrochloric acid to form a salt","D":"turns blue litmus red when it is added as a solid"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.4"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-58","tag":"MC-GCHEM-INORGANIC-TESTS","stem":"Sulfur burns in oxygen. What is seen, and what forms?","options":{"A":"A red flame, forming sulfuric acid","B":"A yellow flame, forming sulfur trioxide","C":"A white flame, forming sulfur oxide","D":"A blue flame, forming sulfur dioxide"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"Sulfur burns with a blue flame to give SO₂: S + O₂ → SO₂. Forming SO₃ needs a catalyst, as in the contact process. The other observations are worth learning: sodium burns yellow-orange, magnesium with a brilliant white flame, and phosphorus with a white flame giving white P₄O₁₀ smoke.","reforge":{"stem":"Sodium burns in oxygen. What is seen?","options":{"A":"A blue flame and a colourless gas","B":"A yellow-orange flame and a white solid","C":"A brilliant white flame and a grey solid","D":"A lilac flame and a yellow liquid"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.4"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-59","tag":"MC-GCHEM-INORGANIC-TESTS","stem":"Sulfur dioxide dissolves in water. What forms, and what is the approximate pH?","options":{"A":"Sulfurous acid, H₂SO₃, about pH 3","B":"Sulfuric acid, H₂SO₄, about pH 7","C":"Sulfur hydroxide, S(OH)₂, about pH 12","D":"Hydrogen sulfide, H₂S, about pH 9"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"SO₂ is an acidic oxide: SO₂ + H₂O → H₂SO₃. Sulfurous acid is a weak acid, so the pH is about 3, not as low as for sulfuric acid. SO₃ gives H₂SO₄ (pH about 0) and P₄O₁₀ gives H₃PO₄ (pH about 0). Non-metal oxides give acids; metal oxides give alkalis or are insoluble.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is a solution of magnesium hydroxide only weakly alkaline, at about pH 9?","options":{"A":"Mg(OH)₂ is a weak acid that partly cancels out its own OH⁻","B":"Magnesium ions react with the OH⁻ ions to form water","C":"Mg(OH)₂ is only slightly soluble, so few OH⁻ ions form","D":"Mg(OH)₂ is fully soluble but only partly ionic in water"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.4"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-60","tag":"MC-GCHEM-BONDING-STRUCTURE","stem":"Why does silicon dioxide not react with water?","options":{"A":"It is a basic oxide, so it reacts only with acids","B":"Breaking its giant covalent lattice would need too much energy","C":"It is an ionic compound that is much too insoluble in water to react","D":"Water molecules are too large to reach the silicon atoms"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"SiO₂ is a giant covalent lattice in which every silicon is bonded to four oxygens. Water cannot provide the energy to break those bonds, so SiO₂ is insoluble and the pH stays at 7. It still behaves as an acidic oxide, because it reacts with hot, concentrated alkali to form silicate: SiO₂ + 2NaOH → Na₂SiO₃ + H₂O.","reforge":{"stem":"Which Period 3 oxide is insoluble in water but reacts with sodium hydroxide?","options":{"A":"Sulfur trioxide","B":"Sodium oxide","C":"Magnesium oxide","D":"Silicon dioxide"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.4"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-61","tag":"MC-GCHEM-INORGANIC-TESTS","stem":"What is the trend in the acid–base nature of the Period 3 oxides from Na₂O to SO₃?","options":{"A":"Amphoteric, then basic, then neutral","B":"Acidic, then amphoteric, then basic","C":"Basic, then amphoteric, then acidic","D":"All basic, because they are all oxides"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"The ionic metal oxides (Na₂O, MgO) are basic, Al₂O₃ is amphoteric, and the covalent non-metal oxides (SiO₂, P₄O₁₀, SO₂, SO₃) are acidic. The change follows the bonding: ionic oxides release O²⁻, which takes protons from water, while covalent oxides react to form oxoacids.","reforge":{"stem":"Sodium oxide reacts with sulfuric acid. What type of reaction is this?","options":{"A":"Neutralisation, forming a salt and water","B":"Redox, as the sodium is oxidised by the acid","C":"Displacement, as sodium displaces hydrogen","D":"Thermal decomposition of the sodium oxide"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.4"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-62","tag":"MC-GCHEM-INORGANIC-TESTS","stem":"Sodium reacts with cold water. What forms, and what is the pH of the solution?","options":{"A":"Sodium oxide and hydrogen gas, about pH 7","B":"Sodium hydride and water, pH 1","C":"Sodium hydroxide and oxygen, pH 10","D":"Sodium hydroxide and hydrogen, pH 13–14"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"2Na + 2H₂O → 2NaOH + H₂. Sodium reacts vigorously, fizzing and moving on the surface, and the NaOH formed is fully soluble, so the solution is strongly alkaline. Magnesium reacts only very slowly with cold water, because it must lose two electrons and has higher ionisation energies.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does sodium react more vigorously with water than magnesium does?","options":{"A":"Magnesium has the lower first ionisation energy of the two","B":"Sodium loses its single outer electron more easily","C":"Sodium has the higher nuclear charge of the two","D":"Magnesium floats, so less of it touches the water"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.4"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-63","tag":"MC-GCHEM-INORGANIC-TESTS","stem":"Phosphorus burns in excess oxygen. Which oxide forms?","options":{"A":"Phosphorus(V) oxide, P₄O₁₀","B":"Phosphorus(III) oxide, P₄O₆","C":"Phosphorus(II) oxide, PO","D":"Phosphorus(IV) oxide, PO₂"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"In excess oxygen, phosphorus burns with a white flame to give clouds of white P₄O₁₀: P₄ + 5O₂ → P₄O₁₀. Phosphorus is in its highest oxidation state, +5, matching its five outer electrons. P₄O₁₀ is a simple molecular, acidic oxide that reacts with water to give phosphoric(V) acid.","reforge":{"stem":"P₄O₁₀ reacts with excess sodium hydroxide solution. Which salt forms?","options":{"A":"Sodium oxide, Na₂O","B":"Sodium phosphide, Na₃P","C":"Sodium phosphate, Na₃PO₄","D":"Sodium hydrogensulfate, NaHSO₄"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.4"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-64","tag":"MC-GCHEM-BONDING-STRUCTURE","stem":"What is the shape of [CuCl₄]²⁻, and why does it differ from [Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺?","options":{"A":"Octahedral, because there are six ligands","B":"Tetrahedral, because the chloride ligands are large","C":"Square planar, because the chloride ligands are small","D":"Linear, because copper has a 2+ charge"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"Water and ammonia are small ligands, so six fit around Cu²⁺, giving an octahedral complex. Chloride ions are larger, so only four fit, giving a tetrahedral complex. Adding concentrated HCl to blue [Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺ therefore gives yellow-green [CuCl₄]²⁻, a change in both coordination number and shape.","reforge":{"stem":"Concentrated hydrochloric acid is added to blue [Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺(aq). What colour does the solution become?","options":{"A":"Colourless","B":"Deep blue","C":"Pale violet-pink","D":"Yellow-green"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.5"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-65","tag":"MC-GCHEM-INORGANIC-TESTS","stem":"A solution containing VO²⁺, with vanadium in oxidation state +4, is what colour?","options":{"A":"Green","B":"Yellow","C":"Blue","D":"Violet"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"Vanadium shows four oxidation states in solution, each with its own colour: VO₂⁺ (+5) yellow, VO²⁺ (+4) blue, V³⁺ (+3) green and V²⁺ (+2) violet. Zinc in acid reduces vanadium(V) through each in turn. The green seen part way through can briefly be a mixture of yellow and blue, so name the states, not just the colours.","reforge":{"stem":"Zinc and acid reduce vanadium(V) one step at a time. What is the final colour, when V²⁺ forms?","options":{"A":"Violet","B":"Colourless","C":"Blue","D":"Green"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.5"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-66","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ACIDS-REDOX","stem":"In the contact process, vanadium(V) oxide catalyses 2SO₂ + O₂ → 2SO₃. How does it work?","options":{"A":"V₂O₅ lowers the overall enthalpy change of the reaction","B":"V₂O₅ adsorbs SO₂ but stays in the same oxidation state throughout","C":"V₂O₅ supplies the oxygen atoms and is gradually used up","D":"V₂O₅ oxidises SO₂, and the V₂O₄ formed is reoxidised by O₂"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"Transition metals make good catalysts because they change oxidation state easily. Here SO₂ + V₂O₅ → SO₃ + V₂O₄, then 2V₂O₄ + O₂ → 2V₂O₅. Vanadium goes from +5 to +4 and back, so the catalyst is regenerated and the route has a lower activation energy. ΔH is unchanged.","reforge":{"stem":"The reaction between MnO₄⁻ and ethanedioate ions starts slowly and then speeds up. Why?","options":{"A":"The concentration of the reactants increases","B":"The Mn²⁺ ions produced catalyse the reaction","C":"The MnO₄⁻ ions break down to form a catalyst","D":"The ethanedioate ions act as the catalyst"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.5"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-67","tag":"MC-GCHEM-BONDING-STRUCTURE","stem":"The anticancer drug cisplatin is [Pt(NH₃)₂Cl₂]. What is its shape?","options":{"A":"Square planar","B":"Tetrahedral","C":"Octahedral","D":"Trigonal planar"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"Platinum(II) complexes with four ligands are square planar, not tetrahedral. In cisplatin the two chloride ligands are next to each other (cis), which is the isomer that works as a drug. Square planar complexes can show cis–trans isomerism; tetrahedral complexes cannot.","reforge":{"stem":"How does cisplatin act as an anticancer drug?","options":{"A":"It oxidises the lipids in cancer cell membranes","B":"It replaces the iron in haemoglobin","C":"It binds to DNA and stops it from replicating","D":"It neutralises the acids in the tumour"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.5"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-68","tag":"MC-GCHEM-INORGANIC-TESTS","stem":"Why is [Fe(H₂O)₆]³⁺ more acidic than [Fe(H₂O)₆]²⁺?","options":{"A":"Fe²⁺ ions are larger, so they hold the water ligands more tightly","B":"Fe³⁺ has a higher charge density, so it polarises the water more","C":"Fe³⁺ has more water ligands arranged around it than Fe²⁺ does","D":"Fe³⁺ is a transition metal ion but Fe²⁺ is not one"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"The small, highly charged Fe³⁺ ion pulls electron density from the O–H bonds of its water ligands, so they release H⁺ more easily: [Fe(H₂O)₆]³⁺ + H₂O ⇌ [Fe(H₂O)₅(OH)]²⁺ + H₃O⁺. M³⁺ solutions are therefore more acidic than M²⁺ solutions. Both complexes are octahedral with six water ligands.","reforge":{"stem":"Sodium hydroxide is added to a solution containing Fe³⁺ ions. What is seen?","options":{"A":"A white precipitate","B":"A green precipitate","C":"A deep blue solution with no solid","D":"An orange-brown precipitate"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.6"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-69","tag":"MC-GCHEM-INORGANIC-TESTS","stem":"Sodium carbonate solution is added to Fe³⁺(aq). What is observed?","options":{"A":"A blue solution and bubbles of oxygen","B":"A green precipitate of iron(III) carbonate","C":"A brown precipitate and bubbles of CO₂","D":"A white precipitate with no gas given off"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"[Fe(H₂O)₆]³⁺ is acidic enough to react with carbonate as an acid, releasing CO₂, so no iron(III) carbonate forms. Instead, brown Fe(H₂O)₃(OH)₃ precipitates. M²⁺ ions are less acidic, so they do form carbonates: Fe²⁺ gives green FeCO₃ and Cu²⁺ gives blue-green CuCO₃, with no fizzing.","reforge":{"stem":"Sodium carbonate solution is added to Cu²⁺(aq). What forms?","options":{"A":"A blue-green precipitate of copper(II) carbonate","B":"A brown precipitate and bubbles of carbon dioxide","C":"A deep blue solution of a copper–ammonia complex","D":"A white precipitate of copper(II) hydroxide"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.6"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-70","tag":"MC-GCHEM-INORGANIC-TESTS","stem":"Aluminium hydroxide dissolves in excess sodium hydroxide because it:","options":{"A":"is a strong base that reacts with water","B":"is reduced by the hydroxide ions","C":"forms a deep blue ammonia complex","D":"is amphoteric, forming [Al(OH)₄]⁻"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"With a little NaOH, Al³⁺(aq) gives a white precipitate of Al(H₂O)₃(OH)₃. With excess, the precipitate acts as an acid and loses another proton, forming the soluble [Al(OH)₄]⁻ ion, so it redissolves to a colourless solution. Iron and copper hydroxides are not amphoteric and stay as precipitates.","reforge":{"stem":"Which metal hydroxide is white and dissolves in excess sodium hydroxide?","options":{"A":"Copper(II) hydroxide","B":"Aluminium hydroxide","C":"Iron(II) hydroxide","D":"Iron(III) hydroxide"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.6"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-71","tag":"MC-GCHEM-INORGANIC-TESTS","stem":"A green precipitate forms when NaOH is added to a solution, and it turns brown on standing in air. Which ion is present?","options":{"A":"Fe²⁺, oxidised in air to iron(III)","B":"Cu²⁺, reduced by the air to copper(I)","C":"Al³⁺, hydrated by water vapour","D":"Fe³⁺, reduced in air to iron(II)"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"Fe²⁺ gives green Fe(H₂O)₄(OH)₂. Oxygen in the air slowly oxidises it to iron(III), so the surface turns orange-brown as Fe(H₂O)₃(OH)₃ forms. Fe³⁺ gives the brown precipitate straight away, Cu²⁺ gives blue, and Al³⁺ gives white. A colour change on standing is the clue that oxidation is happening.","reforge":{"stem":"Ammonia solution is added a drop at a time to Cu²⁺(aq). What is seen first?","options":{"A":"A white precipitate of CuCO₃","B":"A deep blue solution straight away","C":"A pale blue precipitate of Cu(OH)₂","D":"A brown precipitate of Cu(OH)₃"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.6"},{"id":"CHEM-F2-72","tag":"MC-GCHEM-INORGANIC-TESTS","stem":"The hydrolysis [Fe(H₂O)₆]³⁺ + H₂O ⇌ [Fe(H₂O)₅(OH)]²⁺ + H₃O⁺ makes the solution:","options":{"A":"neutral, at pH 7","B":"acidic, about pH 2","C":"alkaline, about pH 12","D":"a buffer near pH 7"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"The equilibrium releases H₃O⁺, so a solution of an Fe³⁺ salt is acidic, around pH 2. Fe²⁺ and Cu²⁺ solutions are only weakly acidic, around pH 5 to 6. This acidity is what lets M³⁺ ions react with carbonate to release CO₂.","reforge":{"stem":"Ammonia added to [Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺ first gives a precipitate. What type of reaction is this first step?","options":{"A":"Redox, as the Cu²⁺ ions are reduced by ammonia","B":"Ligand substitution, as NH₃ replaces all six waters","C":"Precipitation of solid copper(II) nitride","D":"Acid–base, as NH₃ removes H⁺ from water ligands"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.2.6"}]},"CHEM-3":{"label":"Organic Chemistry","color":"#1e3a5f","questions":[{"id":"ORG-01","stem":"A student describes all isomers as molecules with the same molecular formula but different structures. Which type of isomerism is NOT captured by this definition?","options":{"A":"Stereoisomerism","B":"Structural (constitutional) isomerism","C":"Chain isomerism","D":"Positional isomerism"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Structural isomers: same molecular formula, different connectivity (chain, positional, functional group isomers). Stereoisomers: same molecular formula AND same connectivity, but different spatial arrangement. E/Z isomers: restricted rotation around C=C due to pi bond; different groups on same or opposite sides. Optical isomers: chiral carbon (four different groups); non-superimposable mirror images (enantiomers). Both require the same molecular formula AND same structural formula — the student's definition only captures structural isomerism.","reforge":{"stem":"Which molecule can exhibit optical isomerism?","options":{"A":"CH₂Cl₂ — two identical chlorine atoms.","B":"The enthalpy change forming one mole of gas from any liquid under standard conditions in a typical experiment","C":"CHClBrF — the central carbon has four different substituents (H, Cl, Br, F), making it a chiral centre.","D":"CH₃CH₂OH — no carbon with four different groups."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.1"},{"id":"ORG-02","stem":"The mechanism for the reaction between bromine and ethene is electrophilic addition. Why is it addition rather than substitution?","options":{"A":"Because bromine is more electronegative than carbon.","B":"Ethene is unsaturated, so the pi bond opens","C":"Because the reaction occurs at room temperature without UV light.","D":"Because ethene has no hydrogen atoms to be substituted."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-MECHANISMS","scaffold":"Alkenes are unsaturated (C=C double bond contains sigma + pi bond). In addition reactions, the pi bond breaks, both carbons form new bonds to incoming atoms, and the product is saturated (no double bond). Substitution would require replacing an existing atom — but in addition, no atom is lost. Compare with alkanes (saturated): no pi bond available, so bromine reacts by free radical substitution (UV light needed). The unsaturation of alkenes determines the mechanism.","reforge":{"stem":"Why do alkanes undergo free radical substitution with Br₂ while alkenes undergo electrophilic addition?","options":{"A":"Alkanes are more reactive than alkenes — they form stronger C-H bonds.","B":"Alkanes contain oxygen, which enables substitution instead of addition.","C":"Bromine reacts differently with alkanes depending on the temperature used.","D":"Alkanes have no pi bond to attack"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.4"},{"id":"ORG-03","stem":"In the halogenation of benzene (e.g. bromination), why is a halogen carrier (Lewis acid catalyst) required?","options":{"A":"To break the C-H bonds in benzene.","B":"To prevent the reaction from becoming exothermic.","C":"To generate a stronger electrophile","D":"To absorb the HBr produced and shift equilibrium."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-MECHANISMS","scaffold":"Benzene's delocalised pi electrons give it extra stability — electrophiles must be more powerful than for simple alkenes. FeBr₃ (or AlBr₃) reacts with Br₂: Br₂ + FeBr₃ → Br⁺[FeBr₄]⁻. The Br⁺ electrophile attacks the ring. The mechanism is electrophilic substitution (not addition) — the aromatic system is restored after reaction, preserving the delocalisation energy. Addition would destroy aromaticity and is thermodynamically unfavourable.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does benzene undergo electrophilic substitution rather than electrophilic addition (unlike alkenes)?","options":{"A":"Substitution preserves the aromatic stability","B":"Benzene has no pi electrons, so it cannot attract electrophilic reagents.","C":"Benzene molecules are too large geometrically for addition reactions to occur.","D":"Addition would require a Lewis acid catalyst that is simply unavailable for benzene."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.10"},{"id":"ORG-04","stem":"What is the product when ethanol is heated with excess concentrated H₂SO₄ at 170°C?","options":{"A":"Ethanal (an aldehyde)","B":"Diethyl ether","C":"Ethyl hydrogen sulphate","D":"Ethene"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCHEM-FUNCTIONAL-GROUPS","scaffold":"Acid-catalysed elimination (dehydration) of ethanol: concentrated H₂SO₄ at 170°C → ethene + H₂O. At lower temperature (140°C, excess ethanol): intermolecular dehydration → diethyl ether. Temperature determines product. 170°C: intramolecular elimination → alkene. 140°C: intermolecular substitution → ether. Exam trap: students state ether at 170°C or ethene at 140°C. Mechanism at 170°C: protonation of -OH → leaves as H₂O → carbocation → E2 elimination.","reforge":{"stem":"Ethanol is heated with excess concentrated H₂SO₄ at 140°C. What is the product and why different from 170°C?","options":{"A":"Ethene — dehydration occurs at all temperatures above 100°C.","B":"Diethyl ether, from intermolecular condensation","C":"Ethanoic acid — oxidation by sulphuric acid.","D":"Ethyl sulphate — esterification with H₂SO₄."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.5"},{"id":"ORG-05","stem":"Which reagent distinguishes an aldehyde from a ketone?","options":{"A":"Fehling's solution or Tollens' reagent — both oxidise aldehydes (positive test) but not ketones.","B":"Esters are thermally unstable at reaction temperatures, causing decomposition before equilibrium.","C":"Bromine water — both decolourise it.","D":"NaOH — only aldehydes react with sodium hydroxide."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCHEM-FUNCTIONAL-GROUPS","scaffold":"Aldehydes can be oxidised (have a H on the carbonyl carbon); ketones cannot be easily oxidised (no H on carbonyl). Tests: (1) Tollens' (ammoniacal silver nitrate) — aldehyde reduces Ag⁺ to Ag metal (silver mirror); ketone no reaction. (2) Fehling's — aldehyde reduces Cu²⁺ to Cu₂O (brick-red precipitate); ketone no reaction. (3) Acidified K₂Cr₂O₇: aldehydes oxidised (orange → green); ketones are NOT oxidised under normal conditions — this does NOT distinguish them cleanly.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can Tollens' reagent distinguish an aldehyde from a ketone, but acidified K₂Cr₂O₇ is less reliable for this purpose?","options":{"A":"Tollens' reagent is more concentrated, which is why it can react with ketones.","B":"K₂Cr₂O₇ reacts with both aldehydes and ketones but at visibly different rates.","C":"Tollens' and Fehling's oxidise only aldehydes","D":"Only Tollens' reagent produces a colour change; K₂Cr₂O₇ does not change colour at all."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.8"},{"id":"ORG-06","stem":"A carboxylic acid reacts with an alcohol in the presence of an acid catalyst. What type of reaction is this, and what functional group is produced?","options":{"A":"Addition; produces a hemiacetal","B":"Esterification (condensation); produces an ester and water","C":"Ethene — dehydration occurs at all temperatures above 100°C.","D":"Elimination; produces an alkene and water"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCHEM-FUNCTIONAL-GROUPS","scaffold":"Esterification: RCOOH + R'OH ⇌ RCOOR' + H₂O. Acid catalyst (conc. H₂SO₄) speeds up the equilibrium. Condensation reaction — two molecules join with loss of a small molecule (H₂O). The ester functional group is -COO-. Reversible: excess acid or alcohol shifts equilibrium right; water removal (Dean-Stark) drives to completion. Products: ester + water. Students confuse esterification with amide formation (acyl chloride + amine → amide + HCl).","reforge":{"stem":"Why does esterification reach an equilibrium rather than going to completion?","options":{"A":"The acid catalyst is consumed during esterification, reducing the yield.","B":"Esters are thermally unstable at reaction temperatures, causing decomposition before equilibrium.","C":"The reaction requires UV light to proceed beyond the equilibrium point.","D":"Esterification is reversible, so yield is limited"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.9"},{"id":"ORG-07","stem":"In free radical substitution of methane with chlorine, which step produces the chain-carrying radicals that propagate the reaction?","options":{"A":"Termination — two radicals combine","B":"Alkanes are more reactive than alkenes — they form stronger C-H bonds.","C":"Initiation — UV light causes homolytic fission of Cl₂ to give 2Cl•","D":"Substitution — CH₃• replaces H in CH₄"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-MECHANISMS","scaffold":"Free radical substitution mechanism — three stages: (1) Initiation: Cl₂ + UV → 2Cl• (homolytic fission — each atom takes one electron from the shared pair); (2) Propagation: Cl• + CH₄ → HCl + CH₃•; then CH₃• + Cl₂ → CH₃Cl + Cl•. Radicals are regenerated — the chain continues; (3) Termination: two radicals combine (Cl•+Cl•, CH₃•+Cl•, CH₃•+CH₃•) — radicals consumed, chain ends. Initiation creates the first radicals. A common error: stating propagation creates the initial radicals.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does free radical substitution typically produce a mixture of products?","options":{"A":"CH₃Cl can substitute again, giving CH₂Cl₂ and CHCl₃","B":"The UV light selectively breaks different bonds each time.","C":"Chlorine radicals react randomly with any molecule in the mixture","D":"The reaction is reversible, regenerating methane and chlorine."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.2"},{"id":"ORG-08","stem":"Which option correctly explains why amines are basic?","options":{"A":"Amines contain nitrogen which is always positively charged.","B":"Amines react with water to produce OH⁻ ions directly.","C":"Amines are basic because they are derived from ammonia which is a strong base.","D":"The nitrogen lone pair accepts a proton"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCHEM-SYNTHESIS","scaffold":"Amines (R-NH₂): nitrogen has a lone pair of electrons available to donate. As a Brønsted-Lowry base: R-NH₂ + H⁺ → R-NH₃⁺ (proton accepted). In water: R-NH₂ + H₂O ⇌ R-NH₃⁺ + OH⁻ (weak base). Basicity increases with electron-donating alkyl groups (which increase electron density on N, making the lone pair more available). Aromatic amines (aniline) are weaker bases — the lone pair is delocalised into the benzene ring, reducing availability. Ammonia is a weak base (Kb = 1.8×10⁻⁵), not a strong base.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is aniline (C₆H₅NH₂) a weaker base than methylamine (CH₃NH₂)?","options":{"A":"Aniline has more hydrogen atoms, making it harder to accept a proton","B":"The nitrogen lone pair is delocalised into the ring","C":"The benzene ring in aniline physically blocks access to the nitrogen.","D":"Methylamine is not actually more basic than aniline."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.11"},{"id":"CHEM-E3-01","spec":"CHEM-3","stem":"An alkene is identified by the presence of:","options":{"A":"a carbon-carbon double bond","B":"Because bromine is more electronegative than carbon.","C":"a benzene ring only","D":"an ionic bond"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-MECHANISMS","scaffold":"The C=C bond is the characteristic functional group of alkenes.","reforge":{"stem":"A hydrocarbon decolourises bromine water rapidly at room temperature. What functional group does this indicate?","options":{"A":"A carbon-carbon double bond, as found in an alkene","B":"A hydroxyl group alone under standard conditions in a typical experiment","C":"A benzene ring alone","D":"An ionic bond"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.4"},{"id":"CHEM-E3-02","spec":"CHEM-3","stem":"Bromine water tests for:","options":{"A":"a carboxylic acid","B":"an alkene's carbon-carbon double bond","C":"an amine salt","D":"Because the reaction occurs at room temperature without UV light."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-MECHANISMS","scaffold":"The orange bromine solution is decolourised by addition across C=C.","reforge":{"stem":"A few drops of bromine water are shaken with an unknown compound and the orange colour disappears immediately. What does this confirm?","options":{"A":"The compound is a carboxylic acid","B":"The compound contains a carbon-carbon double bond","C":"The compound is an amine salt","D":"The compound contains a transition-metal ion under standard conditions"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.6"},{"id":"CHEM-E3-03","spec":"CHEM-3","stem":"A nucleophile is a species that:","options":{"A":"accepts a pair of electrons","B":"loses a neutron","C":"donates a pair of electrons","D":"always contains a metal"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-MECHANISMS","scaffold":"Nucleophiles attack electron-deficient centres using a lone pair or negative charge.","reforge":{"stem":"A species attacks an electron-deficient carbon atom by donating a lone pair of electrons to form a new bond. What is this species called?","options":{"A":"A species that accepts a pair of electrons","B":"A species that loses a neutron","C":"A nucleophile","D":"A species that must contain a metal"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.3"},{"id":"CHEM-E3-04","spec":"CHEM-3","stem":"Ethanol can be oxidised to:","options":{"A":"ethene only in every condition","B":"a ketone directly","C":"an amine","D":"ethanal and then ethanoic acid"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCHEM-FUNCTIONAL-GROUPS","scaffold":"Primary alcohol oxidation depends on conditions and distillation or reflux.","reforge":{"stem":"Ethanol is heated under reflux with an excess of acidified potassium dichromate. What is the final organic product?","options":{"A":"Ethanoic acid, via the intermediate ethanal","B":"Ethene, under every condition under standard conditions","C":"A ketone, formed directly","D":"An amine"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.5"},{"id":"CHEM-E3-05","spec":"CHEM-3","stem":"Aldehydes give a positive Tollens' test by forming:","options":{"A":"a silver mirror","B":"a blue precipitate","C":"a green gas","D":"an orange solution"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCHEM-FUNCTIONAL-GROUPS","scaffold":"Aldehydes reduce silver ions in Tollens' reagent; ketones generally do not.","reforge":{"stem":"An unknown organic compound is warmed with Tollens' reagent, and a silvery coating forms inside the test tube. What does this confirm?","options":{"A":"The compound forms a blue precipitate","B":"The compound is an aldehyde","C":"The compound releases a green gas","D":"The compound forms an orange solution"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.8"},{"id":"CHEM-E3-06","spec":"CHEM-3","stem":"Carboxylic acids react with carbonates to produce:","options":{"A":"Ethanoic acid — oxidation by sulphuric acid.","B":"a salt, water and carbon dioxide","C":"only water","D":"a polymer and oxygen"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCHEM-FUNCTIONAL-GROUPS","scaffold":"The gas evolved is carbon dioxide, providing a useful qualitative test.","reforge":{"stem":"Ethanoic acid is added to solid sodium carbonate. What products are formed in this reaction?","options":{"A":"An aldehyde and hydrogen gas under standard conditions","B":"Only water","C":"A salt, water and carbon dioxide gas","D":"A polymer and oxygen gas"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.9"},{"id":"CHEM-E3-07","spec":"CHEM-3","stem":"Benzene is unusually stable because it has:","options":{"A":"Because ethene has no hydrogen atoms to be substituted.","B":"an ionic lattice","C":"delocalised electrons in a planar ring","D":"no pi electrons"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-MECHANISMS","scaffold":"Delocalisation makes addition less favourable than substitution.","reforge":{"stem":"Benzene's carbon-carbon bond lengths are all equal and shorter than a typical single bond. What explains this unusual stability?","options":{"A":"A ring of delocalised pi electrons spread across the planar structure","B":"Six isolated single bonds with no delocalisation under standard conditions","C":"An ionic lattice structure","D":"The complete absence of pi electrons"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.10"},{"id":"CHEM-E3-08","spec":"CHEM-3","stem":"A condensation polymer forms when monomers join and:","options":{"A":"oxygen is always added","B":"all double bonds remain unchanged","C":"CH₃CHClCH₃ — the central carbon has two identical methyl groups.","D":"a small molecule such as water is eliminated"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Polyesters and polyamides are common condensation polymers.","reforge":{"stem":"Two monomers join to form a polyamide, releasing a small water molecule at each new bond formed. What type of polymerisation is this?","options":{"A":"A process in which oxygen is always added","B":"Condensation polymerisation, eliminating a small molecule","C":"A process in which all double bonds remain unchanged","D":"A process in which the polymer breaks back into monomers under standard conditions"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.12"},{"id":"CHEM-E3-09","spec":"CHEM-3","stem":"An amino acid contains:","options":{"A":"an amino group and a carboxyl group","B":"only an alkene group","C":"Structural (constitutional) isomerism","D":"a metal ion and a hydroxide"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Amino acids can form zwitterions and peptide bonds.","reforge":{"stem":"Glycine has the general structure H2N-CH2-COOH. Which two functional groups does every amino acid contain?","options":{"A":"Only an alkene group","B":"A halogen and a ketone only under standard conditions","C":"An amino group and a carboxyl group","D":"A metal ion and a hydroxide"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.13"},{"id":"CHEM-E3-10","spec":"CHEM-3","stem":"In proton NMR, chemical shift indicates:","options":{"A":"The colour of light emitted when atoms are heated.","B":"the electronic environment of hydrogen nuclei","C":"the concentration of oxygen","D":"the number of carbon atoms only"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCHEM-MASS-SPECTRA","scaffold":"Different environments absorb at different chemical shifts.","reforge":{"stem":"In a proton NMR spectrum, hydrogen atoms in different chemical environments absorb at different frequencies. What does the chemical shift of a peak reveal?","options":{"A":"The electronic environment of the hydrogen nuclei producing it","B":"The overall mass of the molecule","C":"The concentration of oxygen present under standard conditions in a typical experiment","D":"The number of carbon atoms only"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.15"},{"id":"CHEM-E3-11","spec":"CHEM-3","stem":"In chromatography, the Rf value is:","options":{"A":"mass divided by volume","B":"The concentration of oxygen present under standard conditions in a typical experiment","C":"distance travelled by solute divided by distance travelled by solvent front","D":"the peak area divided by pressure"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCHEM-MASS-SPECTRA","scaffold":"Rf values can help identify substances under the same conditions.","reforge":{"stem":"On a chromatogram, a spot travels 4 cm while the solvent front travels 8 cm from the same baseline. What does the ratio of these distances give?","options":{"A":"The mass of the spot divided by its volume","B":"The Rf value of the spot","C":"The solvent volume divided by the time taken","D":"The peak area divided by applied pressure"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.16"},{"id":"CHEM-E3-12","spec":"CHEM-3","stem":"A halogenoalkane undergoes hydrolysis with aqueous hydroxide to form:","options":{"A":"an alkene only","B":"a carboxylic acid","C":"an amide","D":"an alcohol"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCHEM-FUNCTIONAL-GROUPS","scaffold":"The hydroxide ion substitutes for the halogen in suitable conditions.","reforge":{"stem":"1-bromobutane is heated under reflux with aqueous sodium hydroxide. What organic product forms?","options":{"A":"Only an alkene","B":"A carboxylic acid under standard conditions","C":"Butan-1-ol, an alcohol","D":"An amide"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.3"},{"id":"CHEM-E3-13","spec":"CHEM-3","stem":"The major product of addition of HBr to an unsymmetrical alkene is predicted using:","options":{"A":"the stability of the intermediate carbocation","B":"Acidified potassium dichromate — both aldehydes and ketones are oxidised.","C":"the number of oxygen atoms","D":"the pH of pure water"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCHEM-FUNCTIONAL-GROUPS","scaffold":"Regioselectivity follows the more stable carbocation pathway in the usual mechanism.","reforge":{"stem":"HBr adds across an unsymmetrical alkene, and one product forms in much greater yield than the other. What determines which product predominates?","options":{"A":"The relative stability of the carbocation intermediate formed","B":"The melting point of bromine","C":"The number of oxygen atoms present under standard conditions in a typical experiment","D":"The pH of pure water"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.4"},{"id":"CHEM-E3-14","spec":"CHEM-3","stem":"Optical isomers are compounds that:","options":{"A":"CH₂Cl₂ — two identical chlorine atoms.","B":"are non-superimposable mirror images","C":"contain no carbon","D":"are always structural isomers"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Chiral molecules lack a plane of symmetry and form enantiomers.","reforge":{"stem":"Two forms of the same drug molecule are found to be non-superimposable mirror images of one another. What are these two forms called?","options":{"A":"Compounds with different molecular formulae","B":"Optical isomers","C":"Molecules containing no carbon at all","D":"Structural isomers of each other"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.7"},{"id":"CHEM-E3-15","spec":"CHEM-3","stem":"A peptide bond forms between:","options":{"A":"two alkene groups","B":"two halogen atoms","C":"the amino group of one amino acid and the carboxyl group of another","D":"A process in which the polymer breaks back into monomers under standard conditions"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Condensation forms an amide link and releases water.","reforge":{"stem":"During protein synthesis, the carboxyl group of one amino acid reacts with the amino group of the next. What type of bond forms between them?","options":{"A":"A bond between two alkene groups","B":"A bond between two halogen atoms","C":"A peptide bond","D":"A bond between a hydroxyl group and a metal ion"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.13"},{"id":"CHEM-E3-16","spec":"CHEM-3","stem":"A primary amine contains nitrogen bonded to:","options":{"A":"three carbonyl groups","B":"NaOH — only aldehydes react with sodium hydroxide.","C":"a benzene ring only","D":"one carbon-containing group and two hydrogens"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCHEM-FUNCTIONAL-GROUPS","scaffold":"Primary, secondary and tertiary refer to the number of carbon groups attached to nitrogen.","reforge":{"stem":"Methylamine, CH3NH2, has nitrogen bonded to one carbon-containing group and two hydrogen atoms. What class of amine does this make it?","options":{"A":"A primary amine","B":"An amine bonded to three carbonyl groups","C":"An amine bonded to one oxygen and no hydrogen","D":"An amine containing only a benzene ring"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.11"},{"id":"CHEM-E3-17","spec":"CHEM-3","stem":"Hydrolysis of an ester produces:","options":{"A":"an alcohol and a carboxylic acid or its salt","B":"two alkenes","C":"Tollens' reagent is more concentrated, which is why it can react with ketones.","D":"only carbon dioxide"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCHEM-FUNCTIONAL-GROUPS","scaffold":"Acid or base hydrolysis breaks the ester link in different conditions.","reforge":{"stem":"An ester is heated under reflux with dilute sulfuric acid. What products does this hydrolysis typically give?","options":{"A":"Two separate alkene molecules under standard conditions","B":"An alcohol and a carboxylic acid (or its salt)","C":"An amine and an aldehyde","D":"Only carbon dioxide gas"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.9"},{"id":"CHEM-E3-18","spec":"CHEM-3","stem":"A polymer's repeating unit is found by:","options":{"A":"They have different boiling points, melting points and densities","B":"identifying the structural unit repeated along its chain","C":"removing every carbon atom","D":"counting only the end groups"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"The repeat unit represents the regular pattern in the polymer structure.","reforge":{"stem":"A long polymer chain is examined to find the smallest structural unit that repeats along its length. What is this unit called?","options":{"A":"The sum of all monomer masses with no bonds counted","B":"A unit found by removing every carbon atom","C":"The repeating unit of the polymer","D":"A unit found by counting only the end groups"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.12"},{"id":"CHEM-E3B-01","spec":"CHEM-3","stem":"A condensation reaction between two amino acids forms:","options":{"A":"a halogenoalkane and oxygen","B":"an alkene and hydrogen","C":"a peptide bond and water","D":"only carbon dioxide"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"The amino and carboxyl groups react to form an amide link, releasing water.","reforge":{"stem":"Two amino acid molecules react together, forming a new C-N bond and releasing a small molecule. What is eliminated in this condensation reaction?","options":{"A":"A water molecule, as a peptide bond forms","B":"A halogenoalkane and oxygen under standard conditions","C":"An alkene and hydrogen gas","D":"Only carbon dioxide"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.13"},{"id":"CHEM-E4C-01","spec":"CHEM-3","stem":"In the free-radical substitution of methane, initiation produces:","options":{"A":"a polymer by condensation","B":"methanol by hydrolysis","C":"Alkanes contain oxygen, which enables substitution instead of addition.","D":"chlorine radicals when Cl₂ undergoes homolytic fission"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-MECHANISMS","scaffold":"Ultraviolet radiation breaks the chlorine molecule's covalent bond into radicals.","reforge":{"stem":"UV light shines on a mixture of methane and chlorine, causing the Cl-Cl bond to split symmetrically. What does this initiation step produce?","options":{"A":"A polymer, formed by condensation","B":"Two chlorine radicals, formed by homolytic fission","C":"Methanol, formed by hydrolysis","D":"A carbocation, formed directly from methane under standard conditions"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.2"},{"id":"CHEM-E4C-02","spec":"CHEM-3","stem":"An alkane is relatively unreactive because it contains:","options":{"A":"strong, non-polar C–C and C–H sigma bonds","B":"Bromine reacts differently with alkanes depending on the temperature used.","C":"an ionic lattice","D":"a carbonyl group"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-MECHANISMS","scaffold":"Alkanes lack the polar or pi bonds that provide common reaction sites.","reforge":{"stem":"Alkanes undergo very few reactions compared with alkenes at room temperature. What structural feature explains this low reactivity?","options":{"A":"A reactive carbon-carbon double bond","B":"An ionic lattice structure","C":"Strong, non-polar C-C and C-H sigma bonds throughout the molecule","D":"A carbonyl group present in the chain under standard conditions in a typical experiment"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.2"},{"id":"CHEM-E4C-03","spec":"CHEM-3","stem":"A suitable reagent for converting an alkane into a halogenoalkane is:","options":{"A":"aqueous sodium hydroxide at room temperature","B":"chlorine under ultraviolet light","C":"acidified potassium manganate(VII)","D":"Tollens' reagent"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-MECHANISMS","scaffold":"Ultraviolet light initiates free-radical substitution with chlorine.","reforge":{"stem":"Which reagent and condition would convert an alkane into a halogenoalkane?","options":{"A":"Chlorine gas under ultraviolet light","B":"Aqueous sodium hydroxide at room temperature","C":"Acidified potassium manganate(VII)","D":"Tollens' reagent"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.2"},{"id":"CHEM-E4C-04","spec":"CHEM-3","stem":"In a synthesis pathway, retrosynthesis means:","options":{"A":"heating every intermediate until it boils","B":"Amines are basic because they are derived from ammonia which is a strong base.","C":"working backwards from the target molecule to suitable starting materials","D":"removing all functional groups first"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCHEM-SYNTHESIS","scaffold":"Retrosynthetic analysis identifies practical disconnections and reagents.","reforge":{"stem":"A chemist plans a multi-step synthesis by starting from the target molecule and working out simpler starting materials that could produce it. What is this approach called?","options":{"A":"Heating every intermediate until it boils","B":"Retrosynthesis, working backwards from the target","C":"Measuring the rate without changing any conditions","D":"Removing all functional groups before starting"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.14"},{"id":"CHEM-E4C-05","spec":"CHEM-3","stem":"A good organic synthesis should be judged partly by:","options":{"A":"the mass of solvent discarded only","B":"the number of steps alone","C":"Amines contain nitrogen which is always positively charged.","D":"yield, purity, atom economy and manageable conditions"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCHEM-SYNTHESIS","scaffold":"Several criteria help compare an efficient and practical route.","reforge":{"stem":"Beyond simply forming the desired product, what should a good organic synthesis route also be judged on?","options":{"A":"Only the mass of solvent discarded","B":"Only the total number of steps involved","C":"Its yield, purity, atom economy and how manageable the conditions are","D":"Whether every intermediate happens to be coloured under standard conditions"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.14"},{"id":"CHEM-E4C-06","spec":"CHEM-3","stem":"Which sequence is a plausible route from an alcohol to a carboxylic acid?","options":{"A":"oxidise a primary alcohol under suitable conditions","B":"K₂Cr₂O₇ reacts with both aldehydes and ketones but at visibly different rates.","C":"add bromine water to produce an amine","D":"hydrolyse it with silver nitrate"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCHEM-FUNCTIONAL-GROUPS","scaffold":"Primary alcohols can be oxidised first to an aldehyde and then to a carboxylic acid.","reforge":{"stem":"Which sequence of steps would convert a primary alcohol into a carboxylic acid?","options":{"A":"Oxidise the primary alcohol under suitable conditions","B":"Reduce the alcohol with sodium borohydride under standard conditions","C":"Add bromine water to produce an amine","D":"Hydrolyse it using silver nitrate"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.14"},{"id":"CHEM-E4C-07","spec":"CHEM-3","stem":"A Grignard reagent is useful in synthesis because it can:","options":{"A":"act only as a chromatography solvent","B":"form a new carbon-carbon bond with a carbonyl compound","C":"remove every proton from an alkane","D":"Only Tollens' reagent produces a colour change; K₂Cr₂O₇ does not change colour at all."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCHEM-FUNCTIONAL-GROUPS","scaffold":"Grignard reagents add carbon groups to carbonyl compounds before hydrolysis.","reforge":{"stem":"A Grignard reagent is added to a carbonyl compound during an organic synthesis. What is its main use in this reaction?","options":{"A":"It acts only as a chromatography solvent","B":"It forms a new carbon-carbon bond with the carbonyl compound","C":"It removes every proton from an alkane","D":"It converts benzene directly into an amino acid under standard conditions"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.14"},{"id":"CHEM-E4C-08","spec":"CHEM-3","stem":"A reaction scheme is more convincing when it includes:","options":{"A":"only the product name","B":"Aniline has more hydrogen atoms, making it harder to accept a proton.","C":"reagents, conditions, structures and the reaction type","D":"a yield without any method"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCHEM-SYNTHESIS","scaffold":"A complete scheme shows how the transformation can be carried out and checked.","reforge":{"stem":"A student writes up a multi-step reaction scheme for an exam. What should a fully convincing scheme include?","options":{"A":"Only the name of the final product","B":"The colour of the laboratory apparatus used under standard conditions","C":"The reagents, conditions, structures and reaction type at each step","D":"A yield with no method described"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.14"},{"id":"CHEM-N3-01","stem":"Members of a homologous series always share the same:","options":{"A":"Relative molecular mass.","B":"Number of carbon atoms.","C":"Boiling point and density.","D":"General formula and group"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"A homologous series shares a general formula and functional group, with successive members differing by CH₂. Chemical properties are similar because the functional group governs reactivity, while physical properties change gradually as chain length grows — boiling point rises with stronger London forces. Alkanes are CₙH₂ₙ₊₂, alkenes CₙH₂ₙ, alcohols CₙH₂ₙ₊₁OH, and carboxylic acids CₙH₂ₙ₊₁COOH.","reforge":{"stem":"Boiling point increases along a homologous series because longer molecules have:","options":{"A":"Stronger covalent bonds in the chain.","B":"Stronger London forces","C":"More hydrogen bonding in every case.","D":"A different functional group present."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.1"},{"id":"CHEM-N3-02","stem":"Structural isomers are compounds with the same:","options":{"A":"Molecular formula but different structures","B":"Structure but different molecular formulae.","C":"Empirical formula and identical structure.","D":"Boiling point but different reactivity."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Structural isomers share a molecular formula but differ in atom connectivity: chain isomers (branching), position isomers (functional group location) and functional group isomers (different group entirely, as with propanal and propanone). Stereoisomers share connectivity but differ in spatial arrangement — E/Z isomerism from restricted rotation about a C=C, and optical isomerism from a chiral carbon carrying four different groups.","reforge":{"stem":"Propanal and propanone are examples of:","options":{"A":"Chain isomers differing in branching.","B":"Optical isomers of one another.","C":"Functional group isomers","D":"E/Z isomers about a double bond."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.1"},{"id":"CHEM-N3-03","stem":"E/Z isomerism can occur about a carbon–carbon double bond because:","options":{"A":"The bond is longer than a single bond.","B":"Rotation about the bond is restricted","C":"The carbons carry a permanent charge.","D":"Double bonds are always polar."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"A C=C consists of a sigma bond plus a pi bond formed by sideways overlap of p orbitals; breaking the pi bond to rotate costs too much energy at ordinary temperatures, so groups are locked in position. E/Z isomerism additionally requires each double-bond carbon to carry two different groups. Priority is assigned by atomic number: Z has the higher-priority groups on the same side, E on opposite sides.","reforge":{"stem":"E/Z isomerism is impossible when one carbon of the C=C carries:","options":{"A":"A hydroxyl and a methyl group.","B":"Two different alkyl groups.","C":"A halogen and a hydrogen.","D":"Two identical groups"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.1"},{"id":"CHEM-N3-04","stem":"A chiral carbon atom is one that is bonded to:","options":{"A":"Two identical groups and two different ones.","B":"Three groups and one lone pair.","C":"Four different groups","D":"A double bond and two other groups."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"A chiral centre carries four different groups, giving two non-superimposable mirror images called enantiomers. These have identical physical and chemical properties in an achiral environment but rotate plane-polarised light in opposite directions, and can behave very differently in biological systems, which are themselves chiral. A racemic mixture contains equal amounts of both and shows no net optical rotation.","reforge":{"stem":"A racemic mixture shows no optical activity because it contains:","options":{"A":"Equal amounts of both enantiomers","B":"No chiral centres at any position.","C":"Only the E isomer of the compound.","D":"Molecules with a plane of symmetry."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.7"},{"id":"CHEM-N3-05","stem":"The complete combustion of an alkane produces:","options":{"A":"Carbon monoxide and water only.","B":"Carbon and hydrogen gas.","C":"Carbon dioxide and hydrogen.","D":"Carbon dioxide and water"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCHEM-FUNCTIONAL-GROUPS","scaffold":"Complete combustion in excess oxygen gives carbon dioxide and water. Incomplete combustion in limited oxygen gives carbon monoxide — toxic because it binds irreversibly to haemoglobin — and carbon particulates, which cause respiratory harm and global dimming. Sulfur impurities burn to sulfur dioxide, causing acid rain; and at high engine temperatures nitrogen and oxygen combine to form nitrogen oxides.","reforge":{"stem":"Nitrogen oxides form in a car engine because the high temperature causes:","options":{"A":"Sulfur impurities in the fuel to burn.","B":"Nitrogen and oxygen from air to react","C":"Incomplete combustion of the hydrocarbon.","D":"The catalytic converter to break down."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.2"},{"id":"CHEM-N3-06","stem":"The reaction of methane with chlorine in ultraviolet light proceeds by:","options":{"A":"Free radical substitution","B":"Electrophilic addition.","C":"Nucleophilic substitution.","D":"Elimination of hydrogen chloride."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-MECHANISMS","scaffold":"Free radical substitution has three stages. Initiation: UV light homolytically splits Cl₂ into two chlorine radicals. Propagation: Cl• + CH₄ → •CH₃ + HCl, then •CH₃ + Cl₂ → CH₃Cl + Cl•, so one initiation event causes many cycles. Termination: two radicals combine. The mechanism gives a mixture of mono-, di- and further substituted products plus ethane, which limits its synthetic usefulness.","reforge":{"stem":"Free radical substitution is of limited use in synthesis because it produces:","options":{"A":"Only a single pure product.","B":"No reaction without a catalyst.","C":"A mixture of substitution products","D":"Exclusively ethane as the product."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.2"},{"id":"CHEM-N3-07","stem":"Alkenes readily undergo addition reactions because the C=C bond has:","options":{"A":"A permanent positive charge.","B":"High electron density","C":"An unusually long bond length.","D":"No pi bond to be broken."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-MECHANISMS","scaffold":"The pi bond is an exposed region of high electron density above and below the plane, so it attracts electrophiles — electron pair acceptors. In electrophilic addition to an alkene, the pi electrons attack the electrophile, forming a carbocation intermediate which is then attacked by a nucleophile. With HBr and an unsymmetrical alkene, Markovnikov's rule applies: the major product forms via the more stable carbocation.","reforge":{"stem":"Adding HBr to propene gives mainly 2-bromopropane because the intermediate formed is a:","options":{"A":"Primary carbocation, which is more stable.","B":"Free radical rather than an ion.","C":"Tertiary carbocation with three alkyl groups.","D":"Secondary carbocation, which is more stable"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.4"},{"id":"CHEM-N3-08","stem":"Bromine water is decolourised by an alkene because the alkene undergoes:","options":{"A":"Combustion in the presence of bromine.","B":"Free radical substitution of a hydrogen.","C":"Electrophilic addition across C=C","D":"Oxidation to a carboxylic acid."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCHEM-FUNCTIONAL-GROUPS","scaffold":"Bromine water is the standard test for unsaturation: orange bromine water is decolourised as bromine adds across the C=C. An alkane produces no change without UV light. The bromine molecule is polarised as it approaches the electron-rich pi bond, becoming an induced dipole, and the resulting Br⁺ acts as the electrophile. Other alkene tests include decolourising acidified potassium manganate(VII), which forms a diol.","reforge":{"stem":"Bromine becomes an electrophile as it approaches an alkene because the pi electrons:","options":{"A":"Induce a dipole in the Br₂ molecule","B":"Form a covalent bond with both atoms.","C":"Are transferred completely to bromine.","D":"Remove one electron from bromine."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.6"},{"id":"CHEM-N3-09","stem":"Addition polymerisation of alkenes produces a polymer with:","options":{"A":"Water eliminated at each linkage.","B":"Alternating single and double bonds.","C":"Two different monomer units required.","D":"The monomer empirical formula"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCHEM-SYNTHESIS","scaffold":"Addition polymerisation joins alkene monomers by opening the C=C, with no other product formed — so atom economy is 100% and the repeat unit has the same empirical formula as the monomer. Condensation polymerisation instead loses a small molecule, usually water or HCl, at each linkage, and needs monomers with two functional groups: diols with dicarboxylic acids for polyesters, diamines for polyamides.","reforge":{"stem":"Polyesters are formed by condensation polymerisation, which differs from addition because it:","options":{"A":"Requires only one type of monomer.","B":"Eliminates a small molecule","C":"Has an atom economy of 100%.","D":"Opens a carbon–carbon double bond."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.12"},{"id":"CHEM-N3-10","stem":"The reaction of a haloalkane with aqueous sodium hydroxide produces an alcohol by:","options":{"A":"Nucleophilic substitution","B":"Free radical substitution.","C":"Electrophilic addition.","D":"Elimination of a hydrogen halide."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-MECHANISMS","scaffold":"The carbon–halogen bond is polar, with carbon δ+, so it is attacked by nucleophiles: OH⁻ gives an alcohol, CN⁻ gives a nitrile (extending the carbon chain by one), and ammonia gives an amine. Conditions decide the outcome with hydroxide: aqueous solution favours substitution to the alcohol, whereas hot ethanolic hydroxide favours elimination to form an alkene.","reforge":{"stem":"Hot ethanolic sodium hydroxide reacting with a haloalkane gives mainly:","options":{"A":"An alcohol, by substitution.","B":"A nitrile, by chain extension.","C":"An alkene, by elimination","D":"An amine, by addition of ammonia."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.3"},{"id":"CHEM-N3-11","stem":"The rate of hydrolysis of haloalkanes increases from chloro to iodo because the carbon–halogen bond:","options":{"A":"Becomes more polar down the group.","B":"Becomes weaker down the group","C":"Increases in strength down the group.","D":"Changes from covalent to ionic."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-MECHANISMS","scaffold":"Bond enthalpy falls from C–F through C–Cl and C–Br to C–I, so the C–I bond breaks most readily and iodoalkanes hydrolyse fastest. This outweighs bond polarity, which runs the opposite way — C–F is by far the most polar yet fluoroalkanes are the least reactive. Rates are compared by warming each haloalkane with silver nitrate in ethanol and timing the appearance of the silver halide precipitate.","reforge":{"stem":"Bond polarity would predict fluoroalkanes to hydrolyse fastest, yet they are slowest. This shows that:","options":{"A":"Polarity is the dominant factor throughout.","B":"Fluorine forms no bond with carbon.","C":"The reaction is not a substitution.","D":"Bond enthalpy is the controlling factor"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.3"},{"id":"CHEM-N3-12","stem":"Oxidation of a primary alcohol with acidified potassium dichromate under distillation gives:","options":{"A":"A ketone, collected as it forms.","B":"A carboxylic acid, after full reflux.","C":"An aldehyde, distilled off as formed","D":"An alkene by dehydration."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCHEM-FUNCTIONAL-GROUPS","scaffold":"Primary alcohols oxidise first to an aldehyde and then to a carboxylic acid: distilling the aldehyde off as it forms stops the reaction there, while heating under reflux with excess oxidising agent gives the acid. Secondary alcohols oxidise to ketones and go no further. Tertiary alcohols are not oxidised, since there is no hydrogen on the carbon bearing the OH group. The dichromate turns from orange to green when oxidation occurs.","reforge":{"stem":"A tertiary alcohol is not oxidised by acidified dichromate because the carbon bearing the OH group has:","options":{"A":"No hydrogen atom attached","B":"Too many hydrogen atoms attached.","C":"A double bond to oxygen already.","D":"A permanent positive charge."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.5"},{"id":"CHEM-N3-13","stem":"A student must confirm that a carbonyl compound is an aldehyde rather than a ketone. The best reagent is:","options":{"A":"Bromine water, decolourised by aldehydes.","B":"Universal indicator, turning red with ketones.","C":"Sodium carbonate, fizzing with aldehydes.","D":"Tollens' reagent, giving a silver mirror"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCHEM-INORGANIC-TESTS","scaffold":"Aldehydes are readily oxidised to carboxylic acids, ketones are not. Tollens' reagent gives a silver mirror with an aldehyde only; Fehling's or Benedict's solution turns from blue to a brick-red precipitate with an aliphatic aldehyde. Both carbonyls give an orange precipitate with 2,4-dinitrophenylhydrazine, confirming C=O but not distinguishing the two — so the pair of tests is used together.","reforge":{"stem":"An orange precipitate with 2,4-DNPH confirms the presence of:","options":{"A":"A carbon–carbon double bond.","B":"A carbonyl group","C":"A hydroxyl group only.","D":"A carboxylic acid group."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.8"},{"id":"CHEM-N3-14","stem":"Carboxylic acids react with sodium carbonate to produce:","options":{"A":"Carbon dioxide, water, salt","B":"Hydrogen gas and a salt only.","C":"An ester and water.","D":"An alcohol and carbon monoxide."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCHEM-FUNCTIONAL-GROUPS","scaffold":"Carboxylic acids are weak acids that still show typical acid reactions: effervescence with carbonates giving CO₂, and salt formation with metals, metal oxides and alkalis. The fizz with sodium carbonate distinguishes a carboxylic acid from a phenol or alcohol, neither of which reacts. Esterification with an alcohol requires a concentrated sulfuric acid catalyst and gives a sweet-smelling ester plus water.","reforge":{"stem":"Effervescence with sodium carbonate distinguishes a carboxylic acid from:","options":{"A":"Another carboxylic acid of longer chain.","B":"A metal carbonate in solution.","C":"An alcohol, which does not react","D":"An inorganic strong acid."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.9"},{"id":"CHEM-N3-15","stem":"Esterification of a carboxylic acid with an alcohol requires:","options":{"A":"Aqueous sodium hydroxide at room temperature.","B":"A concentrated sulfuric acid catalyst","C":"Ultraviolet light and chlorine gas.","D":"Acidified potassium dichromate under reflux."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCHEM-SYNTHESIS","scaffold":"Ester formation is a reversible condensation catalysed by concentrated sulfuric acid, producing an ester and water; the acid also removes water, shifting equilibrium towards the product. Esters are sweet-smelling and used as flavourings, perfumes and solvents. Hydrolysis reverses it: acid hydrolysis is reversible and gives the acid and alcohol, while alkaline hydrolysis is irreversible, giving the carboxylate salt and alcohol.","reforge":{"stem":"Alkaline hydrolysis of an ester goes to completion, unlike acid hydrolysis, because it produces:","options":{"A":"A catalyst that is regenerated.","B":"Water as one of the products.","C":"An alcohol of shorter chain length.","D":"The carboxylate salt, not the acid"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.9"},{"id":"CHEM-N3-16","stem":"Fractional distillation of crude oil separates hydrocarbons according to differences in their:","options":{"A":"Chemical reactivity with oxygen.","B":"Density in the liquid state.","C":"Boiling point, from chain length","D":"Colour when heated in the column."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCHEM-SYNTHESIS","scaffold":"Crude oil is separated by fractional distillation: it is vaporised and fed into a column with a temperature gradient, so each fraction condenses where the temperature matches its boiling range. Longer chains have stronger London forces, higher boiling points, and condense lower down. Cracking then converts surplus long-chain fractions into shorter, more valuable alkanes and alkenes, matching supply to demand.","reforge":{"stem":"Catalytic cracking of long-chain alkanes is carried out mainly to:","options":{"A":"Produce shorter, useful molecules","B":"Remove sulfur from the fractions.","C":"Increase the average chain length.","D":"Convert alkenes back into alkanes."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.2"},{"id":"CHEM-N3-17","stem":"In infrared spectroscopy, a broad absorption between 3230 and 3550 cm⁻¹ indicates:","options":{"A":"A C=O bond in an aldehyde.","B":"A C–H bond in an alkane.","C":"A C≡N bond in a nitrile.","D":"An O–H bond in an alcohol"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Bonds absorb infrared at characteristic wavenumbers as they vibrate. Key values: O–H in alcohols broad at 3230–3550; O–H in carboxylic acids very broad at 2500–3300; C=O sharp and strong at 1680–1750; C–H around 2850–3100; N–H at 3300–3500. The fingerprint region below 1500 cm⁻¹ is complex but unique to each compound, so it identifies a substance by comparison with a database spectrum.","reforge":{"stem":"The fingerprint region of an infrared spectrum is useful because it:","options":{"A":"Shows only the C=O absorptions clearly.","B":"Is unique to a particular compound","C":"Contains no absorptions at all.","D":"Gives the relative molecular mass."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.6"},{"id":"CHEM-N3-18","stem":"In a mass spectrum of an organic compound, the molecular ion peak gives the:","options":{"A":"Relative molecular mass","B":"Number of carbon atoms only.","C":"Empirical formula directly.","D":"Boiling point of the substance."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCHEM-MASS-SPECTRA","scaffold":"The molecular ion peak, M, is at the highest m/z (ignoring isotope peaks) and gives the relative molecular mass. Fragmentation peaks arise from the molecular ion breaking apart, and their differences identify lost groups: a loss of 15 indicates CH₃, 29 indicates C₂H₅ or CHO, and 17 indicates OH. An M+1 peak arises from carbon-13, and its relative height indicates the number of carbon atoms.","reforge":{"stem":"A fragment peak 15 units below the molecular ion peak indicates the loss of:","options":{"A":"An OH group.","B":"A C₂H₅ group.","C":"A CH₃ group","D":"A CHO group."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.6"},{"id":"CHEM-N3-19","stem":"In proton NMR, the number of peaks in a spectrum indicates the number of:","options":{"A":"Hydrogen atoms in the whole molecule.","B":"Different hydrogen environments","C":"Carbon atoms in the main chain.","D":"Functional groups present in the compound."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"In ¹H NMR the number of peaks gives the number of different proton environments; the integration ratio gives the relative numbers of protons in each; chemical shift indicates the environment type; and splitting follows the n+1 rule, where a peak splits into one more than the number of protons on adjacent carbons. TMS is the reference standard at 0 ppm, and CDCl₃ is used as a solvent because it contains no ordinary protons.","reforge":{"stem":"A peak in a proton NMR spectrum is split into a triplet. This indicates the adjacent carbon carries:","options":{"A":"One hydrogen atom.","B":"Three hydrogen atoms.","C":"No hydrogen atoms.","D":"Two hydrogen atoms"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.15"},{"id":"CHEM-N3-20","stem":"Benzene does not readily undergo addition reactions because its delocalised ring is:","options":{"A":"Highly strained and unstable.","B":"Unable to attract any electrophiles.","C":"More stable than three double bonds","D":"Composed of single bonds only."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-MECHANISMS","scaffold":"Benzene's six delocalised pi electrons are spread evenly above and below the ring, making all C–C bonds equal in length and the molecule more stable than the theoretical cyclohexa-1,3,5-triene — the difference is the delocalisation energy, shown by comparing enthalpies of hydrogenation. Addition would destroy that stability, so benzene undergoes electrophilic substitution instead, retaining the aromatic ring.","reforge":{"stem":"Benzene reacts with electrophiles by substitution rather than addition because substitution:","options":{"A":"Preserves the delocalised ring","B":"Produces a more reactive product.","C":"Requires no catalyst to proceed.","D":"Breaks all six carbon–carbon bonds."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.10"},{"id":"CHEM-N3-21","stem":"Nitration of benzene requires concentrated nitric acid and concentrated sulfuric acid in order to generate:","options":{"A":"Hydrogen gas as the electrophile.","B":"A free radical from nitric acid.","C":"A nucleophilic nitrate ion.","D":"The nitronium ion, NO₂⁺"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-MECHANISMS","scaffold":"Sulfuric acid protonates nitric acid, which loses water to form the nitronium ion NO₂⁺ — the electrophile. It is attacked by the delocalised ring, forming an unstable intermediate that loses H⁺ to restore aromaticity; the sulfuric acid catalyst is regenerated. Friedel–Crafts alkylation and acylation follow the same electrophilic substitution pattern, using an AlCl₃ halogen carrier to generate the electrophile.","reforge":{"stem":"In Friedel–Crafts acylation, aluminium chloride acts as a:","options":{"A":"Nucleophile attacking the ring.","B":"Halogen carrier for it","C":"Solvent for the reaction mixture.","D":"Reducing agent for the acyl group."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.10"},{"id":"CHEM-N3-22","stem":"Primary aliphatic amines are more basic than ammonia because alkyl groups:","options":{"A":"Push electron density onto the nitrogen","B":"Withdraw electron density from nitrogen.","C":"Remove the lone pair entirely.","D":"Form hydrogen bonds with water."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCHEM-FUNCTIONAL-GROUPS","scaffold":"Basicity depends on the availability of nitrogen's lone pair to accept a proton. Alkyl groups are electron-releasing, increasing lone pair availability, so primary aliphatic amines are stronger bases than ammonia. Aromatic amines such as phenylamine are weaker, because the lone pair is partly delocalised into the benzene ring and so less available. Amines are made from haloalkanes with excess ammonia, or by reducing nitriles or nitroarenes.","reforge":{"stem":"Phenylamine is a weaker base than ethylamine because its nitrogen lone pair is:","options":{"A":"Removed by the aromatic system.","B":"Involved in a dative covalent bond.","C":"Delocalised into the benzene ring","D":"Shielded by two alkyl groups."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.11"},{"id":"CHEM-N3-23","stem":"An amino acid exists as a zwitterion at its isoelectric point because it contains:","options":{"A":"Only a basic amine group.","B":"An acidic and a basic group","C":"Two identical carboxyl groups.","D":"A chiral centre in every case."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCHEM-FUNCTIONAL-GROUPS","scaffold":"Amino acids carry both –COOH and –NH₂, so they are amphoteric. At the isoelectric point the carboxyl group is deprotonated and the amine protonated, giving a zwitterion with no overall charge. In acid the molecule gains a proton and becomes positive; in alkali it loses one and becomes negative. All amino acids except glycine have a chiral centre, since glycine's side chain is a hydrogen.","reforge":{"stem":"Glycine is the only common amino acid that is not chiral because its side chain is:","options":{"A":"A methyl group.","B":"A carboxyl group.","C":"An amine group.","D":"A hydrogen atom"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.13"},{"id":"CHEM-N3-24","stem":"Thin-layer chromatography separates compounds according to their relative:","options":{"A":"Boiling points in the mobile phase.","B":"Molecular masses alone.","C":"Affinity for each of the phases","D":"Colours under ultraviolet light."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCHEM-INORGANIC-TESTS","scaffold":"In TLC the stationary phase is a thin solid layer and the mobile phase a solvent moving up the plate. A component with greater affinity for the mobile phase travels further. Rf = distance moved by spot ÷ distance moved by solvent front, and is constant for a compound under fixed conditions. The baseline must be pencil, and drawn above the solvent level so the spots are not washed off.","reforge":{"stem":"The baseline on a TLC plate is drawn in pencil rather than pen because ink would:","options":{"A":"Dissolve and travel up the plate","B":"React chemically with the stationary phase.","C":"Prevent the solvent rising up the plate.","D":"Make the spots invisible under UV light."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.16"},{"id":"CHEM-N3-25","stem":"Heating a reaction mixture under reflux allows a reaction to be carried out at high temperature without:","options":{"A":"Producing any unwanted by-products.","B":"Requiring any catalyst to be present.","C":"Changing the position of equilibrium.","D":"Losing volatile substances"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCHEM-INORGANIC-TESTS","scaffold":"In reflux, vapour rises into a vertical condenser, condenses and returns to the flask, so the mixture can be heated for a long period without loss of volatile reactants or products. Anti-bumping granules give smooth boiling, and the apparatus must never be sealed, or pressure would build dangerously. Distillation instead collects a product as it forms, which is how an aldehyde is isolated before further oxidation.","reforge":{"stem":"Anti-bumping granules are added to a reflux flask in order to:","options":{"A":"Catalyse the reaction taking place.","B":"Promote smooth and even boiling","C":"Raise the boiling point of the mixture.","D":"Absorb any water produced."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.14"},{"id":"CHEM-N3-26","stem":"Recrystallisation purifies a solid product by exploiting the fact that impurities:","options":{"A":"Stay dissolved in cold solvent","B":"Have a much higher melting point.","C":"Evaporate away during heating.","D":"React with the chosen solvent."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCHEM-INORGANIC-TESTS","scaffold":"Recrystallisation: dissolve the crude solid in the minimum volume of hot solvent, filter hot to remove insoluble impurities, cool slowly so the product crystallises while soluble impurities stay in solution, then filter under reduced pressure, wash with a little cold solvent and dry. Yield is always reduced, since some product stays dissolved. Purity is checked by melting point: a pure solid melts sharply at the literature value.","reforge":{"stem":"An impure solid melts over a range and below its literature value. This indicates the sample is:","options":{"A":"Pure but incorrectly identified.","B":"Completely free of any solvent.","C":"Impure, so the range broadens","D":"A different physical state entirely."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.14"},{"id":"CHEM-N3-27","stem":"The general formula of an alkene is:","options":{"A":"CₙH₂ₙ₊₂","B":"CₙH₂ₙ","C":"CₙH₂ₙ₋₂.","D":"CₙH₂ₙ₊₁OH."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Alkanes CₙH₂ₙ₊₂ are saturated; alkenes CₙH₂ₙ contain one C=C and are unsaturated; alcohols are CₙH₂ₙ₊₁OH. Each degree of unsaturation — a double bond or a ring — removes two hydrogens from the saturated formula, which lets a molecular formula be checked against a proposed structure. Note cycloalkanes share the alkene general formula, so formula alone does not prove a double bond is present.","reforge":{"stem":"A compound with formula C₄H₈ could be either an alkene or a:","options":{"A":"Straight-chain alkane.","B":"Primary alcohol.","C":"Carboxylic acid.","D":"Cycloalkane"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.4"},{"id":"CHEM-N3-28","stem":"In the IUPAC name 2-methylbutan-1-ol, the number 1 indicates the position of the:","options":{"A":"Methyl branch on the chain.","B":"Longest carbon chain's end.","C":"Hydroxyl functional group","D":"Double bond in the molecule."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Naming: find the longest chain containing the functional group for the stem, number from the end giving the functional group the lowest locant, name substituents alphabetically with their positions, and use di-, tri- for repeats. So 2-methylbutan-1-ol is a four-carbon chain with OH on carbon 1 and a methyl on carbon 2. The suffix identifies the principal functional group, and the locant before it gives its position.","reforge":{"stem":"When numbering a carbon chain, the direction is chosen so that the functional group receives:","options":{"A":"The lowest possible number","B":"The highest possible number.","C":"The same number as any branch.","D":"No number at all in the name."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.1"},{"id":"CHEM-N3-29","stem":"A nucleophile is best described as a species that:","options":{"A":"Accepts a pair of electrons.","B":"Contains an unpaired electron.","C":"Always carries a positive charge.","D":"Donates a pair of electrons"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-MECHANISMS","scaffold":"A nucleophile donates an electron pair, so it is attracted to electron-deficient (δ+) carbon — OH⁻, CN⁻, NH₃ and water are examples, each with a lone pair or negative charge. An electrophile accepts an electron pair and is attracted to electron-rich sites such as a C=C. A free radical has an unpaired electron and is neither. Curly arrows always show the movement of an electron pair, from the donor to the acceptor.","reforge":{"stem":"In a reaction mechanism, a curly arrow represents the movement of:","options":{"A":"A single unpaired electron.","B":"A pair of electrons","C":"An entire atom between molecules.","D":"A proton from acid to base."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.3"},{"id":"CHEM-N3-30","stem":"Which pair of reagents would convert a nitrile into a primary amine?","options":{"A":"LiAlH₄, or hydrogen with a catalyst","B":"Acidified potassium dichromate solution.","C":"Concentrated nitric and sulfuric acids.","D":"Aqueous sodium hydroxide, heated under reflux."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCHEM-SYNTHESIS","scaffold":"Nitriles are reduced to primary amines using LiAlH₄ in dry ether, or hydrogen with a nickel catalyst. They are useful in synthesis because the CN⁻ substitution step extends the carbon chain by one, and the nitrile can then be reduced to an amine or hydrolysed to a carboxylic acid. Planning a multi-step synthesis means tracking both the functional group changes and the carbon count at each stage.","reforge":{"stem":"Reacting a haloalkane with KCN is useful in synthesis because it:","options":{"A":"Converts the compound to an alkene.","B":"Removes a carbon from the chain.","C":"Lengthens the chain by one carbon","D":"Introduces a hydroxyl group directly."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.11"},{"id":"CHEM-N3-31","stem":"Phenol reacts with bromine water at room temperature without a catalyst, unlike benzene, because the:","options":{"A":"Hydroxyl group withdraws electron density.","B":"Oxygen lone pair enriches the ring","C":"Ring in phenol contains no delocalisation.","D":"Molecule is far smaller than benzene."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-MECHANISMS","scaffold":"In phenol, a lone pair on the oxygen is partly delocalised into the ring, raising its electron density so it polarises bromine without a halogen carrier — giving an immediate white precipitate of 2,4,6-tribromophenol and decolourising bromine water. Benzene needs a catalyst and more forcing conditions. Phenol is also weakly acidic, reacting with sodium hydroxide but not with sodium carbonate, unlike carboxylic acids.","reforge":{"stem":"Phenol reacts with sodium hydroxide but not with sodium carbonate, showing that phenol is:","options":{"A":"A stronger acid than carboxylic acids.","B":"A base rather than an acid.","C":"Completely neutral in solution.","D":"Weakly acidic, less than an acid"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.10"},{"id":"CHEM-N3-32","stem":"An addition polymer is difficult to dispose of because its carbon backbone is:","options":{"A":"Held together by hydrogen bonds.","B":"Highly reactive towards water.","C":"Unreactive and non-biodegradable","D":"Broken down rapidly by sunlight."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCHEM-SYNTHESIS","scaffold":"Addition polymers have saturated, non-polar carbon backbones with strong C–C and C–H bonds, so they resist chemical attack and are not biodegradable, persisting for centuries. Disposal options: landfill (space and leachate), incineration (energy recovery but toxic emissions if PVC is present), and recycling (needs sorting). Condensation polymers such as polyesters contain polar links that can be hydrolysed, so they break down more readily.","reforge":{"stem":"Polyesters are more easily broken down than poly(ethene) because polyesters contain:","options":{"A":"Ester links, which hydrolyse","B":"Only carbon–carbon single bonds.","C":"A saturated hydrocarbon backbone.","D":"No repeating monomer units."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.12"},{"id":"CHEM-N3-33","stem":"Which technique would best determine the structure of an unknown organic compound?","options":{"A":"Measuring its melting point alone.","B":"Observing its colour and smell.","C":"Weighing a sample accurately.","D":"Combining NMR, IR and mass spectra"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Structure determination combines techniques: mass spectrometry gives relative molecular mass and fragmentation clues; infrared identifies functional groups present; ¹H and ¹³C NMR reveal the carbon–hydrogen skeleton through environments, integration and splitting; and elemental analysis gives the empirical formula. No single method is sufficient, which is why exam questions supply several data sets and expect them to be reconciled.","reforge":{"stem":"Elemental analysis of a compound provides its:","options":{"A":"Full three-dimensional structure.","B":"Empirical formula","C":"Boiling point and density.","D":"Number of proton environments."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.6"},{"id":"CHEM-N3-34","stem":"Optical isomers are difficult to separate by ordinary means because they have identical:","options":{"A":"Physical properties when achiral","B":"Arrangements of atoms in space.","C":"Effects on plane-polarised light.","D":"Numbers of chiral centres always."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Enantiomers share melting point, boiling point, density and solubility in ordinary solvents, so distillation and recrystallisation cannot separate them; they differ only in the direction they rotate plane-polarised light and in behaviour towards other chiral species. This matters pharmaceutically, since one enantiomer may be therapeutic and the other inactive or harmful, so drug synthesis aims to produce a single enantiomer.","reforge":{"stem":"Producing a single enantiomer of a drug rather than a racemic mixture matters because the other enantiomer may be:","options":{"A":"Identical in every biological respect.","B":"Impossible to synthesise at all.","C":"Inactive or harmful in the body","D":"More soluble in organic solvents."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.7"},{"id":"CHEM-N3-35","stem":"A student obtains a low percentage yield in an organic preparation. The most likely cause is:","options":{"A":"Weighing the product too accurately.","B":"Losses in transfer and purification","C":"Using a reflux condenser correctly.","D":"An error in the balanced equation."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCHEM-SYNTHESIS","scaffold":"Yields fall below theoretical because of incomplete or reversible reaction, competing side reactions, and mechanical losses when transferring between vessels, filtering, and washing crystals. Recrystallisation always sacrifices some product, since a little stays dissolved. Improvements: excess of the cheaper reagent, removing product to shift equilibrium, and minimising transfers. Distinguish yield, which measures how much was obtained, from purity, which measures what it contains.","reforge":{"stem":"Percentage yield and purity are different measures because purity describes:","options":{"A":"How much product was obtained overall.","B":"The cost of the reagents used.","C":"The speed at which the reaction occurred.","D":"The proportion that is product"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.1.2"},{"id":"CHEM-N3-36","stem":"Ethanol produced by fermentation differs from ethanol produced by hydration of ethene in that fermentation:","options":{"A":"Runs continuously at high temperature.","B":"Produces a much purer product directly.","C":"Uses a renewable feedstock, in batches","D":"Requires a phosphoric acid catalyst."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCHEM-SYNTHESIS","scaffold":"Fermentation uses sugars from a renewable crop with yeast at about 35 °C, is a slow batch process giving impure dilute ethanol needing distillation, but has low energy needs and is close to carbon neutral. Hydration of ethene uses a finite crude oil feedstock with steam and a phosphoric acid catalyst at high temperature and pressure, but runs continuously and gives a pure product with high atom economy.","reforge":{"stem":"Hydration of ethene is preferred industrially where the priority is:","options":{"A":"Continuous production of ethanol","B":"Minimising energy consumption.","C":"Avoiding the need for any catalyst.","D":"Using a renewable plant feedstock."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.5"},{"id":"CHEM-N3-37","stem":"A curly arrow starting at a lone pair and pointing to a carbon atom represents:","options":{"A":"A proton transferring to the carbon.","B":"An electrophile leaving the molecule.","C":"The homolytic breaking of a bond.","D":"A nucleophile attacking that carbon"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-MECHANISMS","scaffold":"Curly arrows show electron pair movement, starting from a lone pair, a negative charge, or a bond, and pointing where the pair goes. An arrow from a lone pair to a δ+ carbon shows nucleophilic attack; an arrow from a bond to an atom shows heterolytic fission, giving ions. Half-headed arrows show single-electron movement in radical mechanisms. Marks are usually awarded for arrow position and direction, so precision matters.","reforge":{"stem":"Heterolytic fission of a covalent bond produces:","options":{"A":"Two radicals with unpaired electrons.","B":"Two oppositely charged ions","C":"Two identical neutral atoms.","D":"No products until a catalyst is added."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.1"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-01","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-STRUCTURE","stem":"What is the IUPAC name of CH₃CH(CH₃)CH₂CH₃?","options":{"A":"2-ethylpropane","B":"3-methylbutane","C":"2-methylbutane","D":"1,1-dimethylpropane"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"Find the longest carbon chain first: here it is four carbons, so the parent is butane. Number from the end that gives the substituent the lowest number, so the methyl group is on carbon 2, not carbon 3. '2-ethylpropane' fails because it does not use the longest chain.","reforge":{"stem":"How many structural isomers have the molecular formula C₅H₁₂?","options":{"A":"Four","B":"Seven","C":"Three","D":"Five"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.1"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-02","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-STRUCTURE","stem":"Pentan-1-ol and pentan-2-ol are which type of structural isomer?","options":{"A":"Optical isomers","B":"Chain isomers","C":"Functional group isomers","D":"Positional isomers"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"Structural isomers share a molecular formula but differ in structure. Chain isomers differ in the carbon skeleton (branching), positional isomers have the same functional group in a different position, and functional group isomers have different functional groups altogether. Here only the position of the –OH changes, so they are positional isomers.","reforge":{"stem":"Propanoic acid and methyl ethanoate are which type of structural isomer?","options":{"A":"Functional group isomers","B":"Positional isomers","C":"Chain isomers","D":"Geometric E/Z stereoisomers"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.1"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-03","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-STRUCTURE","stem":"Under the CIP rules, priority on each carbon of a C=C bond goes to the attached atom with:","options":{"A":"the higher atomic number","B":"the larger relative mass of its group","C":"the greater number of bonds","D":"the lower electronegativity"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"Cahn–Ingold–Prelog priority is decided by atomic number of the atom directly attached to the double-bond carbon: Br > Cl > O > C > H. Compare the two groups on each carbon separately. If the two higher-priority groups are on the same side, the isomer is Z (zusammen); on opposite sides it is E (entgegen).","reforge":{"stem":"If the two higher-priority groups are on the same side of a C=C bond, the isomer is:","options":{"A":"a racemic mixture","B":"the E isomer","C":"the cis-trans isomer","D":"the Z isomer"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.1"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-04","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-STRUCTURE","stem":"What is the general formula of the alkanes?","options":{"A":"CₙH₂ₙ","B":"CₙH₂ₙ₊₂","C":"CₙH₂ₙ₋₂","D":"CₙH₂ₙ₊₁OH"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"Alkanes are saturated: every carbon has four single bonds, and each end carbon carries an extra hydrogen, giving CₙH₂ₙ₊₂ (methane CH₄, ethane C₂H₆). Alkenes and cycloalkanes are CₙH₂ₙ, and alcohols are CₙH₂ₙ₊₁OH. A general formula is shared by every member of a homologous series.","reforge":{"stem":"What does a skeletal formula show?","options":{"A":"Every atom and every bond in the molecule","B":"Bonds only, with C and its H atoms not labelled","C":"The simplest whole-number ratio of atoms","D":"The arrangement of all atoms in three dimensions"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.1"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-05","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-MECHANISMS","stem":"Thermal cracking differs from catalytic cracking because thermal cracking:","options":{"A":"takes place at low temperature with no extra pressure applied","B":"uses a zeolite catalyst and gives mainly branched alkanes","C":"uses high temperature and pressure and gives mainly alkenes","D":"turns short alkanes into longer, more useful chains"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"Thermal cracking uses high temperature (up to about 1000 °C) and high pressure and gives a high proportion of alkenes, which are used to make polymers. Catalytic cracking uses a zeolite catalyst at a slight pressure and lower temperature and gives motor fuels and aromatic hydrocarbons. Both break long chains into shorter, more useful ones.","reforge":{"stem":"In a catalytic converter, which reaction removes two pollutants at once?","options":{"A":"C₈H₁₈ + 12½O₂ → 8CO₂ + 9H₂O","B":"2CO + O₂ → 2CO₂","C":"N₂ + O₂ → 2NO","D":"2CO + 2NO → 2CO₂ + N₂"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.2"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-06","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-MECHANISMS","stem":"Why is carbon monoxide from incomplete combustion dangerous?","options":{"A":"It is a greenhouse gas with no other effect","B":"It dissolves in rainwater, forming acid rain on land","C":"It destroys ozone in the upper atmosphere","D":"It binds to haemoglobin, reducing oxygen transport"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"Carbon monoxide forms when there is not enough oxygen for complete combustion. It binds to haemoglobin more strongly than oxygen does, so less oxygen is carried in the blood; it is colourless and odourless, so it gives no warning. Acid rain comes from SO₂ and NOx, and ozone loss from chlorine radicals.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can a single chlorine radical destroy thousands of ozone molecules?","options":{"A":"It is a catalyst that is used up slowly","B":"It reacts with ozone to form stable Cl₂","C":"It is regenerated at the end of each cycle","D":"It splits each O₃ molecule into three oxygen atoms"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.2"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-07","tag":"MC-GCHEM-BONDING-STRUCTURE","stem":"Why does a long-chain alkane have a higher boiling point than a short-chain one?","options":{"A":"It has stronger C–H bonds, which must be broken on boiling","B":"Larger molecules have stronger van der Waals forces","C":"It can form hydrogen bonds between its molecules","D":"It is more polar than the shorter-chain alkanes"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"Alkanes are non-polar, so only van der Waals forces act between their molecules. Longer chains have more electrons and more surface contact, so the forces are stronger and more energy is needed to separate the molecules. Boiling never breaks the C–H or C–C bonds. This is why fractional distillation can separate crude oil by chain length.","reforge":{"stem":"Branched alkanes have lower boiling points than their straight-chain isomers because they:","options":{"A":"contain fewer carbon atoms than the straight-chain isomer","B":"pack less closely, so van der Waals forces are weaker","C":"have weaker covalent bonds inside each molecule","D":"form fewer hydrogen bonds between the molecules"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.2"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-08","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-MECHANISMS","stem":"Why is excess ammonia used when making a primary amine from a halogenoalkane?","options":{"A":"To limit further substitution giving 2° and 3° amines","B":"To act as a catalyst for the substitution reaction","C":"To dissolve the halogenoalkane in the reaction mixture","D":"To neutralise the hydrogen halide and stop elimination"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"The primary amine formed still has a lone pair on nitrogen, so it is itself a nucleophile and can attack more halogenoalkane, giving secondary and tertiary amines and then a quaternary ammonium salt. A large excess of ammonia makes it much more likely that each halogenoalkane meets ammonia rather than amine, so the primary amine is the main product.","reforge":{"stem":"Bromoethane reacts with excess ammonia. What is the main organic product?","options":{"A":"Ethylamine","B":"Ethanamide","C":"Ethanenitrile","D":"Ethanol"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.3"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-09","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-MECHANISMS","stem":"Which conditions favour elimination over substitution when a halogenoalkane reacts with hydroxide?","options":{"A":"Cold, with excess aqueous ammonia","B":"Warm, with water as the solvent","C":"Room temperature, in dilute acid","D":"Hot, with ethanol as the solvent"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"Hydroxide can act as a nucleophile (substitution, giving an alcohol) or as a base (elimination, giving an alkene). Aqueous conditions favour substitution. Hot ethanolic KOH favours elimination, because in ethanol the hydroxide acts mainly as a base, removing an H⁺ from the carbon next to the C–X carbon.","reforge":{"stem":"In the elimination of HBr from a halogenoalkane, the hydroxide ion acts as:","options":{"A":"a catalyst, which is unchanged at the end","B":"a nucleophile, attacking the δ+ carbon","C":"an electrophile, accepting a lone pair","D":"a base, removing a proton from a carbon"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.3"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-10","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-MECHANISMS","stem":"In nucleophilic substitution of bromoethane by OH⁻, where does the first curly arrow start and end?","options":{"A":"From a lone pair on O to the δ+ carbon","B":"From the C–Br bond to the oxygen atom","C":"From the bromine to the δ+ carbon atom","D":"From a lone pair on O to the bromine atom"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"Curly arrows show electron pairs moving. The first arrow starts at a lone pair on the hydroxide oxygen and points at the δ+ carbon. The second starts at the C–Br bond and points at bromine, which leaves as Br⁻ taking both electrons. Marks are given for where each arrow starts and ends, so place them precisely.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is the carbon bonded to the halogen in a halogenoalkane attacked by nucleophiles?","options":{"A":"The C–X bond is a double bond rich in electrons","B":"The carbon atom has a lone pair available to share in bonding","C":"The halogen is more electronegative, making the carbon δ+","D":"The halogen donates electrons to the carbon atom"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.3"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-11","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-MECHANISMS","stem":"Which halogenoalkane is hydrolysed fastest when warmed with aqueous silver nitrate in ethanol?","options":{"A":"1-bromobutane","B":"1-chlorobutane","C":"1-iodobutane","D":"1-fluorobutane"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"The rate depends on how easily the C–X bond breaks. Bond enthalpy falls from C–F to C–I, so the C–I bond breaks fastest and the yellow AgI precipitate appears first. Bond polarity would predict the opposite order, so this result shows that bond enthalpy, not polarity, controls the rate.","reforge":{"stem":"In hydrolysis with aqueous silver nitrate, 1-bromobutane gives a precipitate that is:","options":{"A":"white, and forms faster than with 1-iodobutane","B":"cream, and forms more slowly than with 1-iodobutane","C":"yellow, and forms faster than with 1-chlorobutane","D":"black, because the silver bromide is reduced to silver"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.3"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-12","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-MECHANISMS","stem":"CFCs were once widely used in aerosols and refrigerators because they are:","options":{"A":"highly reactive and easily broken down","B":"unreactive, non-toxic and non-flammable","C":"cheap to make by reacting methane with sulfur","D":"good fuels that burn very cleanly"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"CFCs are unreactive, non-toxic and non-flammable, which made them seem ideal. That same stability means they survive long enough to reach the stratosphere, where UV light breaks the C–Cl bond and releases chlorine radicals that catalyse ozone breakdown. Their use is now banned, and HFCs, which contain no chlorine, are used instead.","reforge":{"stem":"Why do CFCs break down in the upper atmosphere but not near the ground?","options":{"A":"Stronger UV there can break the C–Cl bond","B":"There is more oxygen up there, so they burn","C":"It is warmer there, so they melt and react","D":"Water vapour there hydrolyses the C–F bonds"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.3"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-13","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-MECHANISMS","stem":"Ethene reacts with concentrated sulfuric acid, and the product is then warmed with water. What forms?","options":{"A":"Ethyl sulfate only","B":"Ethane","C":"Ethanoic acid","D":"Ethanol"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"Ethene adds H₂SO₄ by electrophilic addition, giving ethyl hydrogensulfate, CH₃CH₂OSO₂OH. Warming that with water hydrolyses it to ethanol and regenerates the sulfuric acid, so overall water has been added across the C=C. The industrial route instead uses steam and a phosphoric acid catalyst.","reforge":{"stem":"In the electrophilic addition of HBr to ethene, what is the intermediate?","options":{"A":"A carbanion with a lone pair","B":"A bromine radical","C":"A primary carbocation","D":"A cyclic alkane"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.4"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-14","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-MECHANISMS","stem":"Why is a tertiary carbocation more stable than a primary carbocation?","options":{"A":"More alkyl groups push electron density onto the positive carbon","B":"It has more hydrogen atoms around it to share out the positive charge","C":"Its positive carbon is bonded to a halogen atom","D":"Tertiary carbocations carry no positive charge at all"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"Alkyl groups are electron-releasing (a positive inductive effect). A tertiary carbocation has three alkyl groups on the positive carbon, which spread out its charge and stabilise it; a primary has only one. The more stable carbocation forms faster, so it decides the major product when HBr adds to an unsymmetrical alkene.","reforge":{"stem":"HBr adds to but-1-ene. What is the major product?","options":{"A":"1-bromobutane","B":"2-bromobutane","C":"2-bromobut-1-ene","D":"1,2-dibromobutane"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.4"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-15","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-STRUCTURE","stem":"Poly(propene) is formed from propene. What is its repeating unit?","options":{"A":"–CH=CH–CH₃–","B":"–CH₂–CH₂–CH₂–","C":"–CH₂–CH(CH₃)–","D":"–CH(CH₃)=CH₂–"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"In addition polymerisation the C=C opens and the two carbons that were double-bonded join the chain; everything else becomes a side group. Propene is CH₂=CH–CH₃, so the chain gets –CH₂–CH(CH₃)– with the methyl group hanging off it. The repeating unit has no double bond, and a chain carbon cannot carry a third chain bond.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is poly(ethene) so unreactive?","options":{"A":"It forms hydrogen bonds that hold the chains apart","B":"Its C=C double bonds are shielded deep inside the chain","C":"It is ionic, so it does not dissolve in any solvents","D":"Its saturated chain has only strong, non-polar bonds"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.4"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-16","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-STRUCTURE","stem":"Plasticisers are added to PVC in order to:","options":{"A":"cross-link the chains so that the plastic becomes rigid","B":"let the chains slide past each other, making it flexible","C":"raise its melting point so that it can be used for pipes","D":"make it biodegradable when it is put in landfill"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"Plasticiser molecules get between the polymer chains and weaken the forces between them, so the chains slide more easily. Unplasticised PVC is rigid and used for pipes and window frames; plasticised PVC is flexible and used for cable insulation and clothing. Plasticisers do not make the polymer biodegradable.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the monomer of poly(chloroethene), PVC?","options":{"A":"CH₂=CHCl","B":"CH₃CH₂Cl","C":"CHCl=CHCl","D":"CCl₂=CCl₂"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.4"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-17","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-MECHANISMS","stem":"Bromine reacts with cyclohexene. What is the product?","options":{"A":"Bromocyclohexane","B":"1,2-dibromocyclohexane","C":"Cyclohexane","D":"1,2-dibromocyclohexene"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"Bromine adds across the C=C bond by electrophilic addition, one bromine atom joining each of the two carbons that were double-bonded. The ring stays intact and the product is saturated, so it is 1,2-dibromocyclohexane, not a cyclohexene. This addition is why cyclohexene decolourises bromine water.","reforge":{"stem":"In the mechanism for Br₂ adding to an alkene, the second step involves:","options":{"A":"Br⁺ attacking the double bond","B":"Br⁻ attacking the carbocation","C":"a radical joining the chain","D":"H⁺ leaving the carbocation"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.4"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-18","tag":"MC-GCHEM-FUNCTIONAL-GROUPS","stem":"Propan-2-ol is heated with acidified potassium dichromate(VI). What forms?","options":{"A":"No reaction occurs","B":"Propanal","C":"Propanoic acid","D":"Propanone"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"Propan-2-ol is a secondary alcohol, so oxidation gives a ketone: propanone. A ketone cannot be oxidised further by dichromate, so no acid forms however long you reflux. Primary alcohols give aldehydes and then carboxylic acids; tertiary alcohols are not oxidised.","reforge":{"stem":"What colour change is seen when acidified dichromate oxidises an alcohol?","options":{"A":"Purple to colourless","B":"Green to orange","C":"Orange to green","D":"Yellow to blue"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.5"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-19","tag":"MC-GCHEM-FUNCTIONAL-GROUPS","stem":"Butan-2-ol is classified as a:","options":{"A":"tertiary alcohol","B":"primary alcohol","C":"secondary alcohol","D":"diol, with two –OH groups"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"Classify an alcohol by counting the carbons attached to the carbon that carries the –OH. In butan-2-ol, CH₃CH(OH)CH₂CH₃, that carbon is bonded to two other carbons, so it is secondary. One carbon makes it primary, three makes it tertiary. The class decides what oxidation gives.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is 2-methylpropan-2-ol a tertiary alcohol?","options":{"A":"It has its –OH on the third carbon","B":"It has three separate –OH groups in each molecule","C":"Its chain contains three carbon atoms","D":"Its C–OH carbon is bonded to three other carbons"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.5"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-20","tag":"MC-GCHEM-FUNCTIONAL-GROUPS","stem":"Ethanol from fermentation is called carbon-neutral because:","options":{"A":"the CO₂ released was absorbed by the crops as they grew","B":"burning ethanol releases no carbon dioxide at all","C":"fermentation absorbs more carbon dioxide than it releases","D":"ethanol contains no carbon atoms to release"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"Plants take in 6CO₂ in photosynthesis to make glucose. Fermentation releases 2CO₂ and burning the ethanol releases 4CO₂, so the totals balance on paper. In practice, energy from fossil fuels is used to grow, harvest, transport and distil the crop, so bioethanol is not truly carbon-neutral.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is bioethanol not truly carbon-neutral in practice?","options":{"A":"Fossil fuels are burned to farm, transport and distil it","B":"Ethanol releases more CO₂ per mole when burned than petrol","C":"Fermentation releases methane as well as CO₂","D":"Photosynthesis does not absorb carbon dioxide"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.5"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-21","tag":"MC-GCHEM-FUNCTIONAL-GROUPS","stem":"Dehydration of an alcohol to an alkene is an example of which type of reaction?","options":{"A":"Oxidation","B":"Substitution","C":"Addition","D":"Elimination"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"Dehydration removes an –OH from one carbon and an H from the neighbouring carbon as water, leaving a C=C: that is elimination. It uses concentrated phosphoric or sulfuric acid as a catalyst with heat. It gives a route to alkenes, and so to polymers, from ethanol made by fermentation instead of from crude oil.","reforge":{"stem":"Dehydration of butan-2-ol can give more than one alkene. Why?","options":{"A":"The H lost can come from either neighbouring carbon","B":"The alcohol breaks into two separate carbon chains","C":"Both oxygen atoms can leave as a water molecule","D":"The –OH group can move along the carbon chain first"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.5"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-22","tag":"MC-GCHEM-FUNCTIONAL-GROUPS","stem":"An alcohol is heated under reflux with acidified dichromate, but the mixture stays orange. The alcohol is:","options":{"A":"tertiary","B":"primary","C":"secondary","D":"ethanol"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"A tertiary alcohol has no hydrogen on the carbon carrying the –OH, so it cannot be oxidised by dichromate without breaking C–C bonds, and the orange colour remains. Primary and secondary alcohols (including ethanol) turn the dichromate green. This makes the test a way of telling tertiary alcohols apart.","reforge":{"stem":"Which reagent can make an alkene from an alcohol in the laboratory?","options":{"A":"Tollens' reagent","B":"Aqueous sodium hydroxide","C":"Acidified potassium dichromate","D":"Concentrated phosphoric acid"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.5"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-23","tag":"MC-GCHEM-FUNCTIONAL-GROUPS","stem":"How is ethanol made industrially from ethene?","options":{"A":"Yeast at about 35 °C, with the air kept out completely","B":"Steam and a phosphoric acid catalyst at about 300 °C","C":"Hydrogen and a nickel catalyst at 150 °C","D":"Aqueous sodium hydroxide under reflux"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"Hydration of ethene: C₂H₄ + H₂O → C₂H₅OH, using steam, a phosphoric acid catalyst, about 300 °C and 6–7 MPa. It is fast, continuous and gives nearly pure ethanol, but it uses a non-renewable feedstock. Fermentation of glucose with yeast at about 35 °C is slower and batch-based, but uses a renewable crop.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the equation for fermentation?","options":{"A":"C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂ → 6CO₂ + 6H₂O","B":"C₆H₁₂O₆ → 2C₂H₅OH + 2CO₂","C":"C₂H₄ + H₂O → C₂H₅OH","D":"C₂H₅OH → C₂H₄ + H₂O"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.5"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-24","tag":"MC-GCHEM-MASS-SPECTRA","stem":"High-resolution mass spectrometry can tell C₃H₈ from CO₂ because:","options":{"A":"only one of them forms a molecular ion","B":"their molecular ions carry different charges in the spectrometer","C":"their precise Mr values differ in the decimal places","D":"they have different numbers of isotopes"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"Both have Mr = 44 to the nearest whole number, but precise atomic masses are not whole: H = 1.0078, C = 12.0000, O = 15.9949. So C₃H₈ is 44.0624 and CO₂ is 43.9898. High-resolution mass spectrometry measures Mr to four decimal places, which picks out the molecular formula.","reforge":{"stem":"Using C = 12.0000, H = 1.0078 and O = 15.9949, which compound has a precise Mr of 44.0261?","options":{"A":"Carbon dioxide, CO₂","B":"Propane, C₃H₈","C":"Ethanal, C₂H₄O","D":"Dinitrogen oxide, N₂O"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.6"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-25","tag":"MC-GCHEM-MASS-SPECTRA","stem":"In infrared spectroscopy, a strong, sharp absorption at about 1700 cm⁻¹ indicates:","options":{"A":"a C=O bond","B":"an O–H bond in an alcohol","C":"a C–H bond","D":"an N–H bond"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"Each bond absorbs IR at a characteristic wavenumber, given on the data sheet: C=O at 1680–1750 cm⁻¹, O–H (alcohol) at 3230–3550, O–H (acid) at 2500–3000, C–H at 2850–3300 and N–H at 3300–3500. Read the main peaks above 1500 cm⁻¹ first to find the functional groups.","reforge":{"stem":"A spectrum shows a very broad absorption from 2500 to 3000 cm⁻¹ and a strong peak at 1710 cm⁻¹. The compound is most likely:","options":{"A":"a carboxylic acid","B":"an alcohol","C":"an alkene","D":"an aldehyde or a ketone"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.6"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-26","tag":"MC-GCHEM-MASS-SPECTRA","stem":"Why does infrared absorption by CO₂ and CH₄ contribute to global warming?","options":{"A":"They absorb UV light from the Sun and break ozone down","B":"Their bonds absorb IR given off by the Earth's surface","C":"They reflect visible sunlight back down onto the Earth","D":"They react with water vapour to form acids"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"The Earth absorbs sunlight and re-emits energy as infrared. Bonds such as C=O in CO₂ and C–H in CH₄ absorb IR at those wavelengths, so the energy is trapped in the atmosphere instead of escaping to space. This is the same bond vibration that IR spectroscopy detects in the laboratory.","reforge":{"stem":"An IR spectrum shows no absorption near 1700 cm⁻¹ and no broad band above 3000 cm⁻¹. Which compound fits?","options":{"A":"Hexanal","B":"Hexan-1-ol","C":"Hexane","D":"Hexanoic acid"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.6"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-27","tag":"MC-GCHEM-FUNCTIONAL-GROUPS","stem":"Fehling's solution is warmed with an unknown liquid and a brick-red precipitate forms. The liquid contains:","options":{"A":"an alkene","B":"a ketone","C":"a tertiary alcohol","D":"an aldehyde"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"Aldehydes are oxidised to carboxylic acids by mild oxidising agents, and ketones are not. Fehling's solution contains blue Cu²⁺, which is reduced to brick-red copper(I) oxide, Cu₂O. Tollens' reagent does the same job, giving a silver mirror. Neither reacts with ketones, alcohols or alkenes.","reforge":{"stem":"Sodium hydrogencarbonate fizzes when it is added to a liquid. Which functional group is present?","options":{"A":"Primary alcohol","B":"Carboxylic acid","C":"Aldehyde","D":"Ester"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.6"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-28","tag":"MC-GCHEM-FUNCTIONAL-GROUPS","stem":"A compound does not decolourise bromine water and gives no silver mirror, but it turns acidified dichromate green. It could be:","options":{"A":"propanone","B":"propene","C":"propan-1-ol","D":"propanoic acid"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"Work through the tests in turn. No change with bromine water rules out an alkene (propene). Dichromate turning green means something was oxidised: a primary or secondary alcohol, or an aldehyde. No silver mirror rules out an aldehyde. That leaves the alcohol, propan-1-ol. Propanone and propanoic acid are not oxidised by dichromate.","reforge":{"stem":"Which test result distinguishes propanal from propanone?","options":{"A":"Only propanal fizzes with sodium carbonate","B":"Only propanone gives a silver mirror with Tollens'","C":"Only propanone decolourises bromine water","D":"Only propanal turns acidified dichromate green"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.6"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-29","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-STRUCTURE","stem":"Which compound has a chiral centre?","options":{"A":"Butan-2-ol","B":"Propan-2-ol","C":"Butan-1-ol","D":"2-methylpropan-2-ol"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"A chiral centre is a carbon bonded to four different groups. In butan-2-ol, C2 carries H, OH, CH₃ and C₂H₅ — four different groups. In propan-2-ol, C2 carries two CH₃ groups; in butan-1-ol, C1 carries two H atoms; in 2-methylpropan-2-ol, C2 carries three CH₃ groups. None of those is chiral.","reforge":{"stem":"How many chiral centres does 2,3-dichlorobutane, CH₃CHClCHClCH₃, have?","options":{"A":"One","B":"Two","C":"Three","D":"Four"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.7"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-30","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-STRUCTURE","stem":"How do the two enantiomers of a chiral compound differ?","options":{"A":"They have different boiling points, melting points and densities","B":"They rotate plane-polarised light in opposite directions","C":"One reacts with water and the other does not","D":"They have different molecular formulae"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"Enantiomers have identical physical properties and the same reactions with non-chiral reagents. The difference is optical activity: they rotate the plane of plane-polarised light by the same angle in opposite directions. They can also behave differently with other chiral molecules, such as enzymes and receptors in the body.","reforge":{"stem":"A solution of one pure enantiomer is placed in a polarimeter. What is seen?","options":{"A":"The plane of polarised light is rotated","B":"The light is absorbed completely","C":"The light is split into its colours","D":"Nothing, as only racemic mixtures rotate light"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.7"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-31","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-STRUCTURE","stem":"HCN adds to ethanal. Why is the product a racemic mixture?","options":{"A":"The reaction is a free-radical substitution","B":"The product formed has no chiral centre at all","C":"CN⁻ attacks the planar C=O equally from either side","D":"Only one of the two enantiomers is stable at room temperature"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"The carbonyl group and the atoms around it are planar, so the nucleophile CN⁻ is equally likely to attack from above or below the plane. Each direction gives one enantiomer of 2-hydroxypropanenitrile, so they form in equal amounts: a racemic mixture, which shows no overall optical activity.","reforge":{"stem":"What type of reaction occurs when KCN and acid convert propanone into a hydroxynitrile?","options":{"A":"Nucleophilic substitution","B":"Electrophilic addition","C":"Nucleophilic addition","D":"Free-radical substitution"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.7"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-32","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-STRUCTURE","stem":"Why is 3-methylhexane chiral when 3-methylpentane is not?","options":{"A":"Only molecules with an –OH group can be chiral","B":"3-methylhexane has a longer chain, so it is more twisted","C":"3-methylpentane has a C=C double bond at carbon 3","D":"In 3-methylpentane, C3 has two identical ethyl groups"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"Check the four groups on C3. In 3-methylhexane they are H, CH₃, C₂H₅ and C₃H₇, all different, so C3 is chiral. In 3-methylpentane they are H, CH₃, C₂H₅ and C₂H₅: two are the same, so it is not. Chirality depends only on four different groups, not on chain length or functional group.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the minimum requirement for a carbon atom to be a chiral centre?","options":{"A":"Bonds to four identical groups","B":"A double bond to one of its neighbouring atoms","C":"At least one halogen atom attached","D":"Four different atoms or groups attached to it"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.7"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-33","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-STRUCTURE","stem":"Optical isomers are drawn as mirror images in three dimensions using:","options":{"A":"skeletal formulae with the chiral carbon left out","B":"wedge and dashed bonds around the chiral carbon","C":"displayed formulae with every bond drawn at 90 degrees","D":"molecular formulae written in mirror order"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"A wedge shows a bond coming towards you and a dashed line a bond going away, so the tetrahedral arrangement around the chiral carbon can be shown on paper. Draw one enantiomer, then its reflection in a mirror line. Swapping any two groups on the chiral carbon also produces the other enantiomer.","reforge":{"stem":"In 2-chlorobutane, which groups are attached to the chiral carbon?","options":{"A":"H, Cl, C₂H₅ and C₃H₇","B":"H, Cl and two CH₃ groups","C":"Cl, CH₃ and two H atoms","D":"H, Cl, CH₃ and C₂H₅"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.7"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-34","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-STRUCTURE","stem":"Lactic acid, CH₃CH(OH)COOH, has one chiral centre. Which carbon is it?","options":{"A":"The carbon bonded to H, OH, CH₃ and COOH","B":"The carbon of the COOH group, bonded to two O","C":"The carbon of the CH₃ group","D":"Every carbon in the molecule"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"Look at each carbon in turn. The CH₃ carbon has three H atoms, and the COOH carbon has only three groups because of its double bond. The middle carbon carries H, OH, CH₃ and COOH, all different, so it is the chiral centre and lactic acid has two enantiomers.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is propan-2-ol, CH₃CH(OH)CH₃, not chiral?","options":{"A":"Its –OH group is on the end of the chain","B":"It has no carbon atom that is bonded to oxygen","C":"Its central carbon carries two identical CH₃ groups","D":"It is a tertiary alcohol, so C2 carries no hydrogen"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.7"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-35","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-STRUCTURE","stem":"One pure enantiomer rotates plane-polarised light clockwise. Its mirror image will:","options":{"A":"absorb all of the plane-polarised light","B":"rotate it clockwise, but by twice the angle","C":"not rotate the light at all","D":"rotate it by the same angle anticlockwise"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"Enantiomers rotate plane-polarised light by equal amounts in opposite directions. A 50:50 mixture (a racemate) therefore shows no rotation, because the two effects cancel. Measuring the rotation with a polarimeter shows which enantiomer is present, or whether a sample is racemic.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can a reaction making a chiral product from non-chiral reactants give a racemic mixture?","options":{"A":"The planar reactant is attacked equally from both faces","B":"The product decomposes into only one enantiomer","C":"Chiral products always racemise when heated","D":"The reactants already contained a mixture of both enantiomers"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.7"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-36","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-STRUCTURE","stem":"Why might only one enantiomer of a drug work when it binds to a receptor in the body?","options":{"A":"One enantiomer is always more acidic than the other","B":"Only one of the enantiomers dissolves in blood plasma","C":"The receptor's 3D shape fits only one mirror image","D":"Enantiomers have different molecular masses"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"Receptors and enzymes are made of chiral amino acids, so their binding sites have a definite 3D shape. Like a right hand in a right-handed glove, only one enantiomer fits. The other may do nothing or cause harmful side effects, which is why a racemic product may need to be separated or a single enantiomer made directly.","reforge":{"stem":"A drug is sold as a racemic mixture. What does this mean for the patient?","options":{"A":"Every molecule in the dose is the active form","B":"Half of each dose is the other enantiomer","C":"The drug has no chiral centres at all","D":"The drug works twice as strongly as a single form"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.7"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-37","tag":"MC-GCHEM-FUNCTIONAL-GROUPS","stem":"Ethanal is reduced by NaBH₄. What is the product?","options":{"A":"Ethanol","B":"Ethanoic acid","C":"Ethane","D":"Ethene"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"NaBH₄ is a reducing agent that supplies hydride ions, H⁻. Aldehydes are reduced to primary alcohols and ketones to secondary alcohols. Ethanal, CH₃CHO, becomes ethanol, CH₃CH₂OH. NaBH₄ does not reduce C=C bonds, so it cannot make an alkane or alkene from a carbonyl.","reforge":{"stem":"Propanone is reduced by NaBH₄. What forms?","options":{"A":"Propan-2-ol","B":"Propan-1-ol","C":"Propanal","D":"Propanoic acid"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.8"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-38","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-MECHANISMS","stem":"In the reduction of a carbonyl compound by NaBH₄, which species attacks the δ+ carbon?","options":{"A":"The borohydride radical","B":"A proton, H⁺","C":"The hydride ion, H⁻","D":"A sodium ion, Na⁺"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"The mechanism is nucleophilic addition. The hydride ion, a nucleophile, attacks the δ+ carbon of C=O, and the π bond breaks onto oxygen, giving an alkoxide ion. That O⁻ then picks up H⁺ from water to give the alcohol. Use the same pattern for CN⁻ attacking a carbonyl.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does C=O undergo nucleophilic addition, while C=C undergoes electrophilic addition?","options":{"A":"C=O is non-polar and rich in electrons, just like C=C","B":"C=O is polar, so its δ+ carbon attracts nucleophiles","C":"C=O has no pi bond at all, unlike the C=C in alkenes","D":"C=O contains a carbon with a lone pair to donate"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.8"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-39","tag":"MC-GCHEM-FUNCTIONAL-GROUPS","stem":"Why is KCN with dilute acid used, rather than HCN itself, to add cyanide to a carbonyl?","options":{"A":"KCN is a much stronger acid than HCN","B":"HCN is a very toxic gas, so less of it is released","C":"KCN turns the carbonyl into an alcohol first","D":"HCN cannot react with carbonyl compounds at room temperature"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"HCN is a highly toxic gas, so the reaction uses KCN, which supplies CN⁻ ions directly, acidified to provide the H⁺ needed in the final step. KCN is itself very toxic, so the reaction still needs a fume cupboard and great care. The product is a hydroxynitrile, with one more carbon than the carbonyl compound.","reforge":{"stem":"Propanal reacts with KCN and dilute acid. What is the product?","options":{"A":"Butanoic acid","B":"Propanenitrile","C":"2-hydroxypropanenitrile","D":"2-hydroxybutanenitrile"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.8"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-40","tag":"MC-GCHEM-FUNCTIONAL-GROUPS","stem":"When Tollens' reagent oxidises an aldehyde, what is reduced?","options":{"A":"The ammonia, to nitrogen","B":"The aldehyde, to an alcohol","C":"Cu²⁺ ions, to copper(I) oxide","D":"Ag⁺ ions, to silver metal"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"Tollens' reagent is [Ag(NH₃)₂]⁺. When it oxidises the aldehyde to a carboxylic acid, the Ag⁺ is reduced to silver metal, which coats the tube as a silver mirror. In Fehling's solution, it is Cu²⁺ that is reduced, to red Cu₂O. In both tests the aldehyde is oxidised.","reforge":{"stem":"Fehling's solution turns from blue to brick-red with an aldehyde. What is the red solid?","options":{"A":"Silver metal","B":"Copper(II) hydroxide","C":"Copper(I) oxide","D":"Iron(III) oxide"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.8"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-41","tag":"MC-GCHEM-FUNCTIONAL-GROUPS","stem":"Why are ketones not oxidised by Tollens' reagent?","options":{"A":"Ketones have no C=O bond in their molecules","B":"No H on the carbonyl carbon can be removed","C":"Ketones are too reactive to be oxidised","D":"Tollens' reagent only reacts with alkenes"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"In an aldehyde the carbonyl carbon carries a hydrogen, and oxidation turns that C–H into C–OH, giving a carboxylic acid. In a ketone the carbonyl carbon is bonded to two carbons, so there is nothing to oxidise without breaking a C–C bond, and mild oxidising agents cannot do that.","reforge":{"stem":"Which compound gives a silver mirror with Tollens' reagent?","options":{"A":"Butane","B":"Butanone","C":"Butan-2-ol","D":"Butanal"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.8"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-42","tag":"MC-GCHEM-FUNCTIONAL-GROUPS","stem":"Ethanal is heated under reflux with acidified potassium dichromate(VI). What forms?","options":{"A":"Ethane-1,2-diol","B":"Ethanol","C":"Ethanoic acid","D":"Propanone"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"An aldehyde is oxidised to a carboxylic acid: CH₃CHO + [O] → CH₃COOH, and the dichromate turns from orange to green. Refluxing ensures the aldehyde stays in the flask until it is fully oxidised. Reducing an aldehyde, for example with NaBH₄, gives the primary alcohol instead.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the correct name for CH₃COCH₂CH₃?","options":{"A":"Butanone","B":"Butanal","C":"Propanone","D":"Butan-2-al"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.8"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-43","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-MECHANISMS","stem":"In nucleophilic addition of CN⁻ to a ketone, what happens after the CN⁻ attacks?","options":{"A":"The O⁻ formed picks up H⁺ to give an –OH group","B":"The C=O bond reforms and the CN⁻ leaves","C":"A carbocation forms on the oxygen atom","D":"The ketone loses a molecule of water to form a C=C"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"CN⁻ attacks the δ+ carbon and the C=O π electrons move onto oxygen, giving an alkoxide intermediate with O⁻. In the second step that O⁻ takes H⁺ (from HCN or the acid present), giving the –OH of a hydroxynitrile. Nothing is lost, which is why it is an addition reaction.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is the product of adding HCN to a carbonyl compound useful in synthesis?","options":{"A":"It converts a ketone into an alkene","B":"It removes a carbon from the chain","C":"It adds one carbon atom to the chain","D":"It forms an ester with no further steps"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.8"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-44","tag":"MC-GCHEM-FUNCTIONAL-GROUPS","stem":"Ethanoyl chloride reacts with water. What forms?","options":{"A":"Ethyl chloride and O₂","B":"Ethanol and HCl","C":"Ethanal and Cl₂","D":"Ethanoic acid and HCl"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"Acyl chlorides react vigorously with water by nucleophilic addition–elimination: CH₃COCl + H₂O → CH₃COOH + HCl. Steamy fumes of HCl are given off. With alcohols the same reaction gives esters, with ammonia amides, and with amines N-substituted amides.","reforge":{"stem":"Ethanoyl chloride reacts with methanol. What is the organic product?","options":{"A":"Ethyl methanoate","B":"Methyl ethanoate","C":"Ethanoic acid","D":"Methanoyl chloride"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.9"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-45","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-MECHANISMS","stem":"What is the mechanism when an acyl chloride reacts with an alcohol?","options":{"A":"Free-radical substitution","B":"Electrophilic substitution","C":"Nucleophilic addition–elimination","D":"Electrophilic addition–elimination"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"The carbonyl carbon of an acyl chloride is strongly δ+, because both O and Cl pull electrons away. A lone pair on the alcohol's oxygen attacks it (addition), the C=O reforms and Cl⁻ is expelled, and H⁺ is lost (elimination of HCl). The same mechanism applies with water, ammonia and amines as the nucleophile.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is ethanoic anhydride used rather than ethanoyl chloride to make aspirin industrially?","options":{"A":"It is the only reagent that can give an ester","B":"It reacts much faster and far more violently with water","C":"It is cheaper, less corrosive and makes no HCl","D":"It produces HCl, which catalyses the reaction"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.9"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-46","tag":"MC-GCHEM-FUNCTIONAL-GROUPS","stem":"Vegetable oils are heated with sodium hydroxide solution. What are the products?","options":{"A":"Propane-1,2,3-triol and soap","B":"Ethanol and long-chain fatty acids","C":"Biodiesel and water","D":"Glucose and glycerol"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"Fats and oils are triesters of propane-1,2,3-triol (glycerol) with long-chain fatty acids. Alkaline hydrolysis breaks the three ester links, giving glycerol and the sodium salts of the fatty acids, which are soaps. Because the acid ends up as a salt, alkaline hydrolysis goes to completion.","reforge":{"stem":"Biodiesel is made by reacting vegetable oils with methanol. What is biodiesel?","options":{"A":"A polymer of propene","B":"A mixture of long-chain alkanes from crude oil","C":"Pure glycerol","D":"A mixture of methyl esters of fatty acids"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.9"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-47","tag":"MC-GCHEM-FUNCTIONAL-GROUPS","stem":"Esters are used in perfumes and food flavourings because they:","options":{"A":"are strong acids that preserve the food","B":"are often volatile, with pleasant, fruity smells","C":"are polymers that thicken the product they are added to","D":"are ionic, so they dissolve easily in water"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"Small esters are volatile and have sweet, fruity smells, so they are used in perfumes and flavourings. They are also used as solvents and as plasticisers. Esters are neutral, covalent and not very soluble in water, which is why they are not used as preservatives or thickeners.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the name of the ester made from propanoic acid and ethanol?","options":{"A":"Ethyl propanoate","B":"Propyl ethanoate","C":"Ethyl propanol","D":"Propanoyl ethanol"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.9"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-48","tag":"MC-GCHEM-FUNCTIONAL-GROUPS","stem":"Ethanoyl chloride reacts with ammonia. What is the organic product?","options":{"A":"Ammonium ethanoate","B":"Ethylamine","C":"Ethanenitrile","D":"Ethanamide"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"The lone pair on ammonia attacks the carbonyl carbon, and HCl is eliminated: CH₃COCl + 2NH₃ → CH₃CONH₂ + NH₄Cl. The product, with –CONH₂, is an amide. With a primary amine instead of ammonia, the product is an N-substituted amide, such as N-methylethanamide from methylamine.","reforge":{"stem":"Ethanoyl chloride reacts with methylamine. What is the organic product?","options":{"A":"Methyl ethanoate","B":"N-methylethanamide","C":"Ethylmethylamine","D":"Ethanamide and methane"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.9"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-49","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-MECHANISMS","stem":"The enthalpy of hydrogenation of benzene is −208 kJ mol⁻¹, not −360 kJ mol⁻¹ as predicted for three C=C bonds. What does this show?","options":{"A":"Benzene has only two C=C double bonds in its ring, not three","B":"Delocalisation makes benzene about 152 kJ mol⁻¹ more stable","C":"Benzene is 152 kJ mol⁻¹ less stable than predicted","D":"The hydrogenation of benzene is endothermic overall"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"Cyclohexene releases 120 kJ mol⁻¹ when one C=C is hydrogenated, so a Kekulé benzene with three C=C bonds should release about 360. The real value is 208, so benzene is about 152 kJ mol⁻¹ lower in energy than predicted. That extra stability comes from the six delocalised π electrons, and it explains why benzene resists addition.","reforge":{"stem":"How many electrons does each carbon atom contribute to benzene's delocalised ring?","options":{"A":"Three","B":"Two","C":"One","D":"Six"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.10"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-50","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-MECHANISMS","stem":"Benzene is nitrated with concentrated nitric and sulfuric acids. What is the organic product?","options":{"A":"Benzoic acid","B":"Phenylamine","C":"Nitrobenzene","D":"Nitrocyclohexane"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"The acids together generate the nitronium ion, NO₂⁺, which attacks the ring by electrophilic substitution: one H is replaced by NO₂, giving nitrobenzene. Keeping the temperature at about 55 °C or below limits further substitution. The ring stays aromatic, so the product is not a cyclohexane.","reforge":{"stem":"Nitration of benzene is the first step in manufacturing many:","options":{"A":"polymers such as PVC","B":"soaps and detergents","C":"fuels for engines","D":"explosives and dyes"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.10"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-51","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-MECHANISMS","stem":"Friedel–Crafts acylation of benzene with ethanoyl chloride gives:","options":{"A":"phenylethanone","B":"ethylbenzene","C":"chlorobenzene","D":"methyl benzoate"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"With an AlCl₃ catalyst, the acyl group CH₃CO– replaces an H on the ring, giving C₆H₅COCH₃, phenylethanone, an aromatic ketone. Acylation is a useful way to form a C–C bond to a benzene ring. Using a chloroalkane instead of an acyl chloride would give an alkylbenzene such as ethylbenzene.","reforge":{"stem":"In Friedel–Crafts acylation, how is the electrophile formed?","options":{"A":"The acyl chloride gains an electron from the AlCl₃ catalyst","B":"AlCl₃ takes Cl⁻ from the acyl chloride, giving RCO⁺","C":"Benzene donates an H⁺ ion to the aluminium chloride","D":"The acyl chloride reacts with water to form RCOOH"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.10"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-52","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-STRUCTURE","stem":"Benzene is a planar molecule. What is the bond angle around each carbon atom?","options":{"A":"180°","B":"109.5°","C":"90°","D":"120°"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"Each carbon in benzene forms three σ bonds, to two carbons and one hydrogen, arranged trigonal planar at 120°. Its fourth electron is in a p orbital above and below the plane, and these overlap to make the delocalised π ring. So the whole molecule is flat, a regular hexagon.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is the Kekulé model of benzene, with alternating single and double bonds, rejected?","options":{"A":"Its C–C bonds are all one length, between single and double","B":"Benzene contains six double bonds, not three","C":"Benzene readily decolourises bromine water","D":"Its hydrogenation is more exothermic than the Kekulé model predicts"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.10"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-53","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-MECHANISMS","stem":"In the nitration mechanism, after NO₂⁺ has added to the ring, what happens next?","options":{"A":"The ring opens up to form a chain","B":"An NO₂⁻ ion attacks the positive intermediate","C":"An H⁺ is lost, restoring the delocalised ring","D":"A second NO₂⁺ adds to the same carbon"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"NO₂⁺ accepts a pair of electrons from the delocalised ring, giving a positive intermediate in which delocalisation is partly broken. The C–H bond on that carbon then breaks, its electrons return to the ring, and H⁺ is lost. Restoring the stable delocalised ring is why benzene substitutes rather than adds.","reforge":{"stem":"What is regenerated at the end of the nitration of benzene?","options":{"A":"The NO₂⁺ electrophile","B":"The nitric acid reactant","C":"The benzene ring's C=C bonds","D":"The sulfuric acid catalyst"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.10"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-54","tag":"MC-GCHEM-SYNTHESIS","stem":"Nitrobenzene is reduced to phenylamine using:","options":{"A":"Tollens' reagent","B":"acidified potassium dichromate, under reflux","C":"concentrated sulfuric acid","D":"tin and concentrated hydrochloric acid"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"Aromatic amines are made in two steps: nitrate benzene, then reduce the nitro group. Tin and concentrated HCl reduce –NO₂ to –NH₂; the product is the salt, so NaOH is added to release free phenylamine. Dichromate is an oxidising agent, the opposite of what is needed.","reforge":{"stem":"Phenylamine is used mainly in the manufacture of:","options":{"A":"azo dyes","B":"fertilisers","C":"fuels","D":"plastics"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.11"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-55","tag":"MC-GCHEM-FUNCTIONAL-GROUPS","stem":"Quaternary ammonium salts with long alkyl chains are used as:","options":{"A":"cationic surfactants in fabric softeners","B":"fuels in internal combustion engines","C":"catalysts in the Haber process for ammonia","D":"monomers for making polyesters"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"A quaternary ammonium ion has four alkyl groups on nitrogen and a permanent positive charge. With a long hydrocarbon chain, it has a charged head and a non-polar tail, so it is a cationic surfactant. The positive head sticks to negatively charged fibres and hair, which is why these salts are used in fabric softeners and conditioners.","reforge":{"stem":"Ethylamine reacts with excess bromoethane. What is the final product?","options":{"A":"Ethanamide","B":"A quaternary ammonium salt","C":"Diethyl ether and ammonia gas","D":"Ethene"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.11"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-56","tag":"MC-GCHEM-FUNCTIONAL-GROUPS","stem":"Amines act as nucleophiles because the nitrogen atom:","options":{"A":"has a positive charge that attracts electrons","B":"has a lone pair that can attack a δ+ carbon","C":"has an empty orbital that accepts electrons","D":"forms radicals easily in UV light"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"Amines, like ammonia, have a lone pair on nitrogen. Donating it to H⁺ makes them bases; donating it to a δ+ carbon makes them nucleophiles. That is why they react with halogenoalkanes and with acyl chlorides. Each substitution leaves a nitrogen that still has a lone pair, so further reactions can follow.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does reacting ammonia with a halogenoalkane give a mixture of amines?","options":{"A":"The halogenoalkane rearranges before it reacts","B":"Ammonia breaks down into several different radicals on heating","C":"The amine formed is itself a nucleophile and reacts again","D":"Hydroxide ions compete with the ammonia"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.11"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-57","tag":"MC-GCHEM-FUNCTIONAL-GROUPS","stem":"Which order shows base strength, strongest first?","options":{"A":"Phenylamine > ammonia > ethylamine","B":"Ethylamine > ammonia > phenylamine","C":"Ammonia > ethylamine > phenylamine","D":"Ammonia > phenylamine > ethylamine"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"Base strength depends on how available the nitrogen lone pair is. An ethyl group pushes electron density onto nitrogen, so ethylamine is stronger than ammonia. In phenylamine the lone pair is partly delocalised into the benzene ring, making it less available, so phenylamine is weaker than ammonia.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the correct name for CH₃CH₂CH₂NH₂?","options":{"A":"Propanamide","B":"Propylamine","C":"Propanenitrile","D":"Aminoethane"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.11"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-58","tag":"MC-GCHEM-FUNCTIONAL-GROUPS","stem":"Ethylamine dissolves in water to form an alkaline solution because it:","options":{"A":"contains hydroxide ions in its structure","B":"donates a proton to water, forming H₃O⁺","C":"accepts a proton from water, forming OH⁻","D":"reacts with the water to release ammonia gas"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"The nitrogen lone pair accepts H⁺ from a water molecule: CH₃CH₂NH₂ + H₂O ⇌ CH₃CH₂NH₃⁺ + OH⁻. The hydroxide ions make the solution alkaline. Because the equilibrium lies to the left, ethylamine is a weak base, like ammonia.","reforge":{"stem":"Ethylamine reacts with hydrochloric acid. What forms?","options":{"A":"Ethylammonium chloride","B":"Chloroethane and ammonia","C":"Ethanamide and hydrogen","D":"Ethanol and ammonium chloride"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.11"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-59","tag":"MC-GCHEM-SYNTHESIS","stem":"Why is phenylamine made by reducing nitrobenzene, not from chlorobenzene and ammonia?","options":{"A":"Nitrobenzene is the only aromatic compound that can react","B":"Ammonia is too strong an acid to react with chlorobenzene","C":"Chlorobenzene is a gas at room temperature","D":"Chlorobenzene's C–Cl bond resists nucleophilic attack"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"In chlorobenzene the C–Cl bond is strengthened because a chlorine lone pair overlaps with the delocalised ring, and the ring's electrons repel an approaching nucleophile. So ammonia does not substitute it under normal conditions. Nitration followed by reduction is the reliable route to aromatic amines.","reforge":{"stem":"Which reagent turns a primary amine into an N-substituted amide?","options":{"A":"Bromine water","B":"Sodium borohydride","C":"Ethanoyl chloride","D":"Tollens' reagent"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.11"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-60","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-STRUCTURE","stem":"The polyester Terylene (PET) is formed from ethane-1,2-diol and:","options":{"A":"benzene-1,4-dicarboxylic acid","B":"hexane-1,6-diamine","C":"ethene","D":"benzene-1,4-diamine with hexanedioic acid"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"A polyester needs a monomer with two –OH groups and one with two –COOH groups. Each –OH reacts with a –COOH to form an ester link, –COO–, eliminating water. Terylene uses ethane-1,2-diol and benzene-1,4-dicarboxylic acid. Diamines make polyamides instead.","reforge":{"stem":"Nylon-6,6 is made from hexane-1,6-diamine and:","options":{"A":"propene","B":"ethane-1,2-diol","C":"benzene-1,4-diol","D":"hexanedioic acid"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.12"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-61","tag":"MC-GCHEM-BONDING-STRUCTURE","stem":"Why are polyamides such as nylon strong?","options":{"A":"The chains are held together by ionic bonds","B":"The chains are cross-linked to each other by strong covalent C–C bonds","C":"Hydrogen bonds form between C=O and N–H groups on neighbouring chains","D":"Each chain is a single giant molecule with no forces between them"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"Every amide link, –CONH–, has a δ− oxygen and a δ+ hydrogen on nitrogen. Neighbouring chains line up so that N–H on one chain hydrogen-bonds to C=O on the next. Many hydrogen bonds along the chains make the material strong, which is why nylon and Kevlar are used for ropes and protective clothing.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the link between monomers in a polyester?","options":{"A":"An ester link, –COO–","B":"An amide link, –CONH–","C":"A C=C double bond","D":"An ether link, –O–"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.12"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-62","tag":"MC-GCHEM-BONDING-STRUCTURE","stem":"Kevlar is a polyamide made from two aromatic monomers. Why is it so strong?","options":{"A":"Its chains are cross-linked by sulfur bridges","B":"Its rigid chains line up and hydrogen-bond strongly","C":"It is a giant ionic lattice of aromatic ions","D":"Its benzene rings form covalent bonds between chains"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"Kevlar is made from benzene-1,4-diamine and benzene-1,4-dicarboxylic acid. The flat benzene rings make each chain rigid and straight, so the chains pack closely side by side with many hydrogen bonds between the amide groups. That gives a very high strength for its weight, used in bulletproof vests.","reforge":{"stem":"A polyamide is hydrolysed by hot aqueous acid. What forms?","options":{"A":"Ammonia and carbon dioxide","B":"An alkene and an alcohol","C":"A diol and a dicarboxylic acid, in equal amounts","D":"A dicarboxylic acid and a diammonium salt"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.12"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-63","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-STRUCTURE","stem":"What is eliminated when a diol and a dicarboxylic acid form a polyester?","options":{"A":"Ammonia gas","B":"Hydrogen chloride","C":"Carbon dioxide","D":"Water molecules"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"Condensation polymerisation joins monomers and releases a small molecule at each link. An –OH and a –COOH join to form an ester link and release H₂O. If a diacyl chloride is used instead of a dicarboxylic acid, HCl is eliminated. Addition polymers eliminate nothing.","reforge":{"stem":"A polyamide is made using a diacyl chloride rather than a dicarboxylic acid. What is eliminated?","options":{"A":"Chlorine gas, Cl₂","B":"Water molecules","C":"Hydrogen chloride","D":"Ammonia gas"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.12"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-64","tag":"MC-GCHEM-SYNTHESIS","stem":"Why is incinerating waste polymers both useful and a problem?","options":{"A":"It releases energy, but can produce toxic gases","B":"It recycles the monomers, but uses a lot of water","C":"It is cheap, but the polymers never ignite","D":"It removes carbon dioxide, but releases heat"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"Burning polymers releases energy that can generate electricity and reduces landfill. But it produces CO₂, and some polymers give toxic gases: PVC releases HCl, for example, so the waste gases must be cleaned. Landfill, recycling and incineration each have costs, so judge them against each other.","reforge":{"stem":"What is an advantage of recycling a polymer instead of sending it to landfill?","options":{"A":"It always produces a stronger polymer","B":"It saves crude oil and reduces landfill use","C":"It releases no carbon dioxide at any stage","D":"It is always cheaper than any other way of disposal"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.12"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-65","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-STRUCTURE","stem":"Which pair of monomers forms a polyamide?","options":{"A":"A diamine and a dicarboxylic acid","B":"A diol and a dicarboxylic acid","C":"An alkene and an alcohol","D":"Two different alkenes with C=C bonds"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"An amide link, –CONH–, forms between an amine group and a carboxylic acid (or acyl chloride) group. A polymer needs both ends of each monomer to react, so you need a diamine and a dicarboxylic acid, or one monomer that carries both groups, like an amino acid. A diol with a dicarboxylic acid gives a polyester.","reforge":{"stem":"When drawing the repeating unit of a condensation polymer, you should:","options":{"A":"show only the C=C bond from each monomer","B":"show both monomers with the eliminated water still attached","C":"show the whole chain with every atom drawn","D":"show one unit of each monomer, joined by one link"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.12"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-66","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-STRUCTURE","stem":"In a protein, the secondary structure (α-helix and β-pleated sheet) is held in place by:","options":{"A":"peptide bonds between the side chains","B":"hydrogen bonds between C=O and N–H groups","C":"disulfide bridges between cysteine units only","D":"ionic bonds between phosphate groups"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"Primary structure is the sequence of amino acids, held by peptide bonds. Secondary structure is the coiling into an α-helix or folding into a β-sheet, held by hydrogen bonds between the C=O and N–H of the backbone. Tertiary structure, the overall 3D shape, also uses disulfide bridges, ionic links and further hydrogen bonds between side chains.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the primary structure of a protein?","options":{"A":"The sequence of amino acids in the chain","B":"The coiling of the chain into an α-helix","C":"The folding of the chain into a 3D shape","D":"The number of chains that join together"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.13"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-67","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-STRUCTURE","stem":"Why are enzymes described as stereospecific?","options":{"A":"They are made from only one type of amino acid","B":"They can only work at one single fixed temperature and pH","C":"Their active site fits only one enantiomer of a substrate","D":"They change the shape of every molecule they meet"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"An enzyme's active site is a 3D pocket made from chiral amino acids. A substrate must fit that shape to bind, so usually only one enantiomer is a substrate. Drugs can be designed as inhibitors that fit the active site and block the real substrate, which is how computer modelling helps drug design.","reforge":{"stem":"How can a drug act as an enzyme inhibitor?","options":{"A":"It adds extra substrate molecules to the enzyme","B":"It binds to the active site, blocking the substrate","C":"It raises the temperature of the active site","D":"It breaks every one of the peptide bonds in the enzyme"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.13"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-68","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-STRUCTURE","stem":"In DNA, adenine pairs with:","options":{"A":"uracil","B":"guanine","C":"cytosine","D":"thymine"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"The four DNA bases pair in a fixed way: adenine with thymine (two hydrogen bonds) and cytosine with guanine (three hydrogen bonds). The pairs fit because their shapes and hydrogen-bonding groups match. Uracil replaces thymine in RNA, not DNA.","reforge":{"stem":"What holds the two strands of the DNA double helix together?","options":{"A":"Ionic bonds between the phosphate groups","B":"Covalent bonds between the sugar groups","C":"Hydrogen bonds between complementary bases","D":"Peptide bonds between the bases on each strand"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.13"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-69","tag":"MC-GCHEM-ORGANIC-STRUCTURE","stem":"A DNA nucleotide is made of:","options":{"A":"ribose, a phosphate and two bases","B":"an amino acid, a sugar and a phosphate","C":"glucose, a base and an amine group","D":"a phosphate, 2-deoxyribose and a base"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"Each nucleotide has a phosphate group, the sugar 2-deoxyribose and one of four bases (A, T, C or G). Nucleotides join through condensation reactions between the phosphate of one and the sugar of the next, forming the sugar–phosphate backbone. The bases stick out from the backbone and pair with the bases on the other strand.","reforge":{"stem":"In the DNA backbone, nucleotides are linked by:","options":{"A":"hydrogen bonds between the neighbouring bases on the same strand","B":"bonds between the sugar of one and the phosphate of the next","C":"peptide bonds between amino groups and acid groups","D":"disulfide bridges between sulfur atoms"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.13"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-70","tag":"MC-GCHEM-FUNCTIONAL-GROUPS","stem":"At low pH, glycine exists mainly as:","options":{"A":"H₃N⁺CH₂COO⁻","B":"H₂NCH₂COO⁻","C":"H₃N⁺CH₂COOH","D":"H₂NCH₂COOH"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"In acid there is plenty of H⁺, so both groups are protonated: the amine becomes –NH₃⁺ and the carboxyl stays –COOH. In alkali both lose H⁺, giving –NH₂ and –COO⁻. At the isoelectric point the amino acid is a zwitterion, H₃N⁺CH₂COO⁻, with no overall charge.","reforge":{"stem":"At high pH, the amino acid alanine exists mainly as:","options":{"A":"H₂NCH(CH₃)COO⁻","B":"H₃N⁺CH(CH₃)COOH","C":"H₃N⁺CH(CH₃)COO⁻","D":"H₂NCH(CH₃)COOH"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.13"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-71","tag":"MC-GCHEM-MASS-SPECTRA","stem":"A mixture of amino acids can be separated and identified by:","options":{"A":"fractional distillation of the mixture under reduced pressure","B":"thin-layer chromatography, with ninhydrin to show the spots","C":"adding bromine water to each one in turn","D":"measuring the mass of the mixture"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"Amino acids are colourless, so after a TLC plate is run it is sprayed with ninhydrin (or viewed under UV light) to show the spots. Each amino acid's Rf value is then compared with known values run under the same conditions. Distillation fails because amino acids decompose before they boil.","reforge":{"stem":"Why do amino acids have high melting points for their size?","options":{"A":"They have strong C=C double bonds","B":"They contain long chains of carbon atoms","C":"They form zwitterions with strong ionic attractions","D":"They are metals with a sea of delocalised electrons"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.13"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-72","tag":"MC-GCHEM-SYNTHESIS","stem":"Which two-step route converts ethene into ethylamine?","options":{"A":"Add HBr, then react with excess ammonia","B":"Add water, then oxidise with dichromate","C":"Add H₂, then react with ammonia","D":"React with KCN, then reduce with H₂"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"Plan a route by matching each step to a reaction you know. HBr adds across the C=C, giving bromoethane. Excess ammonia then substitutes the bromine, giving ethylamine. Ethane, from adding H₂, is too unreactive to take part in nucleophilic substitution, and alkenes do not react with KCN.","reforge":{"stem":"Which route converts bromoethane into propylamine, with one more carbon?","options":{"A":"Eliminate HBr, then add ammonia","B":"React with excess ammonia, then heat the mixture","C":"React with NaOH, then oxidise","D":"React with KCN, then reduce the nitrile"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.14"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-73","tag":"MC-GCHEM-SYNTHESIS","stem":"To convert a halogenoalkane into an alcohol, the reagent and conditions are:","options":{"A":"NaBH₄ in ethanol at room temperature","B":"NaOH in ethanol, heated under reflux","C":"concentrated H₂SO₄, then water","D":"aqueous NaOH, heated under reflux"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"Learn each reaction with its reagent and conditions, because the conditions decide the product. Aqueous NaOH under reflux gives nucleophilic substitution and an alcohol. NaOH in ethanol gives elimination and an alkene. NaBH₄ reduces carbonyl compounds, not halogenoalkanes.","reforge":{"stem":"Which reagent converts butanone into butan-2-ol?","options":{"A":"Sodium tetrahydridoborate(III)","B":"Acidified potassium dichromate(VI)","C":"Concentrated sulfuric acid","D":"Tollens' reagent"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.14"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-74","tag":"MC-GCHEM-SYNTHESIS","stem":"Why do chemists look for synthetic routes with fewer steps?","options":{"A":"Each step loses some product, so overall yield falls","B":"Fewer steps always use more dangerous reagents","C":"Every extra step adds another chiral centre to the product","D":"More steps always give a purer final product"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"The overall yield is the product of each step's yield, so it falls quickly as steps are added: three steps at 70% each give only about 34%. Fewer steps also mean less solvent, energy and waste. That is why industry looks for short routes with high atom economy and safe reagents.","reforge":{"stem":"A three-step synthesis has yields of 80%, 50% and 50%. What is the overall yield?","options":{"A":"60%","B":"20%","C":"180%","D":"50%"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.14"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-75","tag":"MC-GCHEM-MASS-SPECTRA","stem":"Why is TMS, Si(CH₃)₄, used as the reference in NMR?","options":{"A":"It absorbs at a higher δ than any organic compound","B":"It reacts with the sample to form a single, easily seen product","C":"Its 12 equivalent H atoms give one sharp peak, set at δ = 0","D":"It removes water from the sample before it is run"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"All twelve hydrogens (and four carbons) in TMS are in the same environment, so it gives one strong, sharp signal, which is defined as δ = 0. It absorbs at lower δ than almost all organic compounds, so it does not overlap their peaks. It is also inert, non-toxic and volatile, so it is easy to remove afterwards.","reforge":{"stem":"Why are NMR samples dissolved in solvents such as CDCl₃?","options":{"A":"Deuterium reacts with and removes any impurities present","B":"CDCl₃ makes all of the sample's peaks much larger","C":"Deuterium gives no signal in a ¹H NMR spectrum","D":"CDCl₃ provides the reference peak at δ = 0"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.15"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-76","tag":"MC-GCHEM-MASS-SPECTRA","stem":"How many peaks appear in the ¹³C NMR spectrum of propan-2-ol, CH₃CH(OH)CH₃?","options":{"A":"Three","B":"Two","C":"One","D":"Four"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"Count carbon environments, not carbon atoms. The molecule is symmetrical: both CH₃ carbons are in the same environment, and the CH(OH) carbon is different. So there are two peaks. Propan-1-ol, CH₃CH₂CH₂OH, has three different carbons and gives three peaks, which is how ¹³C NMR tells the isomers apart.","reforge":{"stem":"How many peaks appear in the ¹³C NMR spectrum of butanone, CH₃COCH₂CH₃?","options":{"A":"Five","B":"Three","C":"Two","D":"Four"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.15"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-77","tag":"MC-GCHEM-MASS-SPECTRA","stem":"In ¹H NMR, what does the relative area under each peak show?","options":{"A":"The number of carbon environments present","B":"The number of neighbouring H atoms","C":"The type of bond that each H is in","D":"The ratio of H atoms in each environment"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"The integration (relative peak area) is proportional to the number of hydrogens causing the peak. For ethanol, CH₃CH₂OH, the areas are 3 : 2 : 1. Splitting gives the number of neighbouring hydrogens, and chemical shift gives the type of environment, so use all three together.","reforge":{"stem":"Ethanol's ¹H NMR spectrum shows a triplet, a quartet and a singlet. What is their integration ratio, in that order?","options":{"A":"6 : 2 : 1","B":"2 : 2 : 2","C":"3 : 3 : 1","D":"3 : 2 : 1"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.15"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-78","tag":"MC-GCHEM-MASS-SPECTRA","stem":"Using the n + 1 rule, the CH₃ signal in CH₃CH₂Cl is split into a:","options":{"A":"triplet","B":"quartet","C":"doublet","D":"singlet"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"A signal is split into n + 1 peaks, where n is the number of hydrogens on the adjacent carbon(s). The CH₃ hydrogens have two neighbours on the CH₂, so their signal is a triplet. The CH₂ hydrogens have three neighbours on the CH₃, so theirs is a quartet. A triplet and quartet together point to an ethyl group.","reforge":{"stem":"In the ¹H NMR spectrum of CH₃CH₂Cl, the CH₂ signal is split into a:","options":{"A":"doublet","B":"triplet","C":"quartet","D":"quintet"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.15"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-79","tag":"MC-GCHEM-MASS-SPECTRA","stem":"A ¹H NMR peak at δ 9.5–10 suggests which type of hydrogen?","options":{"A":"The H of an alcohol O–H group","B":"An H in an alkane chain","C":"The H of an aldehyde group, –CHO","D":"An H on a carbon next to a halogen"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"Chemical shift depends on how much the hydrogen is deshielded by nearby electronegative atoms. Use the data sheet: R–CH₃ about 0.7–1.2, H–C–X (halogen) about 3.1–4.2, alcohol O–H about 0.5–5.0, and aldehyde –CHO about 9.0–10.0. A peak near 10 is a strong sign of an aldehyde.","reforge":{"stem":"In ¹³C NMR, a peak at δ 160–220 indicates a carbon in a:","options":{"A":"C=O group","B":"C–C bond in an alkane","C":"C–Cl bond","D":"C–H bond in methane"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.15"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-80","tag":"MC-GCHEM-MASS-SPECTRA","stem":"Why do the H atoms of a CH₃ group next to C=O appear at higher δ than those in ethane?","options":{"A":"The C=O group pushes extra electron density onto them","B":"The nearby electronegative O pulls electrons away","C":"They are bonded directly to the oxygen atom","D":"They are further away from the TMS reference"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"Electrons around a nucleus shield it from the applied magnetic field. A nearby electronegative atom draws electron density away, deshielding the hydrogens, so they absorb at higher δ. That is why H–C–C=O appears around 2.1–2.6 while an alkane CH₃ appears around 0.7–1.2.","reforge":{"stem":"How many different hydrogen environments are in methyl propanoate, CH₃CH₂COOCH₃?","options":{"A":"Two","B":"Three","C":"Four","D":"Eight"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.15"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-81","tag":"MC-GCHEM-MASS-SPECTRA","stem":"A compound C₃H₆O shows a single peak in its ¹H NMR spectrum. Which structure fits?","options":{"A":"Propanone, CH₃COCH₃","B":"Propanal, CH₃CH₂CHO","C":"Prop-2-en-1-ol, CH₂=CHCH₂OH","D":"Methoxyethene, CH₂=CHOCH₃"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"One peak means every hydrogen is in the same environment. In propanone the two CH₃ groups are identical, and their neighbour is the C=O carbon, which has no hydrogens, so there is no splitting: one singlet. Propanal has three environments, and the other two isomers have several.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does the CH₃ signal of propanone appear as a singlet?","options":{"A":"There are six H atoms, which cancel out the splitting","B":"The carbon next to it, the C=O, carries no hydrogens","C":"Propanone has only one carbon atom","D":"Singlets always come from CH₃ groups"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.15"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-82","tag":"MC-GCHEM-MASS-SPECTRA","stem":"In ¹H NMR, the splitting pattern of a peak gives information about:","options":{"A":"the mass of the molecule","B":"the total number of carbon atoms in the chain","C":"the number of H atoms on adjacent carbons","D":"the number of C=O groups"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"Spin–spin coupling between hydrogens on neighbouring carbons splits each signal into n + 1 peaks. So the splitting tells you how many hydrogens are next door: a singlet has none, a doublet one, a triplet two and a quartet three. Hydrogens on O–H usually appear as singlets and do not split their neighbours.","reforge":{"stem":"A ¹H NMR signal is a doublet. How many hydrogens are on the adjacent carbon atoms?","options":{"A":"None","B":"Two","C":"One","D":"Three"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.15"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-83","tag":"MC-GCHEM-MASS-SPECTRA","stem":"In thin-layer chromatography, what is the stationary phase?","options":{"A":"The solvent that moves up the plate as it runs","B":"A thin layer of silica or alumina on a plate","C":"The pencil line at the base of the plate","D":"The spots of the mixture being separated"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"Every chromatography method has a stationary phase that does not move and a mobile phase that does. In TLC the stationary phase is a thin layer of silica or alumina on glass, metal or plastic; the mobile phase is the solvent. Substances separate because each balances differently between sticking to the stationary phase and dissolving in the mobile phase.","reforge":{"stem":"In gas chromatography, what is the mobile phase?","options":{"A":"An unreactive carrier gas such as nitrogen","B":"A liquid coated on a solid support in the column","C":"The sample after it has been vaporised","D":"The detector at the end of the column"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.16"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-84","tag":"MC-GCHEM-MASS-SPECTRA","stem":"In gas chromatography, what is the retention time?","options":{"A":"The time taken for all the carrier gas to pass through the column","B":"The time it takes for the column to heat up","C":"The time the sample spends in the injector","D":"Time from injection until the substance reaches the detector"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"Retention time is how long a component takes to travel from the injection point to the detector. It depends on the substance's solubility in the liquid stationary phase and on the conditions (temperature, column, gas flow). Comparing it with known substances run under identical conditions helps identify a component.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is gas chromatography often linked to a mass spectrometer (GC–MS)?","options":{"A":"Linking them removes the need for a carrier gas","B":"MS separates the mixture first, so GC can then measure retention","C":"GC gives molecular masses that MS then confirms","D":"GC separates the mixture and MS identifies each component"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.16"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-85","tag":"MC-GCHEM-MASS-SPECTRA","stem":"How can colourless spots be made visible on a TLC plate?","options":{"A":"By weighing the plate carefully before and after the run","B":"By heating the plate strongly until the spots melt","C":"By adding more solvent to the plate","D":"By shining UV light on it, or by using iodine vapour"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"Many organic compounds are colourless, so the spots must be revealed. Plates containing a fluorescent dye show dark spots under UV light, while iodine vapour or a spray such as ninhydrin (for amino acids) colours them. Mark the spots and the solvent front in pencil straight away, before they fade or evaporate.","reforge":{"stem":"Why must the solvent level be below the baseline when a TLC plate is put in the tank?","options":{"A":"So the solvent can all evaporate before it reaches the spots","B":"So the spots do not dissolve straight into the solvent pool","C":"So the pencil line is not washed away","D":"So that the plate does not fall over"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.16"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-86","tag":"MC-GCHEM-MASS-SPECTRA","stem":"A spot has an Rf value of 0.80. What does this suggest about the substance?","options":{"A":"It is very soluble in the mobile phase","B":"It is strongly held by the stationary phase","C":"It has a high molecular mass","D":"It has not moved from the baseline"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"Rf = distance moved by the spot ÷ distance moved by the solvent front, so it is always between 0 and 1. A high Rf means the substance spent most of its time in the moving solvent; a low Rf means it was held back by the stationary phase. Rf values only compare fairly under the same solvent and plate.","reforge":{"stem":"A solvent front moves 6.0 cm and a spot moves 1.5 cm. What is the Rf value?","options":{"A":"0.40","B":"4.0","C":"0.25","D":"7.5"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.16"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-87","tag":"MC-GCHEM-MASS-SPECTRA","stem":"Why is a lid placed on a TLC tank while the plate runs?","options":{"A":"To keep the plate in the dark","B":"To keep the air saturated with solvent vapour","C":"To keep the mixture warm","D":"To stop the plate from falling over in the tank"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"With a lid, the air inside the tank fills with solvent vapour, so the solvent does not evaporate from the plate as it rises. That keeps the solvent front moving evenly and makes Rf values reliable. A running plate should also be left undisturbed and removed before the solvent reaches the top.","reforge":{"stem":"Two substances give spots with the same Rf value in one solvent. How can you check whether they are the same substance?","options":{"A":"Leave the plate to run for longer","B":"Measure the diameters of the two spots more carefully","C":"Use a pen to draw a clearer baseline","D":"Run them again in a different solvent and compare"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.16"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-88","tag":"MC-GCHEM-MASS-SPECTRA","stem":"In column chromatography, which component leaves the column first?","options":{"A":"The one with the strongest colour","B":"The one with the highest relative molecular mass","C":"The one least attracted to the stationary phase","D":"The one that was added last"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"In column chromatography, solvent (the mobile phase) is run through a column packed with silica or alumina (the stationary phase). Components that are held least strongly by the stationary phase move fastest and come out first. Collecting the liquid in fractions lets each component be obtained separately.","reforge":{"stem":"In column chromatography, the mobile phase is:","options":{"A":"the solvent poured through the column","B":"the silica or alumina packed in the column","C":"the glass wool at the bottom of the column","D":"the mixture being separated"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.16"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-89","tag":"MC-GCHEM-MASS-SPECTRA","stem":"In a gas chromatogram, what does the area under each peak indicate?","options":{"A":"The boiling point of each component","B":"The relative amount of each one","C":"The retention time of each component","D":"The molecular mass of each component"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"A gas chromatogram plots detector response against time. The position of a peak (its retention time) helps identify the substance, and the area under it is proportional to how much of that substance is present. Comparing peak areas gives the relative amounts of the components in a mixture.","reforge":{"stem":"Two components have different retention times in GC. Which one spends longer in the column?","options":{"A":"The one present in the smaller amount in the mixture","B":"The one with the lower boiling point","C":"The one more soluble in the carrier gas","D":"The one more soluble in the stationary liquid"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.16"},{"id":"CHEM-F3-90","tag":"MC-GCHEM-MASS-SPECTRA","stem":"Which technique would best separate and identify the volatile compounds in a perfume?","options":{"A":"Recrystallisation from hot water","B":"Paper chromatography, using a pencil baseline","C":"Titration against a standard acid","D":"Gas chromatography with mass spectrometry"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"Volatile compounds suit gas chromatography: the GC column separates the mixture, and the mass spectrometer gives a spectrum for each component as it leaves, which can be matched against a database. GC–MS is used in forensics, airport security and environmental testing for the same reason.","reforge":{"stem":"A substance's Rf value in TLC depends on:","options":{"A":"how long the plate is left to run","B":"how much of the substance is spotted","C":"the solvent and stationary phase used","D":"the length of the TLC plate that is used"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CHEM-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-chem-3.3.16"}]}});
